window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("deployment", {"topics":[{"key":"deployment/customer-vpc-on-prem-air-gapped","sources":["Official docs: Helm","Official docs: Kubernetes","Official docs: Sigstore (cosign)"],"why":"- **SaaS assumes the customer sends you data.** For a bank's transaction records, a hospital's clinical\n  notes, a defence contractor's documents or a government's citizen data, the contract, regulator or\n  classification policy says the data stays inside a perimeter they control.\n- **Encryption promises do not answer the rule.** \"We encrypt it and promise not to train on it\" does\n  not satisfy a rule about *where processing happens* and *who can be compelled to hand it over*.\n- **Copying your SaaS stack fails three ways.** It quietly depends on the open internet; you no longer\n  own the platform; and you lose your eyes (no dashboards, no logs, no hot-fix at 2 a.m.).\n- **So it is a different product shape.** A signed, self-contained bundle, a configuration surface that\n  captures every environment decision, and an operating model where the customer's platform team holds\n  the keys. The further inward, the more of your product is packaging rather than infrastructure you run.\n\n### The hidden internet dependencies\n| Dependency | Why it breaks |\n|---|---|\n| Container images from public registries | outbound pull a review will question, an air gap breaks |\n| Model weights fetched from a hub at start-up | same, and often 100+ GB |\n| Python packages installed at build time inside their network | no package index reachable |\n| Licence checks and feature flags served from your SaaS | call-home traffic |\n| Traces shipped to an observability vendor | data leaving the perimeter |\n\nPlatform you no longer own: their Kubernetes version, storage classes, certificate authority, egress\nproxy and identity provider.","how":"### Three targets, one spectrum\n| | Customer VPC | On-prem | Air-gapped |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Where it runs | their AWS, Azure or GCP account | their datacentre or colocation | their facility, no network path out |\n| Managed services | yes (their IAM, storage, DB, KMS) | none | none |\n| Models | managed service over private endpoint, or open-weight on GPUs in the VPC | open-weight on their GPUs | open-weight, shipped in |\n| Updates | registry replication | mirror they populate | physical media or diode |\n| Your access | optional cross-account role | usually none | none |\n\n**Customer VPC (often sold as \"bring your own cloud\").**\n- **Their identity and data stores.** Containers run in a VPC/VNet they own; identity is their IAM;\n  storage, databases and KMS keys are theirs.\n- **Model calls stay private.** Calls go to the cloud's managed model service (Bedrock, Azure OpenAI,\n  Vertex AI) over a private endpoint, or to open-weight models on GPU instances in the same VPC.\n- **Vendor access is narrow.** If any, a cross-account role with a time-bound, logged session and an\n  `ExternalId` condition to prevent the confused-deputy problem.\n- **Control plane / data plane split.** Many vendors host a **control plane** (licensing, version\n  catalogue, configuration, upgrade orchestration) while the customer hosts the **data plane** (every\n  component that touches customer data).\n- **Why the split works.** The customer's requirement is about data custody, not metadata, so you keep\n  operability for everything that is not data. It must be optional *because* some customers reject any\n  outbound connection on principle.\n\n**On-prem.**\n- **No managed services.** You get a Kubernetes distribution (OpenShift, Rancher, vanilla upstream, k3s)\n  at whatever version they run, their storage classes and their ingress controller.\n- **Their hardware and trust.** GPUs come through a device plugin or the NVIDIA GPU Operator; there is\n  an internal CA; identity is their directory through OIDC or LDAP.\n- **Egress through a proxy.** Outbound traffic, if allowed at all, goes through a proxy that often\n  terminates TLS with its own certificate.\n- **Deliverable.** A Helm chart or operator plus images in a registry they can reach, usually a mirror\n  they populate from your registry.\n\n**Air-gapped.**\n- **Everything on-prem implies, plus no network path.** Artifacts cross on physical media or through a\n  one-way transfer device (a data diode or a cross-domain solution).\n- **Inside:** a mirrored container registry, a package mirror, their own time source and their own CA.\n- **Nothing can call home.** No licence server, no telemetry, no automatic update, no hosted model.\n- **Updates and support.** Updates are a new bundle carried in; support is a log bundle carried out.\n\n### The delivery pipeline\n1. **Build deterministically.** Pin every dependency, build images from a locked manifest, and ensure\n   nothing at container start needs the network.\n   - Model weights are baked into an image or seeded into a persistent volume from the bundle.\n   - `HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1` and `TRANSFORMERS_OFFLINE=1` turn Hugging Face libraries into pure local loaders.\n   - Tokenizer files are shipped, not fetched.\n2. **Sign and attest.** Sign images and the bundle manifest (cosign is the common choice), ship an SBOM\n   (SPDX or CycloneDX), and publish SHA-256 hashes. Done *because* the receiving side cannot ask you \"is\n   this file right?\" and *because* the security review will ask how they know the artifact is yours and\n   unmodified.\n3. **Package.** Images exported with `docker save` or `skopeo copy` into a tarball or OCI layout, the\n   chart, the weights, a wheelhouse built with `pip download` for anything installed on-site, the docs,\n   and a verify script.\n4. **Transfer.** Registry-to-registry replication for VPC and connected on-prem; media or diode for the\n   air gap. A 70B-parameter model in 16-bit precision is roughly 140 GB of weights (2 bytes per\n   parameter), so the transfer is a planned activity, not a download.\n5. **Install.** `helm install` with a values file holding every environment decision: registry host,\n   storage class, ingress class, CA bundle, proxy, model endpoint, identity provider, resource limits.\n   Hard-coded assumptions are the single biggest source of failed installs.\n6. **Verify.** Smoke tests that exercise the real path inside their network: a model call, a retrieval,\n   an authenticated request, a write to their database.\n7. **Operate.**\n   - Logs and metrics stay inside and flow to *their* backends (ship Prometheus and OpenTelemetry\n     collector configs that point at them).\n   - A `support-bundle` command gathers redacted logs, config and version info into a tarball they can\n     send you.\n   - Upgrade is a new bundle plus `helm upgrade`; rollback is the previous chart version, which only\n     works if your schema migrations are backward compatible.\n\n### Signing for an air gap: key pair vs keyless\n- **Key pair (simplest).** A long-lived key pair whose public key ships out of band; verification needs\n  nothing but the key.\n- **Keyless (cosign's default).** Ties signatures to a short-lived certificate (Fulcio) and a\n  transparency log (Rekor).\n- **Keyless offline is possible but heavy.** It verifies offline only if you also ship the signature\n  bundle with its log inclusion proof and the Sigstore trusted root, and keep that root current: extra\n  machinery many air-gapped sites decline.\n\n### What changes about the software itself\n- **Licensing.** A signed offline licence file with an expiry and a grace period, checked locally.\n- **Feature flags.** Move from a remote service into the values file.\n- **Trust the customer's CA.** Every HTTP client reads their CA bundle (`SSL_CERT_FILE`,\n  `REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE`) *because* their proxy re-signs traffic.\n- **Secrets.** Come from their secret store, not yours.\n- **Time bombs fail loudly.** Certificate expiry, licence expiry and token lifetimes must fail loudly and\n  early *because* nobody will be watching a dashboard when they fail silently.\n- **The model is whatever you shipped.** On-prem and air-gapped customers run open-weight models on vLLM\n  or similar, so your evaluation suite must have run on that exact model and prompt set before the\n  bundle was cut.","example":"### Bundle manifest tool: build and verify\n- **Where it runs.** `build` runs on your connected side after packaging; `verify` runs on the\n  customer's side, before anything is loaded into their mirror registry.\n- **Why verify first.** A corrupted 140 GB copy is caught before the install starts rather than during it.\n- **Authorship too.** The manifest is then signed with `cosign sign-blob` (key-pair mode, so\n  `cosign verify-blob --key` works offline) so the customer checks authorship as well as integrity.\n- **Three distinct rejections.** A file that is missing (truncated transfer); a file whose hash differs\n  (corruption or tampering); a file present but not in the manifest (added after signing, which an\n  installer that globs the directory would otherwise load).\n- **Streaming hash.** Hashing reads 1 MB blocks *because* a 140 GB weights file cannot be read into memory.\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Build and verify an offline install bundle: `build <dir> <version>` or `verify <dir>`.\"\"\"\nimport hashlib, json, sys, pathlib\n\ndef sha256(path, chunk=1 << 20):\n    h = hashlib.sha256()\n    with open(path, \"rb\") as f:\n        for block in iter(lambda: f.read(chunk), b\"\"):\n            h.update(block)\n    return h.hexdigest()\n\ndef build(bundle_dir, version):\n    root = pathlib.Path(bundle_dir)\n    files = [p for p in root.rglob(\"*\") if p.is_file() and p.name != \"manifest.json\"]\n    manifest = {\"version\": version, \"artifacts\": {\n        str(p.relative_to(root)): {\"sha256\": sha256(p), \"bytes\": p.stat().st_size} for p in files}}\n    (root / \"manifest.json\").write_text(json.dumps(manifest, indent=1))\n    total = sum(a[\"bytes\"] for a in manifest[\"artifacts\"].values())\n    print(f\"{len(files)} artifacts, {total / 1e9:.1f} GB\")\n\ndef verify(bundle_dir):\n    root = pathlib.Path(bundle_dir)\n    manifest = json.loads((root / \"manifest.json\").read_text())\n    listed = manifest[\"artifacts\"]\n    present = {str(p.relative_to(root)) for p in root.rglob(\"*\")\n               if p.is_file() and p.name != \"manifest.json\"}\n    missing = [r for r in listed if r not in present]\n    altered = [r for r in listed if r in present and sha256(root / r) != listed[r][\"sha256\"]]\n    extra = sorted(present - set(listed))\n    for label, items in ((\"MISSING\", missing), (\"ALTERED\", altered), (\"UNLISTED\", extra)):\n        if items:\n            print(label, items)\n    ok = not (missing or altered or extra)\n    print(\"OK\" if ok else \"REJECT BUNDLE\")\n    return ok\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    cmd, path = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2]\n    if cmd == \"build\":\n        build(path, sys.argv[3])\n    else:\n        sys.exit(0 if verify(path) else 1)\n# Typical layout: images/app-1.8.3.tar, charts/assistant-1.8.3.tgz, models/<name>/, wheels/, docs/\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: sizing the weights in an air-gap delivery\nWeights are parameters × bytes per parameter, so precision choice decides the media and transfer plan.\nLink speeds are illustrative; a diode or cross-domain solution is often slower and is set by the site.\n\n| Model | Precision | Bytes/param | Weights | At 1 Gbps | At 10 Gbps |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 7B | 16-bit | 2 | ~14 GB | ~2 min | ~11 s |\n| 70B | 16-bit | 2 | ~140 GB | ~19 min | ~2 min |\n| 70B | 8-bit | 1 | ~70 GB | ~9 min | ~56 s |\n| 70B | 4-bit | 0.5 | ~35 GB | ~5 min | ~28 s |\n\n- **Read it as:** 140 GB × 8 = 1,120 Gb, and 1,120 Gb ÷ 1 Gbps ≈ 1,120 s ≈ 19 min, before hashing,\n  media handling and the customer's inbound scanning.\n- **Each extra precision ships as another full copy**, so bundling both 16-bit and 8-bit doubles the\n  media without doubling usefulness; ship the one your eval suite approved.\n\n### Offline-first CI gate: deny egress during the install test\nRun the install test in a namespace whose pods may only reach each other and cluster DNS. Any hidden\ndownload now fails in your CI instead of the customer's cluster. NetworkPolicy is enforced only if the\ncluster's CNI plugin supports it.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: NetworkPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: offline-install-test\nspec:\n  podSelector: {}                 # every pod in this namespace\n  policyTypes: [\"Egress\"]\n  egress:\n    - to:\n        - podSelector: {}         # same-namespace traffic only\n    - to:\n        - namespaceSelector:\n            matchLabels:\n              kubernetes.io/metadata.name: kube-system\n      ports:\n        - { protocol: UDP, port: 53 }\n        - { protocol: TCP, port: 53 }\n```","patterns":["**Offline-first CI gate.** On every release candidate, install the bundle into a namespace with the deny-egress NetworkPolicy above and run the smoke tests. Monitor DNS and connection-timeout errors in pod logs; any public hostname in them is a release blocker.","**One values file for every environment decision.** Expose registry host, storage class, ingress class, CA bundle, proxy and model endpoint as chart values (Bitnami-style charts use `global.imageRegistry` for the registry). Lint with `helm template` against each customer's values file before shipping.","**Mirror relocation with skopeo.** Use `skopeo copy` to move images into an OCI layout for the media, then into the customer's mirror, keeping digests unchanged so signatures still verify. Monitor signature-verification failures at admission.","**Expiry warnings well ahead of the date.** Have the licence and certificate checks log and surface a warning weeks before expiry and again during the grace period. Monitor days-to-expiry per site from support bundles.","**Bounded version support.** Publish how many bundle versions you support at once and require sites to upgrade into that window, so back-ports stay finite. Track the version each site runs.","**Expand-then-contract migrations.** Add columns and tables first, remove old ones a release later, so `helm rollback` to the previous chart still finds a schema it understands. Test rollback in CI."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    B[\"Deterministic build\"] --> S[\"Sign images and manifest\"]\n    S --> P[\"Package bundle\"]\n    P --> T{\"Target?\"}\n    T -->|\"VPC or connected\"| R[\"Registry replication\"]\n    T -->|\"air-gapped\"| M[\"Media or data diode\"]\n    R --> V[\"Verify hashes and signature\"]\n    M --> V\n    V -->|\"reject\"| P\n    V -->|\"ok\"| MR[(\"Customer mirror registry\")]\n    MR --> I[\"helm install with values file\"]\n    I --> ST[\"Smoke tests inside network\"]\n    ST --> O[\"Operate: their logs, support bundle\"]","caption":"Follow the bundle from your connected build side across the boundary; nothing on the right may reach\nback out."},"interview":[{"q":"How do customer-VPC, on-prem and air-gapped deployments differ, and why does a customer choose one?","a":"- **Customer VPC:** your software in their cloud account, with their IAM, storage and KMS; chosen when\n  custody matters but they already trust a cloud, so managed model services stay available.\n- **On-prem:** their hardware and Kubernetes with no managed services; chosen with no cloud footprint, a\n  ban on public cloud, or GPUs needed next to their systems.\n- **Air-gapped:** no network path at all; chosen only when a regulator or classification policy demands\n  it, because every update and support exchange becomes physical."},{"q":"Your chart installs cleanly in your lab but pods sit in ImagePullBackOff at the customer. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Read the event first:** `kubectl describe pod` shows which image reference failed and why\n  (unreachable host, auth, unknown manifest).\n- **Likely causes:** a hard-coded public registry host instead of their mirror, images not yet loaded\n  into the mirror, or a missing pull secret.\n- **Fix at the source:** make the registry a chart value, verify the bundle was imported by digest, and\n  add the offline CI gate so the next release cannot regress."},{"q":"When would you not offer a vendor-hosted control plane?","a":"- **When policy forbids any egress.** Even metadata-only outbound calls are a review item, and some\n  customers reject them on principle, so the split must be optional.\n- **When the air gap is physical.** There is no path to a control plane, so licensing, configuration and\n  upgrades must all work locally from the bundle and values file.\n- **Cost of dropping it:** fleet-wide upgrades and configuration become per-site work, so support load grows\n  with customer count."},{"q":"A customer wants a 70B model delivered into an air-gapped site. What numbers do you plan around?","a":"- **Weights:** 70B × 2 bytes in 16-bit ≈ 140 GB; an 8-bit copy is ~70 GB, so precision choice changes the\n  media plan.\n- **Time:** at 1 Gbps the copy alone is ~19 minutes, before hashing, the customer's scanning and import\n  into their mirror, which often dominate.\n- **Quality and hardware:** confirm their GPUs fit the chosen precision and that your eval suite passed\n  on exactly that model and precision."},{"q":"How can an air-gapped customer verify your artifacts, and why might keyless signing be awkward there?","a":"- **Hash manifest plus signature:** SHA-256 per file proves integrity; a signature over the manifest\n  proves origin; both check offline with a public key shipped out of band.\n- **Keyless needs more shipped state:** cosign keyless relies on Fulcio certificates and the Rekor log, so\n  offline verification needs the inclusion proof and a current Sigstore trusted root carried in too.\n- **So most sites choose key pairs**, accepting key-rotation work in exchange for simpler verification."}],"tradeoffs":["**Data custody vs observability.** The customer keeps custody of data and uses their own cloud discounts and controls, but you lose direct observability and fast iteration. Flips toward hosted SaaS plus a strong DPA when the customer has no platform team to operate what you ship.","**Control-plane split vs zero egress.** The split keeps upgrades and configuration manageable across many customers, but any outbound connection is a review item. Flips to fully self-contained the moment the customer's policy forbids egress.","**On-prem vs elastic capacity.** On-prem removes cloud dependency and per-token fees, but GPU capacity is fixed on a procurement cycle measured in months. Flips when the workload is bursty or needs frontier-model quality.","**Air-gap isolation vs support speed.** The strongest isolation possible, but every bug fix is a physical delivery and bugs surface weeks later through log bundles. Accept it only where mandated, and budget support time accordingly.","**Open-weight portability vs quality.** Shipping open-weight models makes the product deployable anywhere, but your quality bar must be re-established per shipped model, since the hosted model your SaaS uses is not an option inside."],"pitfalls":["**Runtime downloads.** Model weights from a hub, tokenizer files, `pip install` on start, NLTK or tiktoken data. Works in CI, then the pod crash-loops in the customer's cluster with DNS resolution or connection-timeout errors for a public hostname. Fix: bundle them and set the offline flags.","**Hard-coded registry host, DNS domain or storage class in the chart.** Symptom: `ImagePullBackOff` or persistent volume claims stuck in `Pending` on an otherwise healthy cluster. Fix: make them values.","**Not trusting the customer's CA.** Every outbound call from your containers fails with a certificate verification error because their egress proxy re-signs TLS. Fix: mount their CA bundle.","**Licence or certificate expiry inside an air gap.** The system stops on a date, nobody can renew online, and the customer discovers it during business hours.","**Assuming you will have the logs.** Incidents are debugged from screenshots in email until a support bundle command exists and the customer knows how to run it.","**Version sprawl across customers.** Each site is on a different bundle, so a fix must be back-ported to several versions; the symptom is a support matrix nobody can keep in their head.","**Schema migrations that cannot roll back.** A failed upgrade on-site leaves the customer unable to return to the previous chart version, turning a bad hour into a bad week."],"when":"- **Customer VPC when** the blocker is data custody within a cloud the customer already runs; it keeps\n  managed model services and most of your operability.\n- **On-prem when** the customer has no cloud footprint, needs GPU proximity to their systems or has a\n  contractual prohibition on public cloud.\n- **Air-gapped only when** a regulator or classification policy mandates it, and price the support model in.\n- **Measure before committing:** install time per customer, bundle size, number of supported versions,\n  and mean time to deliver a fix.\n- **Day to day** this shows up as the values file, the bundle pipeline and the support-bundle tool.\n- **Interviews:** list what breaks when the network goes away and how the delivery pipeline changes as a\n  result; that is the expected depth.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Removes every runtime network dependency, moves environment decisions into chart values, builds the signed bundle and the support-bundle command, and keeps migrations reversible.","architect":"Decides which deployment tiers the product supports, where the control-plane/data-plane boundary sits, and which models and dependencies each tier is allowed to assume.","fde":"Runs the install on the customer's cluster, negotiates registry, CA, proxy and identity details with their platform and security teams, and operates the sneakernet for air-gapped sites."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants your RAG assistant inside an air-gapped facility. Today the product calls a\nhosted frontier model, sends traces to a SaaS observability tool, downloads its embedding model from a\nhub at start-up and validates a licence against your server. What has to change, in what order, and\nwhat do you tell the customer they are giving up?","a":"First replace the hosted model with an open-weight model served inside (vLLM or similar) and\nre-run the evaluation suite on it, because that decides whether the product is still good enough to\nship. Then remove the start-up download by baking or seeding the embedding weights and setting the\noffline flags; replace the licence call with a signed offline licence file with expiry and grace; and\nredirect traces to a collector targeting the customer's own backend. Package images, chart, weights\nand wheels into a signed bundle with a hash manifest and a verify step, and add a support-bundle\ncommand. Tell the customer they lose frontier-model quality, automatic updates and vendor\nobservability; fixes arrive as bundles on a schedule, and they must operate the stack."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why do many vendors split a customer-VPC deployment into a vendor-hosted control plane and a customer-hosted data plane?","options":["Because cloud providers require the control plane to run in the vendor's account","Because the customer's requirement is custody of data, so keeping only non-data functions vendor-side preserves operability while meeting the requirement","Because it removes the need for the customer to grant the vendor any IAM access","Because Kubernetes cannot run both planes in the same cluster"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The split keeps licensing, configuration and upgrades manageable across customers while every component that touches data stays in the customer's account. It is a design choice, not a platform rule, and it must be optional because some customers forbid any outbound connection."},{"q":"An on-prem install crash-loops; the pod log shows a DNS resolution failure for a public model-hub hostname. What is the root cause and fix?","options":["The customer's cluster lacks a GPU operator, so the model cannot load","The Helm chart has the wrong storage class configured","The application downloads model or tokenizer files at start-up; weights must be bundled and the libraries run in offline mode","The customer's CA certificate is not trusted by the container"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A DNS failure for a hub hostname means code is trying to fetch artifacts at runtime. The fix is in the build: ship the weights and tokenizer files in the bundle and set the offline environment flags. A CA problem would show as a certificate verification error, not a resolution failure."},{"q":"Which artifact lets an air-gapped customer confirm a bundle is complete, unmodified and genuinely from you without contacting you?","options":["A README listing the included files and their versions","A licence file with an expiry date","A manifest of SHA-256 hashes signed with the vendor's key, plus signed images","A telemetry ping sent once the installation completes"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Hashes prove integrity and a signature over the manifest proves origin, and both can be checked offline. A README is unverifiable, a licence file proves entitlement rather than integrity, and a telemetry ping is impossible without a network path."}]},{"key":"deployment/private-endpoints","sources":["Official docs: AWS PrivateLink","Official docs: Azure Private Link","Official docs: Google Cloud Private Service Connect"],"why":"- **The subnet has no internet route on purpose.** A bank's application subnet still needs a hosted\n  model API, object storage and a SaaS observability tool, and every one has a public hostname that\n  resolves to a public IP.\n- **A NAT gateway opens everything.** The same path that reaches the model API also reaches a paste\n  site, which is exactly what the data-loss team was preventing.\n- **IP allowlists are brittle.** Cloud services use large, shared and changing address pools, so the\n  allowlist ends up permitting a huge slice of the cloud.\n- **Peering is too broad.** It is usually not offered for multi-tenant services; where it is, it connects\n  entire networks, requires non-overlapping CIDR ranges and exposes far more than one API.\n- **A private endpoint says exactly what security wants.** \"This subnet may reach this one service, over\n  a path we control, and that service refuses calls that did not come through that path.\" Once every\n  consumer arrives privately, the provider can disable public network access, so a leaked API key is\n  useless from the internet.","how":"The three large clouds implement the same idea under different names:\n\n| Cloud | Name | Consumer object | Publishing your own SaaS |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| AWS | PrivateLink | interface VPC endpoint | endpoint service fronted by a Network Load Balancer |\n| Azure | Private Link | private endpoint | Private Link service behind a Standard Load Balancer |\n| Google Cloud | Private Service Connect | endpoint | service attachment |\n\nThe mechanism has four parts.\n\n### 1. A network interface in the consumer's subnet\n- **What is created.** One or more network interfaces, each with a private IP from the consumer's\n  subnet; packets to that IP travel over the provider's internal network to the service.\n- **Overlapping CIDRs are fine.** The consumer only ever sees an IP in its own range, so address spaces\n  never have to be compatible. This is the main reason it beats peering for a SaaS whose thousands of\n  customers all use 10.0.0.0/8.\n- **One interface per availability zone**, because the interface lives in a zone; a single-zone endpoint\n  is a single point of failure and pushes cross-zone traffic.\n\n### 2. A service published on the provider side\n- **First-party services** (model APIs, storage, key management): the cloud publishes them for you.\n- **Your own SaaS:** you publish it yourself (see the table above).\n- **Decide who may connect** by allowlisting customer accounts or requiring manual acceptance of each\n  connection request, because otherwise anyone who learns the service name could attach to it.\n\n### 3. DNS that points the normal hostname at the private IP\n- **Why DNS matters.** Applications and SDKs use the service's ordinary hostname; if it still resolves to\n  a public IP, the endpoint is never used.\n- **The override.** The endpoint comes with a private DNS zone that overrides the public record inside\n  the consumer network, so the same hostname resolves to the interface's private IP.\n- **Hostname unchanged on purpose**, because TLS certificates still validate and no SDK needs\n  reconfiguring.\n- **Callers outside the VPC** (on-prem over a VPN, or another network) need their DNS queries forwarded\n  into the cloud resolver, otherwise they resolve publicly.\n\n### 4. Policy on both ends\n- **Connectivity is not authorization.** Calls still need IAM credentials or API keys.\n- **Endpoint policy:** which principals and actions may use this endpoint.\n- **Service-side rule:** a resource policy or network rule saying \"only accept requests arriving via\n  endpoint X\". The combination is what makes stolen credentials useless off-network.\n\n### Following one request\nPut the four parts together for a call from a pod to a model API:\n1. **Resolve.** The SDK asks the VPC resolver for `bedrock-runtime.<region>.amazonaws.com`; the private\n   zone answers with the endpoint interfaces' private IPs (say 10.20.1.15 and 10.20.2.15, one per zone).\n2. **Connect.** The pod opens TCP 443 to 10.20.1.15. The **security group on the endpoint interface**\n   must allow 443 from the pod's subnet. It is a separate control from the pod's own egress rules, and\n   forgetting it is the classic \"DNS is right but it still times out\" failure.\n3. **Carry.** The provider network takes the flow to the service's load balancer. The service never sees\n   a route into your VPC; it sees a connection tagged with the endpoint it came through, which is what\n   resource policies such as \"only via endpoint X\" match on.\n4. **Authenticate.** TLS is negotiated against the ordinary hostname, so the provider's public\n   certificate validates; then the API key or IAM signature is checked as usual.\n\n- **Customer identity for your own SaaS.** On AWS, enabling Proxy Protocol v2 on the NLB is how your\n  application learns which customer endpoint a connection came from, *because* the source IP alone is\n  not a reliable customer identifier.\n- **One-way by design.** Connections are initiated by the consumer toward the service; the provider\n  cannot open connections back into the consumer network through the endpoint, which is why security\n  teams approve it more easily than a VPN or peering link.\n\n### Gateway endpoints (AWS only)\n- **Older, different mechanism** available only for S3 and DynamoDB: a route-table entry (a prefix list)\n  instead of an interface.\n- **Cost and reach.** Cost nothing extra, but only work from inside that VPC, not from on-prem or peered\n  networks.\n- **Elsewhere.** Azure and Google Cloud have no direct equivalent; their closest analogues are service\n  endpoints and Private Google Access respectively.","example":"### Deploy-time check that SDK hostnames resolve privately\n- **Setting.** A team runs a RAG service in a private subnet with no NAT. They created an interface\n  endpoint for the model runtime API and one for object storage.\n- **Why check.** A failed check is the earliest signal that private DNS is off, which otherwise shows up\n  only as timeouts (no egress) or, worse, silent internet traffic (if egress exists).\n\n```python\nimport ipaddress, socket, sys\n\n# Hostnames the application's SDKs actually call (region-specific in real deployments).\nREQUIRED_PRIVATE = [\n    \"bedrock-runtime.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com\",\n    \"s3.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com\",\n    \"vectors.internal.example.com\",   # our own SaaS exposed through a private endpoint\n]\n\ndef resolved_ips(host):\n    infos = socket.getaddrinfo(host, 443, proto=socket.IPPROTO_TCP)\n    return sorted({ipaddress.ip_address(info[4][0]) for info in infos})\n\nfailures = []\nfor host in REQUIRED_PRIVATE:\n    try:\n        ips = resolved_ips(host)\n    except socket.gaierror as e:\n        failures.append(f\"{host}: does not resolve ({e})\"); continue\n    public = [str(ip) for ip in ips if not ip.is_private]\n    if public:\n        failures.append(f\"{host}: resolves to public {public}; private DNS not active here\")\n    else:\n        print(f\"ok   {host} -> {[str(ip) for ip in ips]}\")\n\nif failures:\n    print(\"\\n\".join(\"FAIL \" + f for f in failures)); sys.exit(1)\n```\n\n- **Run it from inside the workload** (a pod, a task), not a laptop, because resolution depends on which\n  resolver the caller uses.\n- **Storage may use a gateway route** rather than a private IP; check the route table for those.\n\n### The endpoint in Terraform, with the security group that usually gets forgotten\nOne interface per zone, private DNS on, and an ingress rule for 443 from the workload subnets.\n\n```hcl\nresource \"aws_vpc_endpoint\" \"bedrock_runtime\" {\n  vpc_id              = aws_vpc.main.id\n  service_name        = \"com.amazonaws.eu-west-1.bedrock-runtime\"\n  vpc_endpoint_type   = \"Interface\"\n  subnet_ids          = [aws_subnet.app_a.id, aws_subnet.app_b.id]   # one per AZ\n  security_group_ids  = [aws_security_group.endpoint.id]\n  private_dns_enabled = true                                          # the DNS half\n}\n\nresource \"aws_security_group\" \"endpoint\" {\n  vpc_id = aws_vpc.main.id\n  ingress {\n    from_port   = 443\n    to_port     = 443\n    protocol    = \"tcp\"\n    cidr_blocks = [aws_subnet.app_a.cidr_block, aws_subnet.app_b.cidr_block]\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: three symptoms, three different causes\n| Symptom seen | What the lookup from the pod shows | Cause | Fix |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Flow logs show bytes via NAT to the API | public IP | private DNS off or zone not linked to this VPC | enable private DNS, link the zone |\n| Timeouts, hostname resolves to 10.20.1.15 | private IP | endpoint security group blocks 443 | admit 443 from the workload subnet |\n| Works in cluster, fails from data centre | public IP on-prem only | no DNS forwarding from on-prem | forward the domain to the cloud resolver |\n\nThe second row is the one teams misdiagnose as a provider outage, because DNS already looks correct.","patterns":["**Fail closed at the network layer.** Remove the NAT route from subnets that only need endpointed services, so a call that resolves publicly fails loudly instead of leaking. Monitor VPC flow logs for any bytes to the service via NAT; the target is zero.","**Restrict the service to the endpoint.** For S3, a bucket policy that denies requests unless the `aws:SourceVpce` condition key matches your endpoint id; for Azure services, set public network access to disabled. Monitor denied requests: a spike means a caller is still on the public path.","**Shared endpoint hub.** With dozens of services across many VPCs, host endpoints once in a central VPC and share the private zones, because per-endpoint, per-zone charges add up. Monitor endpoint count and cost per VPC.","**Hybrid DNS forwarding.** Give on-prem resolvers a conditional forward for the service domains to a cloud inbound resolver (Route 53 Resolver inbound endpoints on AWS, Azure DNS Private Resolver on Azure). Monitor the DNS check above run from an on-prem host.","**Manual acceptance for your SaaS endpoint service.** Set `acceptance_required = true` and an explicit principal allowlist on the Terraform `aws_vpc_endpoint_service`, so unknown accounts never attach. Review the connection list on a schedule.","**DNS check as a deploy gate.** Run the resolution script as a pre-start job in each environment and block rollout on failure; alert if it ever flips from private to public."],"diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant P as Pod in private subnet\n    participant R as VPC resolver\n    participant E as Endpoint interface\n    participant L as Provider load balancer\n    participant S as Model API\n    P->>R: resolve the ordinary API hostname\n    R-->>P: private IPs, one per zone\n    P->>E: TCP 443 to private IP\n    Note over E: security group must admit 443\n    E->>L: carried on provider network, tagged with endpoint id\n    L->>S: forward request\n    Note over S: TLS on normal hostname, then IAM or API key, then only-via-endpoint policy\n    S-->>P: response on the same path","caption":"One call from a pod to a model API: the private DNS answer and the endpoint's security group are the\ntwo steps that most often break."},"interview":[{"q":"What is a private endpoint, and why is it preferred over a NAT gateway or IP allowlist?","a":"- **Definition:** a network interface with a private IP in the consumer's subnet, plus a private DNS\n  override, that carries traffic to one managed service over the provider's network.\n- **Versus NAT:** NAT opens the whole internet to the subnet; the endpoint reaches exactly one service.\n- **Versus allowlists:** provider IP pools are shared and change, so allowlists end up admitting a large\n  slice of the cloud."},{"q":"DNS resolves to a private IP but every call times out. Walk me through the debugging.","a":"- **Confirm the path:** from inside the pod, check the resolved IP is the endpoint interface and try a\n  TCP connect to 443.\n- **Check the endpoint's security group first:** it must admit 443 from the workload subnet, and it is\n  separate from the pod's egress rules.\n- **Then check** pod egress policy, network ACLs on the subnet, and that the endpoint has an interface in\n  the zone the pod is calling."},{"q":"When would you choose a VPN or peering instead of private endpoints?","a":"- **Many services or both directions:** endpoints carry traffic only to one published service, so broad\n  or bidirectional flows are simpler over peering or a transit network.\n- **Non-cloud sources needing routes into the VPC:** a VPN joins networks, which endpoints deliberately\n  do not.\n- **Cost:** endpoints bill per endpoint, per zone and per GB, so dozens of services across many VPCs\n  favour a shared hub or a different topology."},{"q":"How do you make a stolen API key useless from outside the customer network?","a":"- **The endpoint alone does not:** it adds a private path but leaves the public one open.\n- **Restrict at the service:** disable public network access, or use a resource policy that only accepts\n  requests via this endpoint (for S3, the `aws:SourceVpce` condition).\n- **Prove it:** call from outside with a valid key and expect a denial; alert on denied-request spikes."},{"q":"You are offering your SaaS over PrivateLink to 300 enterprise customers. What do you design for?","a":"- **Publishing:** an endpoint service behind a Network Load Balancer, with manual acceptance or an\n  account allowlist so only contracted customers attach.\n- **Tenant identity:** Proxy Protocol v2 tells your app which endpoint a connection came from, because\n  source IPs overlap across customers using the same private ranges.\n- **Operations:** availability in every zone customers use, and a runbook for their DNS and security-group\n  setup, since most onboarding failures are on their side."}],"tradeoffs":["**Narrow path vs cost.** You gain a narrow, one-directional path to exactly one service, but pay per-endpoint, per-zone hourly and per-GB charges. With dozens of services across many VPCs, a central shared-endpoint VPC becomes cheaper.","**No public access vs convenience.** Disabling public access blocks leaked credentials from the internet, but also blocks your own engineers' laptops and CI runners unless they reach it over VPN or a private runner.","**No CIDR coordination vs flexibility.** Endpoints avoid the CIDR coordination peering or VPNs need, but only carry traffic to the published service. If workloads need many services or bidirectional flows, peering or a transit network is simpler.","**Path assurance vs data assurance.** Private networking reassures a security team about the path, but does not change who processes the data or where. If the concern is residency or training use, you need contractual and regional controls instead."],"pitfalls":["**Endpoint without private DNS.** Creating the endpoint but not enabling or linking the private DNS zone. Symptom: the SDK times out in a no-egress subnet, or flow logs show traffic still leaving through the NAT gateway.","**Forgetting hybrid callers.** On-prem and peered-VPC callers need DNS forwarding to the cloud resolver. Symptom: \"works from the cluster, fails from the data center\", with the hostname resolving to a public IP there.","**Default security group on the interface.** It does not admit 443 from the workload subnet. Symptom: the hostname correctly resolves to a private IP but every connection times out, which teams misdiagnose as a DNS or provider outage.","**Endpointing the API but not its dependencies.** Such as the identity provider, token service or a separate file-upload host. Symptom: authentication or uploads hang while plain calls succeed.","**Single-zone interface.** Symptom: a full outage of model calls during a zone incident and unexpected cross-zone data charges in normal operation.","**Auto-accept for any principal on your SaaS endpoint service.** Symptom: unknown accounts appear in the connection list, which an audit will flag immediately."],"when":"- **Use when** a customer's workload in a cloud network must call a managed service and policy says \"no\n  internet egress\" or \"no public endpoints for data services\". That covers most regulated buyers calling\n  hosted model APIs, storage and key management in their own account.\n- **Offer your SaaS this way**: it is the standard way to serve enterprise customers without a VPN.\n- **Prefer it over VPN or peering** when the flow is one-directional to a single service.\n- **Do not use it** as a substitute for authorization, encryption or data-processing terms.\n- **Measure:** flow logs (zero bytes to the service via NAT) and the service rejecting non-endpoint traffic.\n- **Interviews:** explaining the DNS override and the one-way property is what separates real experience\n  from slideware.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the endpoint, security group and private DNS resources in Terraform, and adds a deploy check that every SDK hostname resolves to a private address from inside the workload.","architect":"Decides where endpoints live (per VPC or a shared hub), which services must disable public access, and how on-prem DNS forwarding is designed so hybrid callers stay private.","fde":"Walks the customer's network team through publishing or consuming the endpoint, requests account allowlisting, and debugs the inevitable DNS resolution difference between clusters and laptops."},"check":{"q":"A customer created a private endpoint for a hosted LLM API, yet their security team's flow logs\nstill show traffic from the RAG service going out through the NAT gateway. What is the likely cause, and\nhow do you make the setup fail closed?","a":"The endpoint exists but the application's hostname still resolves to the public IP, usually\nbecause private DNS was not enabled on the endpoint, the private zone is not associated with that VPC,\nor the workload uses a custom resolver that does not forward to the cloud resolver. Fix the resolution,\nverify with a lookup from inside the pod, then make it fail closed: remove the NAT route from that\nsubnet so a call that resolves publicly fails loudly, set the service's resource policy to accept only requests via\nthis endpoint, and turn off public network access where the service supports it. Finally add the DNS\ncheck to deployment so a regression is caught before traffic flows."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why can a SaaS vendor offer private endpoints to hundreds of customers who all use overlapping 10.0.0.0/16 ranges?","options":["The vendor runs a separate VPC per customer with a unique CIDR","Each customer reaches the service through an interface with an IP from their own subnet, so the two address spaces are never joined","Private endpoints use IPv6 internally, so IPv4 overlap is irrelevant","The cloud's NAT gateway rewrites addresses in both directions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The consumer only routes to a private IP in its own range and the provider network carries the traffic behind a load balancer, so no routes are exchanged. Peering and VPNs join address spaces and therefore require non-overlapping ranges."},{"q":"An endpoint for a model API is created and healthy, but calls from on-prem servers over the VPN still go to the public internet. What is missing?","options":["A second endpoint in another availability zone","An endpoint policy allowing the on-prem IP range","DNS forwarding so on-prem resolvers send the service's hostname to the cloud resolver that holds the private zone","A BGP route advertising the service's public IP range over the VPN"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The private zone only answers inside the cloud network. On-prem resolvers still return the public record unless queries for that domain are forwarded inward; advertising public ranges over the VPN would be a fragile hack, not the design."},{"q":"A security reviewer says \"we have a private endpoint, so stolen API keys are harmless.\" What makes that statement true or false?","options":["It is true by default, because private endpoints encrypt the API key","It is false, because private endpoints only work for storage services","It is true, because the endpoint policy replaces the need for credentials","It is only true if the service also rejects requests that did not arrive through the endpoint, for example by disabling public access"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The endpoint adds a private path but does not remove the public one. Only a resource-side restriction, such as disabling public access or a policy requiring this endpoint, makes the key useless from outside."}]},{"key":"deployment/vpn","sources":["RFC 7296 — Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2)","RFC 4303 — IP Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP)","Official docs: WireGuard"],"why":"- **The data has no public IP.** Your RAG product runs in the cloud; the customer's contracts live in an\n  on-prem document store and their ticket history in a database in the same data center. Neither has,\n  nor will ever have, a public IP, yet ingestion workers must reach them on private addresses.\n- **Exposing them publicly is rejected.** A database or file share on the internet behind a firewall rule\n  fails the first security meeting, rightly: credentials become the only barrier.\n- **Disks or nightly SFTP exports go stale.** They work once, but give you stale data, a manual process\n  and no way to run live queries or tool calls.\n- **Dedicated circuits are slow to get.** AWS Direct Connect, Azure ExpressRoute or Google Cloud\n  Interconnect are right for heavy, permanent traffic, but take weeks to months through a carrier and\n  cost accordingly.\n- **A VPN is the middle path.** It reuses both sides' internet connections, adds encryption and mutual\n  authentication, and can be up in days, which matters during a pilot. Its weakness is the flip side:\n  once networks are joined, anything routable can talk unless you narrow it, so a VPN is a network\n  integration, not just an encryption feature.","how":"### Site-to-site IPsec\nMost enterprise site-to-site VPNs, including the managed VPN gateways of the major clouds, use IPsec\nnegotiated by IKEv2. Two phases:\n\n1. **IKE SA (authentication and key agreement).**\n   - The gateways exchange Diffie-Hellman values to derive shared keys, then authenticate each other with\n     a pre-shared key or certificates.\n   - Certificates are preferable at scale because a leaked PSK compromises every tunnel that shares it.\n   - The result is an encrypted control channel, usually over UDP 500, moving to UDP 4500 when NAT is\n     detected (NAT traversal), which is why firewalls on both sides must open those ports.\n   - Without NAT, data packets travel as raw ESP (IP protocol 50, not TCP or UDP), which firewalls must\n     permit as well.\n2. **Child SAs (the data plane).** Inside that channel the peers agree keys and traffic selectors for ESP,\n   the protocol that encrypts and authenticates each packet. Keys are rekeyed on a timer so a single\n   compromised key exposes only a bounded window.\n\n| Port or protocol | Used for | Firewall must allow |\n|---|---|---|\n| UDP 500 | IKE negotiation | both directions between peer IPs |\n| UDP 4500 | IKE and ESP when NAT is detected | both directions |\n| IP protocol 50 (ESP) | data packets without NAT | both directions |\n\n### Routing: policy-based vs route-based\n| | Policy-based | Route-based, static | Route-based, BGP |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| What decides encryption | configured source/destination prefixes | tunnel is a virtual interface; routes point at it | same, routes learned dynamically |\n| Adding a subnet | edit both ends | add a route | the other side advertises it |\n| Failover | manual or vendor-specific | needs tunnel monitoring | automatic: routes withdrawn when a tunnel fails |\n\n- **BGP is preferred for production** because each side advertises its prefixes and withdraws them when\n  a tunnel fails, so failover between redundant tunnels is automatic.\n- **Configure both tunnels.** Cloud VPN gateways typically give you two tunnels per connection,\n  terminating on different endpoints, precisely so the provider can maintain one without an outage.\n\n### What the packet pays\n- **Overhead.** Each packet gains ESP headers, padding and an authentication tag plus an outer IP header,\n  tens of bytes in total.\n- **Full-size packets no longer fit.** A 1,500-byte packet is fragmented or, if the \"don't fragment\" bit\n  is set and ICMP is blocked, silently dropped.\n- **MSS clamping.** Gateways clamp the TCP maximum segment size (MSS) in the SYN handshake so endpoints\n  send smaller segments. Cloud VPN docs typically recommend a clamp somewhere in the 1,350–1,400-byte\n  range, but use your provider's figure.\n- **Why at the gateway.** You cannot rely on every host's path-MTU discovery when firewalls drop the ICMP\n  messages it depends on. Clamping fixes TCP only; UDP traffic must be sized by the application.\n- **Throughput ceiling.** Per tunnel it is bounded by the gateway's crypto capacity and the internet path,\n  typically gigabit-class rather than the tens of gigabits a dedicated circuit offers (check your\n  provider's documented limits).\n\n### Remote-access VPN and WireGuard\n- **Client VPNs** connect a single device, for example an FDE's laptop, into a customer network.\n- **WireGuard** is a newer, deliberately small protocol: fixed modern cryptography, peers identified by\n  public keys, and a configuration of a few lines.\n- **Where you meet it.** Its simplicity makes it easy to audit and fast, but many enterprises standardize\n  on IPsec appliances, so you meet WireGuard more in your own infrastructure than in a bank's.\n\n### When address spaces overlap\nRouting only works if each side can tell local addresses from remote ones, so overlapping CIDRs are the\nfirst thing to check, not the last. Three ways out, in rough order of preference:\n1. **Terminate in a fresh network** whose range neither side uses; cheap when you control one end, as a\n   vendor usually does.\n2. **NAT on the gateway**, so the customer sees your traffic from an agreed non-overlapping range and you\n   reach their hosts through translated addresses. It works, but every log and firewall rule now speaks\n   two address vocabularies.\n3. **Re-address one side**, which is correct and almost never quick inside an enterprise.\n\n### Narrowing the blast radius\nBecause a tunnel joins networks, scope it:\n- **Advertise only the specific subnets needed.**\n- **Filter on both ends:** firewall rules or security groups allowing only the required ports to the\n  required hosts.\n- **Terminate in a dedicated integration VPC**, not your core production network.","example":"### Worked scenario: a legal-tech pilot over site-to-site VPN\nA vendor pilots contract search for a firm whose 2 TB document archive sits on an on-prem file server,\nwith case metadata in a SQL Server instance. Plan: a site-to-site VPN from the firm's data center to a\ndedicated \"customer-integration\" VPC in the vendor's cloud account.\n\n1. **Addressing.** The firm uses 10.0.0.0/8 broadly and the vendor's VPC is 10.20.0.0/16, which overlaps.\n   Rather than re-IP anything, the vendor confirms the firm uses nothing in 172.16.0.0/12 and creates the\n   integration VPC as 172.31.240.0/22.\n2. **Scope.** The firm advertises only two /32 host routes (the file server and the database) over BGP.\n   Firewall rules on its side allow only SMB and the SQL Server port, from the ingestion workers' subnet,\n   to those two hosts.\n3. **Capacity.** The uplink and tunnel yield an effective 400 Mbit/s for bulk transfer (an assumption to\n   measure, not a promise). See the table below.\n4. **The classic failure.** Small metadata queries work, but large file reads stall after a few\n   kilobytes. Packet captures show oversized packets dropped with ICMP blocked; enabling MSS clamping on\n   the gateway fixes it.\n5. **Redundancy.** Both tunnels are configured, and the vendor tests failover by disabling one during a\n   sync and confirming BGP shifts traffic without dropping the job.\n\n| Transfer | Size | Arithmetic | Time |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Initial sync | 2 TB | 2 TB × 8 = 16 Tbit; at 0.4 Gbit/s that is 40,000 seconds | about 11 hours, scheduled over a weekend with rate limiting to protect business traffic |\n| Daily delta | around 5 GB | 40 Gbit ÷ 0.4 Gbit/s | about 100 s, under two minutes |\n\n### Capacity and MSS calculator\nPlan the sync window and the clamp value before the change request goes in.\n\n```python\ndef bulk_sync_hours(data_tb, effective_mbit_s):\n    bits = data_tb * 1e12 * 8\n    return bits / (effective_mbit_s * 1e6) / 3600\n\ndef mss_for(tunnel_mtu, ip_header=20, tcp_header=20):\n    return tunnel_mtu - ip_header - tcp_header\n\nfor tb, mbit in ((2, 400), (2, 100), (0.005, 400)):\n    h = bulk_sync_hours(tb, mbit)\n    print(f\"{tb} TB at {mbit} Mbit/s: {h:.1f} h ({h*3600:.0f} s)\")\nfor mtu in (1500, 1400):\n    print(f\"tunnel MTU {mtu}: clamp MSS to {mss_for(mtu)}\")\n# 2 TB at 400 Mbit/s: 11.1 h (40000 s)\n# 2 TB at 100 Mbit/s: 44.4 h (160000 s)\n# 0.005 TB at 400 Mbit/s: 0.0 h (100 s)\n# tunnel MTU 1500: clamp MSS to 1460\n# tunnel MTU 1400: clamp MSS to 1360\n```\n\n- **MSS is the MTU minus 40 bytes** of IPv4 and TCP headers (without options), so a 1,400-byte tunnel\n  MTU means clamping MSS to 1,360.\n\n### Remote access with WireGuard\nA minimal client config for an engineer's laptop that routes only the integration range through the\ntunnel. Keys are placeholders; each side holds its own private key.\n\n```ini\n[Interface]\nPrivateKey = <laptop private key>\nAddress = 172.31.250.10/32\n\n[Peer]\nPublicKey = <gateway public key>\nEndpoint = vpn.example.com:51820\nAllowedIPs = 172.31.240.0/22      # only the integration VPC, not 0.0.0.0/0\nPersistentKeepalive = 25\n```","patterns":["**Two tunnels, BGP, tested failover.** Configure both provider tunnels with BGP and, before go-live, disable one during a sync to confirm traffic shifts. Monitor tunnel state and BGP session flaps (on AWS Site-to-Site VPN, the CloudWatch `TunnelState` metric per tunnel).","**Clamp MSS on self-managed gateways.** On a Linux gateway, the iptables `TCPMSS` target rewrites MSS in SYN packets (rule below). Verify with `ping -M do -s <size> <host>` and watch for retransmissions of large segments.\n  ```\n  iptables -t mangle -A FORWARD -p tcp --tcp-flags SYN,RST SYN -j TCPMSS --clamp-mss-to-pmtu ```","**Host routes and port filters only.** Ask the customer to advertise /32 routes for the hosts you need and allow only those ports from your worker subnet. Review the advertised prefix list in every change.","**Integration VPC per customer.** Terminate each customer tunnel in its own small VPC with a range chosen to avoid theirs, so overlaps never touch production. Keep an address-allocation register.","**Rate-limited bulk sync.** Schedule large initial syncs off-hours with a bandwidth cap, then switch to incremental deltas. Monitor throughput against plan and the customer's uplink utilisation.","**DNS across the tunnel.** Forward the customer's internal domain to their resolvers over the tunnel so workers use hostnames, not IPs. Alert when a configured hostname stops resolving."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    W[\"Ingestion workers\"] --> IV[\"Integration VPC, non-overlapping range\"]\n    IV --> G[\"Cloud VPN gateway, MSS clamp\"]\n    G -->|\"tunnel 1, BGP\"| CG[\"Customer VPN gateway\"]\n    G -.->|\"tunnel 2, standby\"| CG\n    CG --> FW{\"Firewall: port and host allowed?\"}\n    FW -->|\"SMB\"| FS[(\"File server, /32 route\")]\n    FW -->|\"SQL port\"| DB[(\"Database, /32 route\")]\n    FW -->|\"anything else\"| X[\"Dropped\"]","caption":"Traffic from cloud workers to two on-prem hosts: scoped routes, a clamped MSS and two BGP tunnels are\nthe three design points."},"interview":[{"q":"What does a site-to-site IPsec VPN actually set up, and why does it need certain firewall ports?","a":"- **Two phases:** an IKE SA authenticates the gateways and agrees keys, then child SAs carry data\n  encrypted with ESP and rekeyed on a timer.\n- **Ports follow from that:** UDP 500 for IKE, UDP 4500 when NAT traversal is detected, and IP protocol\n  50 for raw ESP without NAT, so both firewalls must allow all three between the peers."},{"q":"The tunnel is up but no traffic from your VPC reaches the customer's hosts. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Check addressing first:** if ranges overlap, each side routes the other's addresses locally and\n  packets never enter the tunnel.\n- **Then routes:** confirm the needed prefixes are advertised over BGP (or statically configured) on both\n  ends and that your subnet route tables point at the gateway.\n- **Then filters:** security groups and the customer's firewall must allow your worker subnet to the\n  specific host and port; a capture on each gateway shows where packets stop."},{"q":"When would you not use a VPN for a customer integration?","a":"- **One-way calls to one cloud service:** a private endpoint is narrower and does not join address spaces.\n- **Large, permanent traffic or latency guarantees:** a dedicated circuit gives predictable bandwidth,\n  often with IPsec or MACsec on top for regulated customers.\n- **People needing a few internal tools:** per-application zero-trust access beats a full-network client VPN."},{"q":"How long will a 2 TB initial sync take over the tunnel, and how do you plan it?","a":"- **Arithmetic:** 2 TB × 8 = 16 Tbit; at an effective 400 Mbit/s that is 40,000 s, about 11 hours.\n- **Plan:** treat the rate as an assumption to measure, run the sync over a weekend with a bandwidth cap,\n  then switch to daily deltas (around 5 GB takes under two minutes).\n- **Make it resumable**, so a tunnel failover mid-sync does not restart the job."},{"q":"Why clamp MSS at the gateway instead of relying on path MTU discovery?","a":"- **PMTUD depends on ICMP** \"fragmentation needed\" messages, which many enterprise firewalls block, so\n  oversized don't-fragment packets vanish silently.\n- **Clamping rewrites the SYN handshake** so both ends send segments that fit, fixing every TCP flow\n  without touching hosts.\n- **Limit:** it fixes TCP only; UDP traffic must be sized by the application."}],"tradeoffs":["**Quick and cheap vs predictable.** A VPN stands up quickly over existing internet links, but throughput and latency vary with the internet path. Once traffic is large and permanent, a dedicated circuit pays for itself.","**Any protocol vs attack surface.** Joining networks enables any protocol in either direction, which helps legacy integrations, but widens the attack surface compared with a one-way private endpoint to a single service.","**Dedicated circuit vs encryption.** A circuit gives predictable bandwidth and latency, but is private rather than encrypted by default, so regulated customers often still run IPsec (or MACsec where offered) over it. The choice flips back to VPN alone when volumes are modest and lead time matters.","**Static routes vs BGP.** Static routes are simple for one or two subnets but carry no liveness signal, so failover depends on vendor-specific tunnel monitoring (dead-peer detection, route tracking) or manual edits. BGP adds configuration and debugging skill in return for standard, automatic failover.","**Client VPN vs zero-trust access.** Remote-access VPN gives engineers broad network access easily, but identity-aware zero-trust access per application is safer when many people need narrow access to a few internal tools."],"pitfalls":["**Overlapping CIDR ranges discovered after the tunnel is up.** Symptom: a healthy tunnel status with traffic that never arrives, because each side routes the other's addresses locally.","**No MSS clamping or path MTU discovery when ICMP is filtered.** Symptom: small requests succeed while large uploads, file reads or long model responses hang mid-transfer.","**Configuring only one of the two provided tunnels.** Symptom: an outage during the provider's routine maintenance window, often in the middle of the night.","**Advertising whole corporate supernets \"to keep it simple\".** Symptom: the security review finds the vendor network can reach thousands of customer hosts it never needed.","**Leaving DNS out of the design.** Symptom: IP addresses hard-coded in config because internal hostnames do not resolve across the tunnel, which then break when the customer re-IPs a server."],"when":"- **Use a site-to-site VPN when** cloud workloads need private, bidirectional or multi-protocol access to\n  a customer's on-prem systems: databases, file shares, legacy APIs, internal identity providers. It is\n  the default for pilots and moderate steady-state traffic.\n- **Prefer a private endpoint** when the flow is one-way to a single cloud service, and a dedicated\n  circuit when sustained volume or latency guarantees matter.\n- **For people rather than systems**, prefer per-application zero-trust access over a full-network\n  client VPN.\n- **Measure:** tunnel uptime, BGP state changes, throughput against the plan, and dropped or fragmented\n  packets.\n- **Interviews and design reviews:** mentioning address planning, scope and MTU unprompted signals that\n  you have actually operated one.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Configures ingestion workers to use internal DNS names over the tunnel, sets timeouts and retries that survive tunnel failover, and recognises an MTU stall from its distinctive symptom.","architect":"Chooses VPN versus private endpoint versus dedicated circuit, plans non-overlapping address space, and decides where the tunnel terminates so its blast radius stays small.","fde":"Fills in the customer's VPN request form with exact peer IPs, prefixes and ports, schedules the bulk sync around their business hours, and joins the call when the tunnel will not come up."},"check":{"q":"Your ingestion job reads from a customer's on-prem database over a new site-to-site VPN. Short\nqueries return instantly, but any query returning more than a few kilobytes hangs until timeout. The\ntunnel shows \"up\". What is happening and how do you confirm and fix it?","a":"That size-dependent pattern points to MTU. IPsec encapsulation adds tens of bytes, so full-size\npackets exceed the tunnel MTU; if \"don't fragment\" is set and ICMP \"fragmentation needed\" messages are\nblocked by a firewall, those packets are silently dropped while small ones pass. Confirm by sending pings\nwith the don't-fragment flag (on Linux, `ping -M do -s 1400 <host>`) at decreasing sizes to find the largest packet that passes, or by capturing\ntraffic and seeing retransmissions of large segments. Fix it by enabling TCP MSS clamping on the VPN\ngateway to a value that fits the tunnel overhead, or by allowing the relevant ICMP messages so path MTU\ndiscovery works. Then retest with a large result set."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why do production site-to-site VPNs usually use BGP rather than static routes?","options":["BGP encrypts traffic more strongly than static routing","BGP advertises and withdraws prefixes dynamically, so traffic fails over to the redundant tunnel automatically","Static routes cannot be used with IPsec","BGP removes the need for non-overlapping address ranges"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A static route says nothing about whether its tunnel is alive, so failover relies on vendor-specific tunnel monitoring or manual action. BGP withdraws routes from a failed tunnel as part of the protocol. Encryption is ESP's job, and overlapping ranges remain a problem with either routing method."},{"q":"A customer asks for a VPN so your SaaS can call their single internal REST API. Their network team worries about exposure. What is the strongest scoping you can offer?","options":["Use a pre-shared key instead of certificates to keep configuration simple","Advertise the customer's full 10.0.0.0/8 so routing never needs updating","Terminate in a dedicated integration network, advertise only the API host's address, and allow only its port from your worker subnet","Enable split tunneling on every engineer's laptop"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Scoping routes to a single host and filtering ports on both sides limits what the joined networks can reach. Broad advertisements create the exposure the network team fears, and PSK choice affects authentication rather than reach."},{"q":"When is a dedicated circuit such as Direct Connect or ExpressRoute a better choice than a VPN?","options":["When the integration is a two-week pilot that needs to start tomorrow","Whenever the data is confidential, because VPNs are not encrypted","When the customer has overlapping CIDR ranges","When traffic is large and permanent or needs predictable latency and bandwidth that the internet path cannot guarantee"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Dedicated circuits trade long lead times and higher fixed cost for consistent bandwidth and latency. VPNs are encrypted, and a circuit does nothing to fix overlapping address ranges, which still require re-addressing or NAT."}]},{"key":"deployment/terraform","sources":["Official docs: Terraform","Official docs: OpenTofu","Kief Morris, 2020 — Infrastructure as Code (2nd edition)"],"why":"- **An AI platform is a lot of infrastructure.** A VPC with private subnets, private endpoints to the\n  model API and storage, a Kubernetes cluster with a GPU node pool, a vector database, KMS keys, IAM\n  roles, log sinks.\n- **Console work works once.** Then you need a staging copy, the customer wants it in their own account\n  in another region, and an auditor asks who opened port 443 to the world and when. Console changes\n  leave no reviewable record, cannot be reproduced exactly, and drift: the third environment is never\n  quite like the first.\n- **Imperative scripts are brittle.** A shell file of CLI calls encodes steps rather than outcomes, so\n  running it twice fails (\"already exists\") or duplicates resources, and it cannot say what would change\n  before changing it.\n- **Declarative IaC makes the files the source of truth.** You state what should exist; the tool works\n  out the create, update and delete calls and shows them to a reviewer first. Changes go through pull\n  requests, so a security reviewer approves the diff rather than trusting a screenshot.\n- **Decisive for forward-deployed work.** The same reviewed module can be run by the customer's own\n  platform team, in their account, under their change control.","how":"### Configuration, providers and the resource graph\n- **Resources in HCL.** You write `.tf` files declaring resources (`aws_vpc_endpoint`,\n  `google_container_node_pool`, `azurerm_private_endpoint`) with arguments.\n- **Providers are plugins** translating each resource type into API calls for a cloud or service; that\n  plugin model is why one tool covers AWS, Azure, Google Cloud, Kubernetes, DNS providers and many SaaS\n  products.\n- **A dependency graph.** References between resources (a security group ID used by an endpoint) become\n  a graph; independent resources are created in parallel and dependent ones in order, which is why you\n  rarely write ordering by hand.\n\n### State\n- **What it records.** What Terraform created, and the real IDs, in a state file.\n- **Why it exists.** The tool must map `aws_s3_bucket.documents` in your code to one specific bucket in\n  the account, and know which resources it owns so it can delete one you removed from the code.\n- **Remote with locking.** Teams store state remotely (object storage, Terraform Cloud/HCP, or similar)\n  with locking so two applies cannot run at once and corrupt it.\n- **It is a secret.** State holds every attribute the provider returns, including generated passwords and\n  keys, in plain text, so encrypt and access-control it like a secret.\n\n### Plan and apply\n1. **Plan.** `terraform plan` refreshes state from the real APIs, compares with your configuration and\n   prints the create/update/replace/destroy actions.\n2. **Read \"replace\" carefully.** Some argument changes cannot be updated in place and force\n   destroy-then-create, which for a database or bucket means data loss.\n3. **Apply exactly what was reviewed.** `terraform apply` executes a plan; in CI you save the plan file\n   and apply exactly that plan after approval, so what was reviewed is what runs.\n\n### How the diff decides update versus replace\n- **Three inputs per resource:** the configuration, the last-recorded state, and what the refresh read\n  from the API.\n- **The provider schema decides.** If an argument differs, the schema says whether the API can change it\n  in place (a tag, an instance count) or whether it is immutable once created (a bucket name, a\n  cluster's subnet), in which case the action becomes replace.\n- **Order of replacement.** Defaults to destroy-then-create *because* many names must be unique;\n  `lifecycle { create_before_destroy = true }` reverses the order for stateless things like launch\n  templates or certificates, so there is no gap.\n\n| Plan symbol | Action | Risk |\n|---|---|---|\n| `+` | create | low |\n| `~` | update in place | usually low; read the changed arguments |\n| `-/+` | destroy then create (replace) | data loss for stateful resources |\n| `+/-` | create then destroy (`create_before_destroy`) | brief duplicate; names must allow it |\n| `-` | destroy | check it was intended |\n\n### Resource addresses are identity\n- **State keys objects by code address** (`module.rag.aws_s3_bucket.documents`), not by cloud ID.\n- **So refactors look like replacement.** Renaming a block, moving it into a module or switching `count`\n  to `for_each` reads as \"delete the old address, create a new one\", even though nothing real changed.\n- **The fix.** A `moved { from = ..., to = ... }` block (or `terraform state mv`) says the object merely\n  changed address, and the plan becomes a no-op. This is the mechanism behind most \"harmless refactor\n  recreated the database\" stories.\n\n### Modules, environments and drift\n- **Modules are functions.** They package resources behind input variables and outputs. A \"customer\n  deployment\" module might take a VPC ID, subnet IDs, region and KMS key, and output the service URL;\n  each environment or customer is a small root configuration calling it with different inputs.\n- **Drift.** If someone changes a resource in the console, the next plan shows drift and proposes\n  reverting it.\n- **Import.** Existing hand-built resources are adopted with an `import` block or `terraform import`, so\n  they come under management without being recreated.\n\n### One state per environment\nEach environment or customer gets its own state, so a plan for staging cannot touch production.\n\n| Layout | Pros | Cons |\n|---|---|---|\n| Separate root directories per environment | explicit; each has its own backend key and credentials | some duplication |\n| CLI workspaces sharing one configuration | less duplication | same credentials; an easy-to-forget `terraform workspace select` |\n\nFor customer accounts with separate change boards, separate roots with separate backends is the usual\nchoice *because* the isolation boundary should match the approval boundary.\n\n### Alternatives\n| Tool | Shape | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| CloudFormation (AWS), Bicep/ARM (Azure) | native, single-cloud | no state file to manage, since the provider tracks it |\n| Pulumi, AWS CDK | general-purpose languages | helps loops and abstraction; reviews depend on reading code rather than a declarative file |\n| OpenTofu | community fork of Terraform | created after HashiCorp moved Terraform to the Business Source License in 2023; keeps HCL and the provider ecosystem; open source |","example":"### Module for private model access\n- **What it contains.** A locked remote state, a security group that only admits the app subnet, an\n  interface endpoint for the model runtime with private DNS, and a document bucket protected from\n  accidental destruction.\n- **Check the names.** Argument names follow the AWS provider; check them against the provider version\n  you pin.\n\n```hcl\nterraform {\n  required_providers {\n    aws = { source = \"hashicorp/aws\", version = \"~> 5.0\" }\n  }\n  backend \"s3\" {\n    bucket         = \"acme-tfstate\"\n    key            = \"ai-platform/prod.tfstate\"\n    region         = \"eu-west-1\"\n    use_lockfile   = true         # S3-native lock file (added in 1.10, GA in 1.11); supersedes dynamodb_table\n    encrypt        = true\n  }\n}\n\nvariable \"vpc_id\"             { type = string }\nvariable \"private_subnet_ids\" { type = list(string) }\nvariable \"app_cidr\"           { type = string }\n\nresource \"aws_security_group\" \"model_ep\" {\n  name   = \"model-runtime-endpoint\"\n  vpc_id = var.vpc_id\n  ingress {\n    from_port   = 443\n    to_port     = 443\n    protocol    = \"tcp\"\n    cidr_blocks = [var.app_cidr]\n  }\n}\n\nresource \"aws_vpc_endpoint\" \"model_runtime\" {\n  vpc_id              = var.vpc_id\n  service_name        = \"com.amazonaws.eu-west-1.bedrock-runtime\"\n  vpc_endpoint_type   = \"Interface\"\n  subnet_ids          = var.private_subnet_ids     # one subnet per availability zone\n  security_group_ids  = [aws_security_group.model_ep.id]\n  private_dns_enabled = true\n}\n\nresource \"aws_s3_bucket\" \"documents\" {\n  bucket = \"acme-rag-documents-prod\"\n  lifecycle { prevent_destroy = true }   # plan fails instead of deleting customer data\n}\n```\n\n- **Fresh account:** `terraform plan` shows three resources to add.\n- **Changing `bucket` later** would show a replace, and `prevent_destroy` turns that into an error\n  rather than a deleted archive.\n- **Limit of the guard.** It lives in the resource block, so it does not protect against someone\n  deleting the block itself; pair it with bucket versioning or a deletion-protection setting on the\n  cloud side.\n\n### Refactor and adopt without recreating anything\nMoving the bucket into a module, and adopting a hand-built KMS key, both as plan-visible code.\n\n```hcl\nmoved {\n  from = aws_s3_bucket.documents\n  to   = module.rag.aws_s3_bucket.documents\n}\n\nimport {\n  to = aws_kms_key.rag\n  id = \"1234abcd-12ab-34cd-56ef-1234567890ab\"   # the existing key's ID\n}\n```\n\nExpected plan: zero destroys, one import, and no change for the moved bucket. Anything else means the\nrefactor is not what you think.\n\n### CI guard that blocks replacing data resources\nReads the JSON form of a saved plan (`terraform show -json tfplan`) and fails the pipeline if a protected\nresource type would be destroyed or replaced.\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Fail CI if a Terraform plan deletes or replaces protected resources.\nUsage: terraform show -json tfplan > plan.json && python plan_guard.py plan.json\"\"\"\nimport json, sys\n\nPROTECTED = {\"aws_s3_bucket\", \"aws_db_instance\", \"aws_opensearch_domain\", \"aws_kms_key\"}\n\ndef risky_changes(plan):\n    for rc in plan.get(\"resource_changes\", []):\n        actions = rc[\"change\"][\"actions\"]          # e.g. [\"update\"], [\"delete\", \"create\"]\n        if \"delete\" in actions:\n            kind = \"REPLACE\" if \"create\" in actions else \"DESTROY\"\n            yield kind, rc[\"address\"], rc[\"type\"]\n\ndef main(path):\n    plan = json.load(open(path))\n    blocked = 0\n    for kind, address, rtype in risky_changes(plan):\n        flag = \"BLOCK\" if rtype in PROTECTED else \"review\"\n        blocked += flag == \"BLOCK\"\n        print(f\"{flag:6} {kind:7} {address}\")\n    sys.exit(1 if blocked else 0)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main(sys.argv[1])\n# BLOCK  REPLACE module.rag.aws_s3_bucket.documents\n# review REPLACE aws_launch_template.gpu\n```","patterns":["**Saved plan, exact apply.** In CI run `terraform plan -out=tfplan` on every pull request, attach the rendered plan to the review or change ticket, and apply with `terraform apply tfplan` after approval. Monitor applies that ran without a matching reviewed plan; the target is none.","**Scheduled drift detection.** Run `terraform plan -detailed-exitcode` nightly against main; exit code 2 means changes are pending. Alert on non-empty plans and trace each to a console change or a missing back-port.","**Pin providers and commit the lock file.** Use version constraints like `~> 5.0` and commit `.terraform.lock.hcl`; upgrade deliberately with `terraform init -upgrade` in its own pull request. Watch for plans that change resources nobody touched.","**Provider mirror for isolated accounts.** Build it with `terraform providers mirror <dir>`, ship it in the release bundle, and point the customer's CLI configuration at it with a `filesystem_mirror` block, so `terraform init` never reaches the public registry.","**Split state by layer.** Separate network, cluster and application states so a routine app change cannot plan against the VPC. Pass values between them with outputs and data sources, and track plan duration per state.","**Guard data resources twice.** `prevent_destroy` in code plus versioning or deletion protection in the cloud, and the plan-guard script above in CI for anything stateful."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    C[\"HCL code in a pull request\"] --> P[\"terraform plan\"]\n    S[(\"Remote state with lock\")] --> P\n    API[\"Cloud provider APIs\"] -->|\"refresh\"| P\n    P --> F[\"Saved plan file\"]\n    F --> R{\"Reviewer: any replace or destroy?\"}\n    R -->|\"yes, unexpected\"| C\n    R -->|\"approved\"| A[\"terraform apply saved plan\"]\n    A --> API\n    A -->|\"new IDs and attributes\"| S","caption":"The reviewed plan, not the code, is what gets applied; state is the bridge between code addresses and\nreal objects."},"interview":[{"q":"What is declarative IaC, and why is it better than a script of CLI calls?","a":"- **Declarative:** you describe the desired end state and the tool computes create, update and delete\n  calls by diffing code, state and the real APIs.\n- **Why better:** it is idempotent (running twice changes nothing), it can show a plan before acting, and\n  changes go through code review instead of screenshots.\n- **Scripts encode steps**, so re-runs fail with \"already exists\" or duplicate resources."},{"q":"A pull request that only renamed modules shows 14 destroys and 14 creates. What happened and what do you do?","a":"- **Cause:** state keys objects by address, so new addresses look like new resources and old ones look\n  removed.\n- **Fix:** add `moved` blocks from each old address to the new one (or `terraform state mv`) and re-plan\n  until it is a no-op.\n- **Guard:** a CI check that fails on destroys of stateful types would have stopped the apply."},{"q":"When would you choose CloudFormation, Bicep or Pulumi over Terraform?","a":"- **Single-cloud and mandated:** native templates remove the state file and locking you would otherwise\n  operate.\n- **Logic-heavy topologies:** Pulumi or CDK let you use loops and abstraction in a real language, at the\n  cost of reviewers reading code rather than declarative files.\n- **Terraform or OpenTofu wins** when you deploy across clouds and many customer accounts, or the\n  customer's team already runs it."},{"q":"How do you keep Terraform state safe in production?","a":"- **Remote backend with locking** so two applies cannot corrupt it; the S3 backend supports a native\n  lock file (`use_lockfile`).\n- **Encrypt and restrict it** because it stores generated passwords and keys in plain text.\n- **One state per environment or customer**, matching the approval boundary, plus versioning on the\n  state bucket so a bad write can be rolled back."},{"q":"How do you deliver Terraform into an air-gapped customer account?","a":"- **Ship providers with the release:** build a mirror with `terraform providers mirror`, and configure a\n  filesystem mirror so `terraform init` never contacts the registry.\n- **Pin versions and include the lock file** so the mirror and the plan agree.\n- **Ship modules as files** with an expected-plan summary, since the customer cannot pull them from a\n  registry either."}],"tradeoffs":["**Reviewable plans vs critical state.** Declarative plans make every change reviewable before it happens, but the state file becomes critical shared infrastructure; losing or corrupting it is worse than losing the code.","**Multi-cloud breadth vs native simplicity.** One tool across clouds and SaaS providers helps teams deploying into varied customer accounts, but a single-cloud team may find native CloudFormation or Bicep simpler since there is no state to operate.","**Restricted HCL vs expressive code.** HCL keeps configurations readable for security reviewers, but complex logic (dynamic topologies, heavy loops) is awkward; general-purpose IaC tools win when logic dominates.","**One big state vs split states.** Large single state files give one view of everything but make plans slow and blast radius big. Splitting by layer (network, cluster, app) limits damage at the cost of cross-state references."],"pitfalls":["**Committing state or secrets to git, or storing state unencrypted.** Symptom: a secret scanner alert, or an auditor finding database passwords readable by everyone with repository access.","**Approving a plan without reading \"must be replaced\" lines.** Symptom: a vector database or bucket recreated empty after an apparently harmless argument change.","**Refactoring names or module structure without `moved` blocks.** Symptom: a plan showing an equal number of destroys and creates for a pull request that \"only renamed things\".","**Unpinned provider versions or an uncommitted `.terraform.lock.hcl`.** Symptom: a plan that suddenly proposes changes nobody made, after a new provider release changed a default.","**Emergency console fixes never back-ported.** Symptom: the next routine apply silently reverts the fix and re-causes the incident.","**Assuming an air-gapped environment can download providers.** Symptom: `terraform init` fails, fixed only by shipping a provider mirror with the release bundle."],"when":"- **Use IaC for** anything that must exist in more than one environment or survive an audit: every\n  production AI deployment, and every customer-VPC deployment.\n- **Terraform or OpenTofu by default** when you deploy across clouds or into many customer accounts, or\n  when the customer's platform team already uses it.\n- **Native templates** when an organization is single-cloud and mandates them.\n- **Not for application rollout.** Keep container versions and model weights in a deploy tool such as\n  Helm or Argo rather than Terraform, since those change far more often.\n- **Measure:** drift (plans that are not empty on main) and time to stand up a new environment.\n- **Interviews:** expect probes on state, plan review and the replace hazard.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes modules for GPU node pools, endpoints and buckets, runs plan in CI on every pull request, and reads each replace or destroy action before approving.","architect":"Sets the state layout, module boundaries and versioning, decides which layer IaC owns versus the deploy tool, and makes plan output part of the change-approval evidence.","fde":"Hands the customer a versioned module and variables file their platform team can run in their own account, plus a provider mirror when their environment cannot reach the registry."},"check":{"q":"A customer's platform team must run your deployment in their own AWS account under their change\nboard, and they forbid your engineers from having write access. How do you structure the Terraform\ndelivery so it is reviewable, repeatable and safe for them to run?","a":"Ship a versioned module with a narrow, documented variable interface (VPC and subnet IDs, KMS key,\nregion, sizing), no hard-coded account details, and pinned provider versions. Their root configuration\ncalls it, with state in their backend under their access controls and locking. Their CI runs plan and\nattaches the saved plan to the change ticket; the board reviews it, and apply executes exactly that\nplan. Protect data resources with `prevent_destroy`, document every replace-triggering argument, and\npublish an IAM policy listing the permissions the apply role needs. For upgrades, release a new module\nversion with a changelog and an expected-plan summary so they can compare. If the account is isolated,\ninclude a provider mirror."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Terraform need a state file at all, when it could just query the cloud APIs?","options":["To cache API responses so plans run faster","To map each resource in the code to the specific real object it manages, including ones removed from code that must be deleted","Because cloud APIs do not report resource attributes","To store the HCL source in a binary form"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Without state, Terraform cannot know which real bucket corresponds to a resource block, or which objects it owns when a block is deleted. Refreshing does query the APIs, but only for objects state identifies."},{"q":"A reviewer sees a plan line saying the vector database cluster \"must be replaced\" after a minor-looking argument change. What is the right response?","options":["Approve it, since Terraform preserves data during replacement","Run apply twice so the second run cancels the replacement","Treat it as destroy-and-recreate with data loss, and find an in-place path or a planned migration before applying","Delete the state file so Terraform forgets the cluster exists"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Replacement destroys the existing resource and creates a new one, so stored data is lost unless migrated. Deleting state would make Terraform try to create a duplicate and abandon the original unmanaged."},{"q":"Why might an AWS-only team reasonably prefer CloudFormation over Terraform?","options":["CloudFormation supports more third-party SaaS providers","Terraform cannot manage AWS networking resources","CloudFormation lets you write infrastructure in Python","AWS tracks the stack state itself, so there is no separate state file and locking setup to operate"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The native service manages state and rollback for you, which removes an operational burden. Terraform's advantage is breadth across clouds and SaaS, and Python-based infrastructure belongs to CDK or Pulumi."}]},{"key":"deployment/security-questionnaires","sources":["Official docs: Cloud Security Alliance — Consensus Assessments Initiative Questionnaire (CAIQ)","Official docs: Shared Assessments — Standardized Information Gathering (SIG) questionnaire","Official docs: AICPA — SOC 2"],"why":"- **Buyers cannot inspect every vendor.** A large company may have thousands of vendors, each a possible\n  path to a breach, so its risk team standardizes the inquiry: an industry template such as the Shared\n  Assessments SIG or the Cloud Security Alliance's CAIQ, or a custom spreadsheet built from the buyer's\n  own control framework.\n- **Answers have consequences.** They are scored, gaps become findings, and serious gaps block\n  procurement or become contract clauses.\n- **AI vendors get extra scrutiny** because the product's core function is sending customer data to a\n  model. Buyers ask what they never asked a CRM vendor: is our data used to train any model, which model\n  providers see it, how long do they retain prompts, can your agent take actions, how do you stop prompt\n  injection from exfiltrating data.\n- **The naive approaches fail.** Answering from memory gives inconsistent answers across customers, which\n  a sophisticated buyer or a later dispute will expose. Answering \"Yes\" to everything creates\n  misrepresentation risk, because questionnaires are often incorporated by reference into the contract.\n- **It is an interface, not paperwork.** Treated as separate from engineering, answers drift as the\n  system changes. The questionnaire is a structured interface between your actual controls and the\n  buyer's risk model, and should be maintained like one.","how":"### What arrives\n- **Format.** Typically a spreadsheet or a portal from a third-party risk platform, with\n  yes/no/not-applicable columns, a free-text comment and an evidence-upload field per question.\n- **Domains:** governance and policies, access control and identity, encryption in transit and at rest,\n  key management, network security, vulnerability management and penetration testing, logging and\n  monitoring, incident response and notification, business continuity, data retention and deletion,\n  privacy, subprocessors, secure development, and increasingly an AI section.\n\n### What happens on the buyer's side\nUnderstanding the reviewer's workflow explains what they reward:\n1. **Inherent-risk tiering.** Before sending anything, the risk team classifies the engagement by what\n   you will touch: data sensitivity, volume, network or system access, and how critical you are to\n   operations.\n   - A vendor processing patient records with agent tool access lands in the top tier; one that sees only\n     marketing copy does not.\n   - The tier sets the depth, *because* the team's review hours are the scarce resource.\n   - Templates are built for this: the SIG comes in a shorter Lite and a fuller Core form, and CAIQ maps\n     each question to a control in the CSA Cloud Controls Matrix.\n2. **Scoring and findings.** Each answer is compared with the buyer's required control. A \"No\" or a vague\n   answer on a required control becomes a finding with a severity.\n3. **Follow-up and evidence requests.** Reviewers ask for proof on the answers that matter most to their\n   tier; precise first answers mean fewer rounds.\n4. **Disposition.** Findings are accepted as risk, mitigated by a compensating control, or turned into\n   remediation commitments with dates, which often land in the contract.\n5. **Reassessment.** High-tier vendors are typically re-reviewed periodically and on material change, so\n   today's answer will be compared with next year's.\n\nThe upshot: a reviewer wants residual risk they can sign off on, not a perfect score, so a clearly scoped\ngap with a mitigation is easier to approve than an ambiguous \"Yes\".\n\n### Evidence that shortcuts it\nBuyers accept independent assurance in place of many answers:\n\n| Evidence | What it shows |\n|---|---|\n| SOC 2 Type II report | an auditor's opinion that controls operated effectively over a period |\n| SOC 2 Type I report | control design at a point in time only |\n| ISO/IEC 27001 certificate | a certified information security management system |\n| Recent penetration test summary | independent testing, without the full findings |\n| Public subprocessor list, trust center page | answers many questions at once |\n\nSending the report early, under NDA, often removes a large share of the questionnaire.\n\n### The answer library\n- **One library of approved answers**, each tied to the underlying control, an owner (security,\n  infrastructure, legal, ML), the evidence that proves it and a review date.\n- **Answering is retrieval.** New questionnaires are answered by matching each question to library\n  entries and adapting wording; many teams use RAG over the library to draft responses.\n- **A human owner approves every answer**, because a confidently wrong generated answer is precisely the\n  misrepresentation you are trying to avoid.\n\n### Writing a good answer\n- **Fact, scope, mechanism, evidence.** State the fact, its scope and the mechanism, and say where it is\n  evidenced.\n- **Answer the question asked**, not a neighbouring one.\n- **Honest gaps.** When the answer is \"No\" or \"Partially\", say so and add the compensating control or a\n  dated roadmap item; reviewers deal with gaps constantly, but discovered misstatements destroy trust.\n- **\"Not applicable\" only with a one-line reason.**\n\n### The AI section\n- **Expect questions on:** training use of customer data (yours and every model provider's), prompt and\n  output retention by each subprocessor, region of processing, model provenance and evaluation, human\n  oversight, guardrails against prompt injection and data leakage, tool permissions for agents, and how\n  customers can delete their data including embeddings and caches.\n- **Answer per data flow**, because the honest answer often differs between the hosted-API path and a\n  self-hosted model path.","example":"### Worked scenario: three answers in a 300-question health-tech review\n| Question | Weak answer | Strong answer | Why |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| \"Is customer data used to train AI models?\" | \"No.\" | \"No. Customer prompts, documents and outputs are not used to train or fine-tune our models. Our LLM subprocessor processes data under API terms that exclude training use; its retention terms and region are listed on our subprocessor page. Evidence: DPA section 4, subprocessor list.\" | covers the subprocessor, which is what the buyer really wants to know |\n| \"Is all data encrypted at rest with customer-managed keys?\" | \"Yes.\" | \"Partially\": data at rest is encrypted with provider-managed keys; customer-managed keys exist only on the dedicated-deployment tier. Explain both tiers and point to the dedicated option. | \"Yes\" is true for one tier and false for the one this customer bought |\n| \"Do you retain prompts and responses?\" | \"No.\" | \"Yes, for 30 days for debugging, access-restricted, deletable on request.\" | engineers found traces with full prompts kept for 30 days in the observability tool, which nobody had listed |\n\n- **The third answer surfaced a real gap** before an audit did; the observability vendor was added to\n  the subprocessor list.\n- **Overall**, the SOC 2 report removed about a third of the questions as covered (an illustrative\n  proportion), and the remaining answers were drafted from the library and approved by owners within the\n  week.\n\n### Answer library: match a new question and flag stale entries\nA minimal sketch of the retrieval and freshness checks. Real libraries use embeddings or a\nquestionnaire platform; the routing to an owner and the review-date check are the parts that matter.\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import date\n\nLIBRARY = [\n    {\"id\": \"AI-01\", \"q\": \"is customer data used to train ai models\",\n     \"owner\": \"ml\", \"evidence\": \"DPA s4, subprocessor list\", \"reviewed\": date(2026, 6, 1)},\n    {\"id\": \"ENC-03\", \"q\": \"is data encrypted at rest with customer managed keys\",\n     \"owner\": \"infra\", \"evidence\": \"SOC 2 report, tier matrix\", \"reviewed\": date(2025, 9, 15)},\n    {\"id\": \"RET-02\", \"q\": \"do you retain prompts and responses\",\n     \"owner\": \"security\", \"evidence\": \"retention policy, trace config\", \"reviewed\": date(2026, 8, 20)},\n]\n\ndef words(text):\n    return set(text.lower().replace(\"?\", \"\").replace(\"-\", \" \").split())\n\ndef best_match(question, library=LIBRARY):\n    q = words(question)\n    score = lambda e: len(q & words(e[\"q\"])) / len(q | words(e[\"q\"]))\n    entry = max(library, key=score)\n    return entry, round(score(entry), 2)\n\ndef stale(library=LIBRARY, today=date(2026, 10, 20), max_age_days=180):\n    return [e[\"id\"] for e in library if (today - e[\"reviewed\"]).days > max_age_days]\n\nentry, s = best_match(\"Are customer prompts used to train or fine-tune models?\")\nprint(entry[\"id\"], s, \"-> route to owner:\", entry[\"owner\"])\nprint(\"needs review before reuse:\", stale())\n# AI-01 0.38 -> route to owner: ml\n# needs review before reuse: ['ENC-03']\n```","patterns":["**Answer library with owners and review dates.** Store each answer with its control, owner, evidence link and last-reviewed date; block reuse of entries past their review window (for example six months) until the owner re-approves. Track the share of stale entries.","**Architecture-change trigger.** Add a pull-request template question such as \"does this change a data flow, region, retention or subprocessor?\" that pings the library owner. Monitor answers changed after engineering review; a high count means the library lags the system.","**Trust center plus NDA-gated reports.** Publish the subprocessor list, data-flow summary and AI data-use statement; share the SOC 2 report and full pen-test results only under NDA. Measure questions answered by reference to it.","**Subprocessor list reconciled with egress.** Periodically compare the published list with the vendors seen in DNS and egress logs and billing, because an unlisted observability or model vendor is the classic miss. Alert on any new external domain in production egress.","**Per-flow AI answers.** Keep separate entries for the hosted-API path and the self-hosted path for training use, retention and region, and quote the one matching the customer's tier.","**Measure the process.** Turnaround time, follow-up rounds per questionnaire and findings per review, tracked per buyer tier."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    T[\"Buyer: inherent-risk tiering\"] --> Q[\"Questionnaire arrives\"]\n    Q --> E[\"Send SOC 2 or ISO evidence early\"]\n    E --> M[\"Match questions to answer library\"]\n    L[(\"Answer library: owner, evidence, review date\")] --> M\n    M --> D[\"Draft answer, possibly RAG\"]\n    D --> O{\"Control owner approves?\"}\n    O -->|\"no, fix or mark gap\"| D\n    O -->|\"yes\"| S[\"Submit to buyer\"]\n    S --> F[\"Buyer scores, raises findings\"]\n    F -->|\"follow-ups\"| M\n    F --> C[\"Disposition: accept, mitigate, contract\"]\n    S -.->|\"new or changed answers\"| L","caption":"A questionnaire flows from the buyer's tiering through your answer library, where every draft passes\nan accountable owner before it becomes a commitment."},"interview":[{"q":"Why do enterprises send security questionnaires, and why do the answers matter so much?","a":"- **Scale:** a buyer with thousands of vendors cannot inspect each one, so it collects standardized\n  written claims and evidence, tiered by how much risk the vendor carries.\n- **Consequence:** answers are scored into findings and are often incorporated by reference into the\n  contract, so a false answer becomes a legal problem, not just a technical one."},{"q":"A questionnaire asks whether you retain prompts. You are not sure. What do you do?","a":"- **Do not guess.** Route it to the owner of the data flow and check every place prompts can land:\n  application logs, traces, error trackers, caches, and each model provider's retention terms.\n- **Answer what you find**, with duration, access controls and deletion path, for example \"Yes, 30 days\n  for debugging, access-restricted, deletable on request\".\n- **Fix the record:** add any newly found vendor to the subprocessor list and update the library entry."},{"q":"When is pursuing SOC 2 not yet worth it?","a":"- **Low deal volume:** an early-stage vendor with few enterprise deals may get further with a strong\n  answer library, a clear data-flow diagram and a pen-test summary.\n- **Cost:** a Type II audit costs significant time and money every year and covers a period, so it pays\n  off once questionnaire volume and deal size justify it.\n- **Signal to watch:** repeated deals stalling on \"do you have a SOC 2 report?\""},{"q":"How would you use an LLM to speed up questionnaire responses safely?","a":"- **RAG over the approved library**, not over raw documents, so drafts reuse wording an owner already\n  signed off.\n- **Owner approval on every answer**, because a fluent wrong answer is a misrepresentation; track which\n  answers were edited before sending.\n- **Freshness gate:** refuse to draft from entries past their review date."},{"q":"What does a strong answer to an AI training-use question contain?","a":"- **The direct answer** for your own models, then the same for every model subprocessor and their\n  retention and region.\n- **The mechanism and evidence:** the API terms that exclude training, the DPA section, the subprocessor list.\n- **Per data flow:** separate statements for the hosted-API path and a self-hosted path, because the\n  honest answers differ."}],"tradeoffs":["**Templates vs custom forms.** Industry templates such as SIG or CAIQ let you answer once and reuse widely, but buyers with their own frameworks still send custom forms, so the library must map to both.","**Independent reports vs cost.** Reports like SOC 2 shortcut many questions and carry weight, but cost significant time and money annually. For an early-stage vendor with few enterprise deals, a strong library may suffice first.","**Specific answers vs commitments.** Detailed answers build trust and reduce follow-ups, but each detail is a commitment. Where the system changes often, describe the control and its guarantee rather than its exact implementation.","**AI drafting vs accuracy.** Drafts from the library save hours per questionnaire, but only work when every generated answer is reviewed by the owner of the underlying control."],"pitfalls":["**Answering aspirationally to unblock a deal.** Symptom: a later audit or incident shows the contract-referenced answer is false, turning a technical gap into a legal one.","**Forgetting subprocessors in the data-flow story**, especially model providers and observability tools. Symptom: a buyer finds an unlisted vendor in your trace exports or DNS logs.","**Letting answers diverge across customers.** Symptom: two customers compare notes, or the same question is answered differently in a renewal, prompting a full re-review.","**Never revisiting answers after architecture changes.** Symptom: stale claims, for example \"data never leaves the EU\" after someone added a US-hosted model fallback route.","**Attaching sensitive evidence without controls.** A full penetration-test report with unfixed findings or a detailed network diagram, on a portal outside an NDA. Symptom: your internal security detail circulates among people you have never met. Share summaries and attestation letters by default, full reports under NDA on request.","**Treating \"not applicable\" as a shortcut.** Symptom: reviewer follow-ups asking why, which slow the review more than a one-line justified answer would have."],"when":"- **Prepare before the first deal.** Every sale to a regulated or large enterprise passes through one:\n  write the answer library, the subprocessor list, a data-flow diagram and the AI data-use statement\n  while the architecture is fresh.\n- **Pursue SOC 2 or ISO 27001** when the volume of questionnaires and deal size justify the audit cost.\n- **Route through control owners**, not the account team alone.\n- **Measure:** turnaround time, follow-up rounds and the number of answers changed after engineering review.\n- **Interviews (FDE and architect):** expect \"how do you answer a question where the honest answer is\n  no?\" The answer: honestly, with compensating controls and a dated plan.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Owns accurate answers about the systems they build, such as retention in traces, embedding deletion and model routing, and flags when a change invalidates a published answer.","architect":"Designs data flows so the answers are good ones, for example a no-retention model path or a region-pinned tier, and keeps the authoritative data-flow diagram the answers rest on.","fde":"Shepherds the customer's questionnaire through internal owners, explains gaps and compensating controls on calls, and feeds recurring asks back to product as roadmap items."},"check":{"q":"A prospect's questionnaire asks \"Does any customer data leave the EU?\" Your app runs in an EU\nregion, but its fallback model route goes to a US-hosted provider when the EU model is rate-limited, and\nyour error tracker is US-hosted. How do you answer, and what do you do beyond answering?","a":"The honest answer is \"Yes, in specific cases,\" followed by each flow: the fallback route may send\nprompts to a US region when the EU model is throttled, and error events may contain request fragments sent\nto a US-hosted tracker. Name the subprocessors, the transfer mechanism in the DPA, and any mitigations such\nas scrubbing payloads from error events. Beyond the answer, offer a configuration that keeps data in the EU:\ndisable cross-region fallback for this tenant, accepting more throttling or degraded responses, and switch\nthe tracker to an EU instance or strip content. Then update the answer library and subprocessor list so the\nnext questionnaire is correct, and tell engineering the routing change has compliance consequences."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does sending a SOC 2 Type II report early often shorten a security questionnaire?","options":["It certifies the product is free of vulnerabilities","It is an independent auditor's opinion that controls operated over a period, which buyers accept in place of many individual answers","It replaces the need for a data processing agreement","It proves compliance with HIPAA and GDPR"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A Type II report gives third-party assurance on control operation over time, so buyers can mark whole domains as covered. It is not a vulnerability guarantee, a legal agreement or proof of any regulation."},{"q":"The honest answer to \"Do you support customer-managed encryption keys?\" is \"only on our dedicated tier,\" and this buyer is on the shared tier. What is the best response?","options":["Answer \"Yes\", since the product supports the feature","Answer \"Not applicable\", since this buyer did not purchase that tier","Answer \"Partially\", explain which tier offers it and what protects data on the shared tier","Leave the question blank and raise it on a call later"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A scoped, accurate answer with the alternative is both honest and useful. \"Yes\" would misstate this buyer's protection, and \"not applicable\" is wrong because the question applies to them."},{"q":"Your team uses an LLM over the answer library to draft questionnaire responses. What control matters most?","options":["Using the largest available model to reduce errors","Letting the model answer only yes/no questions","Re-running the model until two drafts agree","Requiring the owner of each underlying control to approve the drafted answer before it is sent"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Retrieval speeds drafting, but answers are commitments, and a fluent wrong answer is a misrepresentation. Model size and agreement between drafts do not establish truth; an accountable owner's review does."}]},{"key":"deployment/security-and-architecture-reviews","sources":["Adam Shostack, 2014 — Threat Modeling: Designing for Security","Official docs: OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications","Official docs: NIST AI Risk Management Framework"],"why":"- **A questionnaire captures claims; a review tests the design.** It cannot tell the buyer how your\n  system fits theirs: which networks it touches, what identity reads their SharePoint, whether an agent\n  could email a customer, where a prompt containing patient data is logged.\n- **Those answers depend on the deployment**, so large organizations route new systems through an\n  architecture review board and a security review before production access is granted. Good engineering\n  teams run the same review on their own designs before building.\n- **Without a review, problems surface in the worst places:** an over-privileged service account noticed\n  during an incident, a data path to an unapproved region found by an auditor, an agent's tool that can\n  modify records discovered by a user. Fixing design after deployment costs far more than changing a diagram.\n- **Marketing slides fail.** Reviewers want specifics: every data store, every boundary crossing, every\n  credential. Vague answers produce a long list of open actions and a second meeting weeks later.\n- **AI makes it harder.** Reviewers often meet LLM-specific risks for the first time and need you to\n  explain how the model can be manipulated and what bounds the damage. The vendor who brings that\n  analysis unprompted earns trust a slide deck cannot.","how":"### The typical flow\n1. **Intake.** The sponsor files a request with a system description, data classification and owners.\n   Data classification sets the depth: a system touching regulated personal data gets more scrutiny than\n   an internal FAQ bot.\n2. **Pre-read package.** You submit documents in advance, because reviewers want to come with questions,\n   not discover the design in the meeting.\n3. **Review session.** Reviewers walk the diagram, probe boundaries and ask \"what happens if\" questions.\n4. **Findings.** Each gets a severity, an owner and an outcome: fix before go-live, fix by a date, accept\n   the risk with a named approver, or mitigate with a compensating control.\n5. **Decision.** Approve, approve with conditions, or reject; conditions are tracked to closure, usually\n   in the customer's risk register.\n\n### What the pre-read contains\n- **Architecture diagram** at container level (C4-style is common): every service, data store, external\n  dependency and trust boundary, including model providers and observability tools.\n- **Data flow diagram** for each sensitive flow: what data, from where, to where, encrypted how, retained\n  how long. Reviewers scrutinize this most, because breaches follow data.\n- **Identity and access:** how users authenticate (their SSO), how services authenticate to each other\n  and to customer systems, which permissions each role and service account has, and why.\n- **Network:** ingress and egress paths, private endpoints, VPNs, what is publicly reachable.\n- **Encryption and keys:** in transit, at rest, who holds keys, whether customer-managed keys are used.\n- **Logging and monitoring:** what is logged, where, for how long, whether prompts or outputs are included.\n- **Threat model:** for example STRIDE per boundary, plus AI-specific threats from the OWASP LLM list:\n  prompt injection (direct and via retrieved documents), sensitive information disclosure, excessive\n  agency of tools, improper output handling (called insecure output handling in the 2023 list), vector\n  and embedding weaknesses, and supply chain of models and datasets.\n- **Evidence:** penetration test summary, vulnerability scanning, SBOM, SOC 2 or ISO reports.\n\n### Applying STRIDE at a boundary\nA threat model is not a list of scary words; it is the same six questions asked wherever data or control\ncrosses a trust boundary. It works *because* boundaries are finite and enumerable, while \"think of\nattacks\" is not.\n\n| Letter | Question | At \"retrieved document text enters the model prompt\" |\n|---|---|---|\n| **S**poofing | can someone pretend to be a legitimate caller? | (ask per caller of the retrieval API) |\n| **T**ampering | can data be altered in transit or at rest? | a planted instruction in a document (indirect prompt injection) |\n| **R**epudiation | could an actor deny an action because nothing recorded it? | an action with no log tying it to a user and a source document |\n| **I**nformation disclosure | can data reach someone not entitled to it? | the model quoting a passage the user may not see |\n| **D**enial of service | can the path be exhausted? | (ask per path: index size, prompt length) |\n| **E**levation of privilege | can a caller gain rights it was not given? | the injected instruction causing a tool call with the agent's broader credentials |\n\nEach answer points to a control, which is exactly what the reviewer wants to see written down.\n\n### The architecture half\nAn architecture review board asks questions a security reviewer may not:\n- **Fit:** does the design use approved technologies and integration patterns, or introduce a new\n  database the customer's operations team has never run?\n- **Resilience:** what are the availability target and the recovery point and time objectives, and does\n  the design meet them?\n- **Operations:** who operates it, and does its monitoring land in their tooling?\n- **Cost:** what does it cost at expected volume, and how does that scale?\n- **Exit:** can data and indexes be exported if the vendor is replaced?\n\nThese exist *because* the customer will live with the system long after the project team moves on, so fit\nwith their estate matters as much as internal elegance.\n\n### AI-specific questions to prepare for\n| Reviewer asks | Prepared control |\n|---|---|\n| Can a document in the index instruct the model to leak other documents? | permission-filtered retrieval, output validation |\n| Does retrieval respect the user's permissions in the source system? | permission-filtered retrieval |\n| Which tools can the agent call, with whose credentials, and which actions need human approval? | least-privilege tool credentials, approval gates |\n| What happens when the model is wrong, and how would you know? | evaluation results and monitoring |\n| Can a user's data be deleted from indexes, caches and fine-tuned weights? | documented deletion path per store |\n\n### Your own reviews\n- **Run the same process internally first.** A design review with a peer architect and a security\n  engineer catches the obvious findings cheaply.\n- **Record decisions as ADRs** so the reasoning survives staff changes.","example":"### Worked scenario: a bank's review of a policy assistant\nAn FDE prepares a bank's architecture review for an internal-policy assistant that retrieves from\nSharePoint and can draft, but not send, emails. The pre-read has a container diagram, two data-flow\ndiagrams (indexing and query), an access matrix and a threat model. The review produces five findings:\n\n1. **High:** the indexing service account can read all SharePoint sites, so retrieval would ignore\n   per-user permissions.\n   - Fix before go-live: each chunk carries the source document's access list captured at index time;\n     retrieval filters by the user's group memberships synced from the identity provider; the indexer's\n     scope is reduced to the approved site collections.\n   - Residual risk, noted in the finding, is staleness: a permission revoked in SharePoint still applies\n     in the index until the next sync, so the sync interval becomes a documented, agreed number.\n2. **High:** full prompts and retrieved passages are written to the vendor-hosted trace store. Fix before\n   go-live: traces stay in the bank's log platform; vendor telemetry receives only metadata.\n3. **Medium:** indirect prompt injection through a malicious document could make the assistant produce a\n   misleading draft. Mitigation accepted: drafts are never auto-sent, the UI marks generated text, and\n   red-team results on injection are attached as evidence.\n4. **Medium:** no documented response if the model provider changes the model version. Fix by date: pin\n   the model version and add a regression eval gate before upgrades.\n5. **Low:** the internal ingress still accepts TLS 1.2 with legacy CBC-mode cipher suites, while the\n   bank's standard requires AEAD-only suites. Risk accepted by the bank's security lead with a review in\n   six months.\n\n| Finding | Severity | Outcome | Closed by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 Indexer reads all sites | High | fix before go-live | two weeks, with a permission-filter test |\n| 2 Prompts in vendor traces | High | fix before go-live | two weeks, with a log-flow screenshot |\n| 3 Indirect injection in drafts | Medium | mitigation accepted | red-team evidence |\n| 4 Unpinned model version | Medium | fix by date | condition on approval |\n| 5 TLS 1.2 CBC suites | Low | risk accepted | review in six months, condition on approval |\n\nThe board approves with conditions on findings 4 and 5, tracked in the bank's risk register.\n\n### Evidence test for finding 1: user A cannot retrieve user B's restricted file\nThe reviewer wants a test, not a promise. This pure-Python sketch shows the shape: filter by the\ncaller's groups before ranking, then assert on the restricted document.\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Evidence for finding 1: retrieval honours source-system permissions.\"\"\"\nCHUNKS = [\n    {\"doc\": \"travel-policy.docx\", \"text\": \"Economy class for flights under 6 hours\", \"acl\": {\"all-staff\"}},\n    {\"doc\": \"layoffs-2026.docx\", \"text\": \"Restructuring plan for the retail unit\", \"acl\": {\"hr-leads\"}},\n]\nGROUPS = {\"user_a\": {\"all-staff\"}, \"user_b\": {\"all-staff\", \"hr-leads\"}}   # synced from the IdP\n\ndef retrieve(user, query, chunks=CHUNKS):\n    allowed = GROUPS.get(user, set())\n    visible = [c for c in chunks if c[\"acl\"] & allowed]                 # filter BEFORE ranking\n    return [c[\"doc\"] for c in visible if any(w in c[\"text\"].lower() for w in query.lower().split())]\n\ndef test_user_a_cannot_retrieve_restricted_file():\n    assert \"layoffs-2026.docx\" not in retrieve(\"user_a\", \"restructuring plan\")\n\ndef test_user_b_can_retrieve_it():\n    assert retrieve(\"user_b\", \"restructuring plan\") == [\"layoffs-2026.docx\"]\n\ndef test_unknown_user_gets_nothing():\n    assert retrieve(\"mallory\", \"policy plan\") == []\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    for t in (test_user_a_cannot_retrieve_restricted_file, test_user_b_can_retrieve_it,\n              test_unknown_user_gets_nothing):\n        t(); print(\"pass\", t.__name__)\n```\n\n- **Against the real system**, run the same three assertions through the deployed retrieval API with two\n  test identities, and attach the run output to the finding.","patterns":["**Reusable pre-read template.** Keep a standard package (container diagram, one DFD per sensitive flow, access matrix, STRIDE table per boundary, AI threats from the OWASP LLM list, evidence index) and update it per deployment. Track high findings per review; they should fall as the template matures.","**Diagram generated from or checked against production.** Reconcile the architecture diagram with the deployed inventory (cloud resource listings, egress destinations) before each review, because a missing service destroys reviewer confidence.","**Findings as tickets with dates.** Every finding gets an owner, severity, outcome and due date in the tracker, mirrored to the customer's risk register. Monitor the percentage closed by due date.","**Expiring risk acceptances.** Each accepted risk records the approver and a review date, such as the six-month TLS review above, and resurfaces automatically. Count acceptances past their date.","**Change triggers for re-review.** New data sources, new agent tools, new model providers and new regions require a delta review before release; add the check to the release checklist.","**Evidence tests in CI.** Keep the permission-filter and log-destination checks as automated tests, so the evidence stays true after go-live."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    I[\"Intake: description, data class, owners\"] --> P[\"Pre-read: diagrams, DFDs, threat model, evidence\"]\n    P --> S[\"Review session: walk boundaries\"]\n    S --> F[\"Findings with severity and owner\"]\n    F --> O{\"Outcome per finding\"}\n    O -->|\"fix before go-live\"| X[\"Engineering fix plus evidence\"]\n    O -->|\"fix by date\"| R[(\"Customer risk register\")]\n    O -->|\"accept or mitigate\"| R\n    X --> D{\"Board decision\"}\n    R --> D\n    D -->|\"reject\"| P\n    D -->|\"approve with conditions\"| G[\"Go-live, conditions tracked\"]","caption":"The review is a loop: findings return to the design until each one has a fix, a mitigation or a named\nacceptance."},"interview":[{"q":"How is a security review different from a security questionnaire?","a":"- **Questionnaire:** standardized written claims about your controls, largely independent of how you are\n  deployed.\n- **Review:** an examination of this specific deployment's design: which networks it touches, which\n  identities it uses, where sensitive data flows and is logged, and what an agent can do.\n- **So prepare differently:** a library answers questionnaires; a review needs accurate diagrams, data\n  flows, a threat model and evidence for this system."},{"q":"Walk me through threat-modelling one boundary in a RAG agent.","a":"- **Pick the boundary:** \"retrieved document text enters the model prompt\".\n- **Ask STRIDE:** tampering is a planted instruction; information disclosure is quoting a passage the user\n  may not see; elevation is an injected tool call with the agent's credentials; repudiation is an\n  unlogged action.\n- **Map each to a control:** permission-filtered retrieval, least-privilege tools, approval gates, and\n  logs tying actions to user and source document."},{"q":"A reviewer raises a finding you think is overstated. How do you handle it?","a":"- **Do not argue it away.** Understand the risk they see and restate it in your own words.\n- **Offer options:** a compensating control, a dated fix, or a formal acceptance by their named approver\n  with a review date.\n- **Bring evidence** (a test, a config excerpt) rather than assertions; reviewers escalate when they\n  feel resisted."},{"q":"When is a full architecture review overkill?","a":"- **Low-risk internal tools** with no sensitive data, no integrations with identity or internal systems,\n  and no actions: a short template review is proportionate.\n- **Small changes inside an approved design** need a delta review, not the whole process.\n- **Not overkill** when an agent gains a new tool, a new model provider or a new region, because those\n  change the data flows the original approval rested on."},{"q":"Which numbers would you track to show your review process is working?","a":"- **Time from intake to approval**, per data classification.\n- **High findings per review**, which should fall as the pre-read template matures.\n- **Percentage of findings closed by due date**, plus the count of risk acceptances past their review date."}],"tradeoffs":["**Early vs late engagement.** Engaging reviewers at design stage finds structural problems while they are cheap, but the design may change and need re-review. Late engagement saves meetings but risks findings that force a redesign.","**Thorough pre-read vs preparation time.** It shortens the meeting and the follow-up cycle, but takes days to prepare. For a low-risk internal tool, a lightweight review with a short template is proportionate.","**Risk acceptance vs invisible debt.** Accepting risks with a named approver keeps launches moving, but accumulated accepted risks become invisible debt unless they carry expiry dates and are re-reviewed.","**Frameworks vs deployment specifics.** STRIDE and the OWASP LLM list give reviewers shared vocabulary, but checklists miss deployment-specific risks; the data-flow walkthrough is what finds those."],"pitfalls":["**A diagram that does not match production.** Symptom: a reviewer asks about a service the diagram omits, and the review loses confidence in everything else presented.","**Omitting the model provider and observability vendors from data flows.** Symptom: a high-severity finding for undisclosed data transfer, often discovered from a network-egress question.","**Agreeing to findings without owners and dates.** Symptom: the same findings reappear at the next review, or block go-live at the last minute.","**Treating the review as a one-time gate.** Symptom: a later feature, such as a new agent tool or a cross-region fallback, ships without review and is flagged by an audit.","**Arguing every finding.** Symptom: a reviewer escalates to a reject, when a compensating control or a dated fix would have closed it."],"when":"- **Expect a customer review** for any system touching confidential or regulated data, integrating with\n  identity or internal systems, or taking actions.\n- **Run your own internal review** for every new system and for significant changes: new data sources,\n  new tools for an agent, new model providers, new regions.\n- **Scale the depth** to data classification and blast radius.\n- **Measure:** time from intake to approval, the number of high findings per review (it should fall as\n  your template matures) and the percentage of findings closed by their due date.\n- **Interviews:** a strong candidate describes the pre-read package and names AI-specific threats with\n  concrete controls, rather than claiming the system is secure.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Produces the evidence reviewers ask for, such as a test proving permission-filtered retrieval and log configuration excerpts, and implements fixes against named findings.","architect":"Owns the diagrams, data flows, threat model and ADRs, presents the design, and negotiates findings into fixes, mitigations or formally accepted risks with expiry dates.","fde":"Navigates the customer's review process, books the right reviewers, translates their concerns into engineering tickets and keeps the findings tracker moving until approval."},"check":{"q":"In a customer's review, the security architect asks: \"If someone uploads a document containing\ninstructions to the model, what stops your agent from emailing our customer list to an outside address?\"\nWhat is a strong answer?","a":"Acknowledge that indirect prompt injection cannot be fully prevented at the model level, then show\nlayered controls that bound impact. The agent's email tool can only create drafts, so a human must send;\nrecipients are restricted to internal domains by the tool, not the prompt; the agent's credentials cannot\nread the customer list because retrieval is filtered by the requesting user's permissions and the CRM is not\nconnected; outputs are validated and links stripped; and actions are logged for audit. Add evidence: red-team\nresults for injection attempts and the tool's permission policy. Then offer monitoring and an alert on\nanomalous tool use. The key point is that safety rests on capability limits, not on the model obeying."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which pre-read document do reviewers usually scrutinize most closely, and why?","options":["The product roadmap, because it shows future security features","The data-flow diagrams, because they show what sensitive data crosses which boundaries and where it is stored","The penetration test report, because it proves there are no vulnerabilities","The pricing sheet, because it determines the review tier"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Breaches and compliance problems follow data, so reviewers trace each sensitive flow across trust boundaries. A penetration test is useful evidence but shows only what testers found, not the absence of flaws."},{"q":"A review finding says the indexing service account can read every SharePoint site. Why is this rated high for a RAG assistant?","options":["Because SharePoint APIs are rate-limited","Because broad read access slows down indexing","Because the account password might expire","Because without per-user permission filtering at retrieval, any user could receive content they are not entitled to read"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The assistant would answer from documents the requesting user cannot access, bypassing the source system's permissions. The fix is permission-aware retrieval and a narrower indexing scope, not tuning throughput."},{"q":"A finding cannot be fixed before launch. What is the most appropriate way to proceed?","options":["Remove it from the findings list since it cannot be fixed now","Launch and revisit it if an incident occurs","Record a risk acceptance or compensating control with a named approver and a review or fix date","Ask the reviewer to downgrade the severity so it does not block"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Formal acceptance makes the decision visible and owned, and a date ensures it is revisited. Deleting or downgrading the finding hides the risk, which is what the review exists to prevent."}]},{"key":"deployment/compliance-constraints","sources":["Official docs: EU General Data Protection Regulation (Regulation (EU) 2016/679)","Official docs: US HHS — HIPAA Privacy and Security Rules","Official docs: EU Artificial Intelligence Act (Regulation (EU) 2024/1689)","Official docs: AICPA — SOC 2"],"why":"- **Finished is not deployable.** A hospital cannot send patient notes to a model provider that has not\n  signed a business associate agreement. A European insurer may need contractual safeguards before\n  prompts with customer data leave the EU. A defense contractor's data may be export-controlled, so only\n  certain personnel may touch the system.\n- **These are obligations, not preferences.** Breach means fines, lost certifications, voided contracts\n  or worse.\n- **\"Do compliance at the end\" fails** because the requirements are architectural (see the table below).\n  Discovering them after the build means rework, sometimes a different deployment model entirely, such\n  as moving from SaaS into the customer's VPC.\n- **The useful skill is translation:** hearing \"we are subject to HIPAA and SOC 2\" and turning it into a\n  short list of concrete constraints the design must satisfy and the evidence it must produce.\n\n| Requirement | What it decides in the architecture |\n|---|---|\n| Data residency | which regions and model endpoints you may use |\n| No-training, limited retention | which model providers qualify |\n| Erasure | whether you may fine-tune on customer data, since deleting a record from model weights is not practical |\n| Audit | what your logs must contain |\n| Minimization | what your logs may contain |","how":"### From obligation to evidence\nEvery requirement flows through the same chain:\n\n```\nobligation (law, contract clause, framework criterion)\n  → constraint on the system\n  → control that enforces it\n  → evidence that the control works\n```\n\nAuditors and reviewers check the last link, so design controls that produce evidence naturally:\nconfiguration in code, logs, test results.\n\n### The regimes you will meet most\n**GDPR (EU personal data).**\n- **Core duties:** a lawful basis, purpose limitation and data minimization, data processing agreements\n  with processors, honoring rights such as erasure and access, and safeguards for transfers outside the\n  EEA (adequacy decisions or mechanisms such as standard contractual clauses).\n- **Residency is usually contractual.** GDPR does not strictly mandate EU hosting, but many customers\n  impose EU residency contractually because it is the simplest way to stay safe.\n- **Controller and processor.** The customer is usually the **controller** and you the **processor**, so\n  you act only on their documented instructions, and your subprocessors (model, cloud, tracing) need\n  their approval.\n- **Remote access counts.** European regulators generally treat *remote access* from outside the EEA,\n  such as a US support engineer opening an EU tenant's traces, as a transfer. Residency is about who can\n  read the data, not only where disks sit.\n\n**HIPAA (US health data).**\n- **BAA required.** Covered entities may share protected health information with a vendor only under a\n  business associate agreement, and that obligation flows down to the vendor's subprocessors, including\n  model and cloud providers.\n- **Security Rule safeguards** cover access control, audit controls and transmission security.\n\n**Other regimes.**\n\n| Regime | What it is | Typical constraint |\n|---|---|---|\n| SOC 2, ISO/IEC 27001 | assurance frameworks customers require of vendors, not laws | change management, access reviews, logging, incident response, vendor management |\n| PCI DSS | payment cards; any system that stores, processes or transmits cardholder data is in scope | keep card data out of prompts and logs so the AI system stays out of scope |\n| FedRAMP | US federal cloud use requires FedRAMP-authorized services at an appropriate impact level | approved services only |\n| Export control (for example ITAR) | restricts who may access the data | often pushes deployments on-prem or air-gapped |\n| EU AI Act | risk-based regulation phasing in obligations over several years | see below |\n| Sector rules | financial services model risk management (validation, monitoring, documentation); record-keeping rules | minimum retention, model documentation |\n\n**EU AI Act.** Certain practices are prohibited; high-risk systems carry requirements for risk\nmanagement, data governance, logging, human oversight and transparency; general-purpose model providers\nhave their own duties. Which tier a use case falls into is a legal determination, so involve counsel.\n\n### The constraint categories\nTranslate any regime into these questions:\n\n| Category | Question |\n|---|---|\n| **Where** | where may data be processed and stored? |\n| **Who** | who may access it, including vendors and personnel? |\n| **What** | what data may enter prompts, logs and indexes? |\n| **How long** | how long must or may it be kept? |\n| **How** | how must it be protected (encryption, customer-managed keys)? |\n| **What proof** | which audit trails and change records must exist? |\n| **AI-specific** | no training use, human oversight, disclosure that users are talking to AI |\n\n### Enforce at a chokepoint, check in the pipeline\nA constraint written only in a policy document is broken by the first engineer who adds a fallback\nregion. So enforce each constraint where every request must pass, and check it where every change must pass:\n1. **Runtime chokepoint.** Route all model calls through one gateway holding a per-tenant policy (allowed\n   providers, allowed regions, whether content may be logged) that refuses anything else. It is a\n   chokepoint *because* a single enforcement point cannot be bypassed by a new code path the way\n   scattered checks can, and its configuration plus its refusal logs are the evidence.\n2. **Change-time check.** Run policy checks on infrastructure plans (for example, fail any Terraform plan\n   that creates storage outside approved regions or a log sink without a retention setting), and require\n   review when a new subprocessor appears in the dependency or egress list. This catches the violation\n   before production, which is when it is cheapest.\n3. **Periodic proof.** Access reviews; deletion tests that confirm an erased record is gone from the\n   database, the vector index and the caches; and a reconciliation of actual egress against the declared\n   subprocessor list.\n\n### Constraints conflict\n| Conflict | Explicit resolution |\n|---|---|\n| Audit retention vs erasure | keep the audit record of actions; keep content where it can be deleted |\n| Debug logging vs minimization | log metadata and hashes rather than content |\n| Cross-region fallback vs residency | in-region fallback or a degraded mode |\n| Fine-tuning on customer data vs deletion | keep customer data in retrieval indexes (which can be deleted) instead of in fine-tuned weights |","example":"### Worked scenario: a US hospital's note-summarizing assistant\nCustomer requirements: HIPAA, the vendor's SOC 2 Type II, and their own policy of seven-year audit\nretention. The translation into constraints and controls:\n\n1. **BAA chain.** Notes are PHI, so the vendor signs a BAA with the hospital, and every subprocessor that\n   sees PHI must be covered too: the cloud provider and the model provider. The model provider's offering\n   is checked for BAA eligibility; a provider without one is excluded, whatever its quality.\n2. **Retention and training.** The model API path must not retain prompts beyond what the BAA allows and\n   must not train on them. Where that cannot be confirmed, the design switches to a self-hosted\n   open-weight model in the hospital's VPC.\n3. **Logs.** Debug traces originally captured full prompts. Under minimum-necessary handling, traces now\n   keep request IDs, token counts, latency and a hash of the input; full content is stored only in the\n   hospital's own audit store, with access logged.\n4. **Audit.** Seven-year retention applies to the record of who generated which summary and when, not to\n   the vendor's debug traces, which are kept for 30 days. Separating the two resolves the conflict\n   between audit and minimization.\n5. **Human oversight.** Summaries are drafts a clinician must approve before they enter the record; the\n   approval event is part of the audit trail.\n6. **Evidence pack.** BAA list, data-flow diagram, retention configuration in IaC, access review records,\n   and the SOC 2 report, ready for the hospital's security review.\n\n| Store | Contents | Retention | Owner |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Hospital audit store | who generated which summary, when, approval event, full content | seven years | hospital |\n| Vendor debug traces | request ID, token counts, latency, input hash | 30 days | vendor |\n\nThe result changed two design decisions (model provider selection and log content) before any code shipped.\n\n### A per-tenant policy at the model gateway\nThe runtime chokepoint in miniature: deny by default, allow only listed provider and region pairs, and\nlog a hash instead of content unless the tenant's policy allows content.\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Per-tenant compliance policy enforced at the model gateway (the chokepoint).\"\"\"\nimport hashlib, json, time, uuid\n\nPOLICIES = {\n    \"eu-insurer\": {\"providers\": {\"provider-a\"}, \"regions\": {\"eu-west-1\", \"eu-central-1\"},\n                   \"log_content\": False},\n    \"us-hospital\": {\"providers\": {\"provider-b\"}, \"regions\": {\"us-east-1\"},      # BAA-covered only\n                    \"log_content\": False},\n}\n\nclass PolicyViolation(Exception):\n    pass\n\ndef route(tenant, provider, region, prompt):\n    policy = POLICIES.get(tenant)\n    if policy is None:\n        raise PolicyViolation(f\"no policy for tenant {tenant}: deny by default\")\n    if provider not in policy[\"providers\"] or region not in policy[\"regions\"]:\n        raise PolicyViolation(f\"{tenant}: {provider}/{region} not allowed\")\n    trace = {\"request_id\": str(uuid.uuid4()), \"tenant\": tenant, \"provider\": provider,\n             \"region\": region, \"ts\": int(time.time()),\n             \"input_sha256\": hashlib.sha256(prompt.encode()).hexdigest()[:16]}\n    if policy[\"log_content\"]:\n        trace[\"prompt\"] = prompt\n    return trace          # the call to the provider would happen here\n\nprint(json.dumps(route(\"eu-insurer\", \"provider-a\", \"eu-west-1\", \"Claim 4411 for J. Doe\"))[:120])\nfor args in ((\"eu-insurer\", \"provider-a\", \"us-east-1\"), (\"unknown\", \"provider-a\", \"eu-west-1\")):\n    try:\n        route(*args, prompt=\"x\")\n    except PolicyViolation as e:\n        print(\"REFUSED:\", e)          # refusal logs are compliance evidence\n# REFUSED: eu-insurer: provider-a/us-east-1 not allowed\n# REFUSED: no policy for tenant unknown: deny by default\n```\n\n- **Hash caveat.** A plain hash of a short, guessable input can be reversed by guessing; in production\n  use a keyed hash (HMAC with a secret) so the trace still correlates requests without exposing content.","patterns":["**Gateway policy, deny by default.** Every model call goes through one gateway with per-tenant allowlists of providers and regions and a content-logging flag, as in the sketch above. Monitor refusal counts per tenant; a spike after a release means a code path tried a disallowed route.","**Policy as code on IaC plans.** Convert plans with `terraform show -json` and evaluate them with a policy engine such as Open Policy Agent (via Conftest) to reject storage outside approved regions or log sinks without retention. Track failed policy checks per pull request.","**Split audit and debug stores.** Audit records go to a write-once, long-retention store (for example S3 with Object Lock); debug traces get a short lifecycle expiry such as 30 days. Alert if any trace store lacks an expiry.","**Erasure that reaches derived data.** An erasure job deletes from the database, the vector index, caches and eval datasets, then a test queries each for the subject's identifiers. Monitor time to complete erasure requests and failed verification checks.","**Egress reconciliation.** Compare actual egress destinations and vendor invoices against the declared subprocessor list each month, because new SDKs and fallbacks arrive silently. Alert on any new external domain.","**Compliance as non-functional requirements.** Capture each regime as where/who/what/how-long/how/proof constraints in discovery, with an owner and the evidence each must produce."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    O[\"Obligation: law, contract, framework\"] --> C[\"Constraint: where, who, what, how long, how, proof\"]\n    C --> G[\"Runtime: model gateway per-tenant policy\"]\n    C --> P[\"Change time: policy check on IaC plans\"]\n    C --> R[\"Periodic: access reviews, deletion tests\"]\n    G -->|\"refusal logs, config\"| E[(\"Evidence pack\")]\n    P -->|\"failed plans, approvals\"| E\n    R -->|\"review records, test results\"| E\n    E --> A[\"Auditor or customer review\"]\n    A -.->|\"new finding or regime change\"| C","caption":"Each obligation becomes a constraint, then a control at runtime or change time, and the controls emit\nthe evidence auditors check."},"interview":[{"q":"Why are compliance requirements architectural rather than paperwork?","a":"- **They select components:** residency picks regions and endpoints, no-training terms pick model\n  providers, and BAAs decide whether a hosted API is allowed at all.\n- **They shape data design:** erasure rules argue against fine-tuning on personal data, and audit versus\n  minimization decides what logs contain.\n- **So learn them in discovery**, because finding them after the build can force a different deployment\n  model."},{"q":"A customer says \"we are HIPAA-regulated\". What do you translate that into?","a":"- **BAA chain:** a BAA with the customer and coverage for every subprocessor that sees PHI, including the\n  cloud and model providers; exclude providers that cannot sign.\n- **Data handling:** no training and bounded retention on the model path, minimum-necessary logging, and\n  access-logged storage for content.\n- **Evidence:** BAA list, data-flow diagram, retention config in IaC and access review records."},{"q":"How do you stop an engineer from silently breaking residency with a new fallback?","a":"- **Runtime chokepoint:** all model calls go through a gateway with per-tenant allowed providers and\n  regions, so an unlisted route is refused and logged.\n- **Change-time checks:** policy checks on infrastructure plans and review when a new subprocessor appears\n  in dependencies or egress.\n- **Periodic proof:** reconcile actual egress against the subprocessor list."},{"q":"When would you choose self-hosting a model over an API provider for compliance reasons?","a":"- **When terms cannot be confirmed:** no BAA, no clear no-training or retention terms, or no in-region\n  endpoint.\n- **When access itself is restricted**, as with export-controlled data or air-gapped mandates.\n- **The cost:** GPU capacity, operations and often model quality, so prefer an API with suitable terms\n  when one exists."},{"q":"An auditor wants seven years of records; privacy wants 30-day logs. How do you size and split storage?","a":"- **Two stores:** an audit record of who did what and when, retained seven years with write-once\n  protection; and debug traces with metadata and hashes, expiring at 30 days.\n- **Content lives with the customer** in an access-controlled audit store, not in vendor telemetry.\n- **Size the audit store from event counts** (events per day × record size × seven years), which stays\n  small because it holds metadata, not prompts."}],"tradeoffs":["**Region pinning vs failover.** Region-pinning and single-provider routing satisfy residency simply, but remove cross-region failover. If availability targets are strict, you need multiple in-region providers or accept degraded modes.","**Self-hosting vs cost and quality.** Self-hosting a model in the customer's environment satisfies the strictest data-custody constraints, but costs GPU capacity, operations and often model quality; API providers with suitable terms win when available.","**Minimal logging vs debuggability.** It lowers compliance exposure but makes production debugging harder; structured metadata, hashes and on-demand, access-logged content capture recover most of the debugging value.","**One compliant tier vs many.** A single compliant tier for every customer simplifies operations but over-serves smaller customers; separate tiers cost more to run and must be described precisely in questionnaires."],"pitfalls":["**Treating \"compliant\" as a vendor property rather than a configuration.** Symptom: using a provider's HIPAA-eligible service without a signed BAA or with ineligible features enabled.","**Forgetting derived data.** Embeddings, caches, eval datasets and traces contain the same personal data. Symptom: an erasure request completed in the database while vectors and logs still hold it.","**Fine-tuning on customer personal data without a deletion story.** Symptom: an erasure request that can only be honored by retraining or discarding the model.","**Engineering changes that silently violate a constraint**, such as a new fallback region or a new analytics SDK. Symptom: an audit finding against a previously compliant system.","**Deciding the EU AI Act risk tier or other legal classifications in engineering alone.** Symptom: a design built for the wrong obligations, discovered when counsel finally reviews it."],"when":"- **Ask in discovery**, before choosing a deployment model or model provider: which regimes apply, which\n  customer policies go beyond them, and what evidence their reviewers will want.\n- **Translate and record** each into where/who/what/how-long/how/proof constraints as non-functional\n  requirements, and enforce them in code and configuration so evidence comes for free.\n- **Revisit** whenever data sources, regions, providers or features change.\n- **Do not interpret law yourself:** bring counsel in for classification and contract terms.\n- **Go deeper** in the security-governance-compliance module for the full design of controls, audit\n  trails and residency.\n- **Interviews:** show the translation from regime to architecture.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements constraints as code: region and provider allowlists in the gateway, log redaction, retention policies in IaC, and deletion that reaches embeddings and caches.","architect":"Maps each applicable regime to architectural constraints, resolves conflicts such as audit retention versus minimization, and chooses the deployment model and providers that satisfy them.","fde":"Elicits the customer's real obligations and internal policies early, confirms subprocessor agreements like BAAs, and assembles the evidence pack for their compliance and security teams."},"check":{"q":"An EU customer says \"we are GDPR-regulated, so everything must stay in the EU.\" Your system uses an\nEU-hosted model by default, a US-hosted fallback, and fine-tunes a small model on customer tickets. What\ndo you check and change?","a":"First separate the law from the policy: GDPR permits transfers with safeguards, but the customer's\ncontractual requirement is stricter and is what you must meet. The US fallback violates it, so disable\ncross-region fallback for this tenant and use a second EU-region provider or a degraded mode instead. Check\nevery subprocessor for EU processing, including tracing and error tools. Fine-tuning on tickets creates an\nerasure problem, because personal data in weights cannot be selectively removed; either drop fine-tuning in\nfavor of retrieval over deletable indexes, train only on anonymized data, or agree a retraining policy.\nEnsure deletion reaches embeddings and caches. Document it all in the DPA and data-flow diagram."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why are compliance requirements best gathered before choosing the deployment model?","options":["Because auditors require requirements documents to be dated before code","Because constraints such as residency, subprocessor agreements and retention determine which regions, providers and deployment models are allowed","Because compliance requirements rarely change after launch","Because compliance reviews are faster for systems with fewer services"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Compliance rules are architectural: they can rule out a model provider, a region or SaaS hosting entirely. Learning them after the build forces rework, which is the cost early discovery avoids."},{"q":"A hospital asks whether your assistant may use a hosted model API for clinical notes. What is the deciding requirement under HIPAA?","options":["The model must score above a set threshold on medical benchmarks","The API must be hosted in the same state as the hospital","The prompts must be encrypted with the hospital's own keys","The model provider, as a subprocessor handling PHI, must be covered by a business associate agreement"],"answer":3,"explanation":"PHI may flow to a vendor and its subprocessors only under BAAs. Encryption and hosting location matter for security, but without a BAA the flow is not permitted regardless of quality or location."},{"q":"An auditor requires seven-year retention of activity records while the privacy team demands minimal logging of personal data. Which design resolves both?","options":["Keep full prompts and outputs for seven years in the debug trace store","Disable logging entirely to satisfy minimization","Retain an audit record of who did what and when, with content in an access-controlled store, while debug traces keep only metadata for a short period","Encrypt the logs so retention rules no longer apply"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Separating audit records from debug telemetry satisfies each requirement on its own terms. Keeping all content for seven years violates minimization, encryption does not remove retention duties, and disabling logs fails the audit obligation."}]},{"key":"deployment/deployment-readiness-reviews","sources":["Beyer, Jones, Petoff, Murphy, 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering"],"why":"- **Launch risk is spread across many owners.** Builders of the prompt and retrieval pipeline, the\n  customer's security, network and identity teams, the business owner who defined success, and whoever is\n  paged at 3 a.m. Each checks its own slice; nobody checks the seams.\n- **The seams are where launches fail.** The eval passed, but on last month's index. Security approved the\n  architecture, but not the tool the agent gained afterwards. Dashboards exist, but no alert routes to a\n  human. A rollback plan exists, but nobody has run it.\n- **\"The tech lead decides\" fails twice.** One person cannot hold the full checklist, and the items they\n  forget are the ones outside their expertise. Schedule pressure from a date promised to a sponsor\n  rationalises every gap as \"we'll fix it in hypercare\".\n- **The review makes the decision structural.** Criteria are agreed before the date, evidence replaces\n  assertions, and the record says which risks were accepted and by whom.\n- **It has precedent.** Google's SRE practice formalised the Production Readiness Review, run before an\n  SRE team agrees to carry a service's pager. The same logic applies when a customer's operations team is\n  about to inherit your system.","how":"### 1. Agree the criteria early\nDraft the checklist at the start of the build, not the week before launch, because criteria written after\nthe system exists describe what was built rather than what is needed.\n\n| Category | Typical items for an AI system |\n|---|---|\n| **Quality** | Golden-set and customer regression evals meet the acceptance criteria from scoping; UAT signed off; known failure modes documented |\n| **Safety and security** | Security and architecture review approved for the *current* design; guardrails tested against the red-team set; retention, PII and residency match the contract |\n| **Operability** | Health checks, dashboards and alerts route to a named on-call; every alert links to a runbook; logs and traces kept long enough to debug |\n| **Capacity and cost** | Load test at expected peak plus headroom; provider quotas and rate limits confirmed in writing; per-request and daily cost ceilings enforced, not just estimated |\n| **Release mechanics** | Rollout stages, promotion criteria and rollback triggers defined; rollback rehearsed in a production-like environment; feature flag or kill switch verified |\n| **People** | Support model and escalation paths agreed; customer team trained; hypercare staffed |\n\n### 2. Evidence, not assertions\n- **Every item has an owner and a link.** An eval report with its dataset version, a load-test result, a\n  screenshot of an alert firing in staging, the ticket where security signed off.\n- **No evidence means not done**, because \"done\" is the cheapest word to say and the most expensive to be\n  wrong about.\n- **Pin evidence to the deploying configuration.** Record a release fingerprint (model identifier, prompt\n  version or hash, retrieval config, index build, tool list) and attach it to every eval and load-test\n  result, because an eval on last week's prompt is evidence about a different system.\n- **Mismatched fingerprint reverts the item to not done.** That catches the \"passed, but on last month's\n  index\" gap mechanically instead of by luck.\n\n### 3. Classify every item\n- **Blocking:** no launch without it, e.g. rollback works, PII is not logged.\n- **Non-blocking:** launch can proceed if the gap is accepted.\n- **Waiver:** how a non-blocking gap is accepted: a named risk owner, a compensating control and an expiry\n  date. Waivers expire because \"temporary\" exceptions without dates become permanent.\n\n### 4. Decide and record\n1. **Fix decision rights before the meeting:** who chairs, who decides, and which owners hold a veto on\n   their blocking items (security on data handling, the operating team on operability). Otherwise the most\n   senior person decides everything and you are back to \"tech lead decides\", with more attendees.\n2. **Put the consequence-carriers among the deciders.** The team taking the pager and the business owner\n   decide; they are not the audience.\n3. **Walk the open items, not the green ones.** Good prep keeps the meeting short.\n4. **Pick an outcome:** *go*, *conditional go* (launch with listed waivers), or *no-go* with the specific\n   items that must change.\n5. **Write it down with its waivers**, because when an incident happens in week two the first question\n   will be \"did we know about this?\".\n\n### 5. Re-run on material change\n- **A review approves one configuration:** model version, prompt, tools, index, data sources.\n- **Swapping the model or adding a write-capable tool is a new launch** for review purposes, scaled down to\n  the items the change touches.","example":"### Readiness gate as code\nA readiness gate kept as data next to the code, so the go/no-go is computed rather than argued. The\nscenario: an insurer's claims-triage assistant, two weeks before go-live.\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import date\n\nitems = [  # (id, blocking, evidence_link_or_None, passed, waiver)\n    (\"eval.golden_set>=0.85\",   True,  \"evals/run-0412.json\", True,  None),\n    (\"security.review_current\", True,  \"SEC-2291\",            True,  None),\n    (\"rollback.rehearsed\",      True,  None,                  False, None),\n    (\"alerts.route_to_oncall\",  True,  \"staging-alert.png\",   True,  None),\n    (\"load.peak_x2\",            False, \"load/0409.html\",      False,\n        {\"owner\": \"cust-ops-lead\", \"control\": \"rate limit 20 rps\", \"expires\": date(2026, 11, 30)}),\n    (\"training.agents_trained\", False, None,                  False, None),\n]\n\ndef decide(items, today):\n    blockers, waived, gaps = [], [], []\n    for iid, blocking, evidence, passed, waiver in items:\n        ok = passed and evidence is not None          # no evidence means not done\n        if ok:\n            continue\n        if blocking:\n            blockers.append(iid)\n        elif waiver and waiver[\"expires\"] > today:\n            waived.append((iid, waiver[\"owner\"], waiver[\"expires\"]))\n        else:\n            gaps.append(iid)                            # non-blocking but unowned: still a no-go\n    if blockers or gaps:\n        return \"NO-GO\", blockers + gaps\n    return (\"CONDITIONAL GO\" if waived else \"GO\"), waived\n\nprint(decide(items, date(2026, 10, 20)))\n# ('NO-GO', ['rollback.rehearsed', 'training.agents_trained'])\n```\n\nRehearsing the rollback and either training the agents or writing a waiver for it turns this into a\nconditional go, with the load-test gap explicitly owned by the customer's ops lead until 30 November.\n\n### Fingerprint check that catches stale evidence\nEach piece of evidence stores the release fingerprint it was produced against; the gate compares it with\nthe release candidate.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, json\n\ndef fingerprint(release: dict) -> str:\n    keys = (\"model\", \"prompt_hash\", \"retrieval_config\", \"index_build\", \"tools\")\n    blob = json.dumps({k: release[k] for k in keys}, sort_keys=True)\n    return hashlib.sha256(blob.encode()).hexdigest()[:12]\n\ncandidate = {\"model\": \"model-v3\", \"prompt_hash\": \"a1f9\", \"retrieval_config\": \"k=8\",\n             \"index_build\": \"idx-2026-10-18\", \"tools\": [\"lookup_policy\"]}\neval_run = dict(candidate, index_build=\"idx-2026-09-15\")   # eval ran on last month's index\n\nstale = fingerprint(eval_run) != fingerprint(candidate)\nprint(\"eval evidence stale:\", stale)   # True -> item reverts to not done\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: what the review caught\nIllustrative findings from one review of the claims-triage launch.\n\n| Finding | Class | Outcome |\n|---|---|---|\n| Rollback never rehearsed | Blocking | Rehearsed in staging two days later |\n| Load test at 2x peak failed | Non-blocking | Waiver: owner cust-ops-lead, rate limit 20 rps, expires 30 November |\n| Eval evidence on old index | Blocking (fingerprint mismatch) | Eval re-run on the release candidate |\n| Agent training not done | Non-blocking, no waiver | Training scheduled before go-live |","patterns":["**Checklist in the repo, evidence by link.** When releases are frequent, keep the checklist as a version-controlled file reviewed in the release pull request; CI fails if a blocking item has no evidence link. Monitor how many items are green without a link (should be zero).","**Release fingerprint on every artifact.** Stamp eval reports and load-test results with a hash of model, prompt, retrieval config, index build and tool list; the gate compares it with the candidate. Monitor fingerprint mismatches per review.","**Waiver register with expiry alerts.** Store waivers in a tracker with owner, compensating control and expiry; a scheduled job flags waivers within a week of expiry. Monitor open waiver count and how many are renewed rather than closed.","**Risk-tiered reviews.** A prompt tweak behind a flag gets an automated eval gate; a new write-capable tool gets the full meeting. Write the tier rules down so the trigger is not negotiated per change.","**Escape analysis after launch.** For each incident in the first weeks, ask whether a checklist item would have caught it, and add one if so. Monitor incidents per launch that map to a missing item."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    I[\"Checklist item\"] --> E{\"Evidence linked and fingerprint matches?\"}\n    E -->|\"yes\"| P[\"Passed\"]\n    E -->|\"no\"| B{\"Blocking item?\"}\n    B -->|\"yes\"| NG[\"No-go: must fix\"]\n    B -->|\"no\"| W{\"Waiver with owner, control, expiry?\"}\n    W -->|\"yes, not expired\"| CG[\"Conditional go\"]\n    W -->|\"no\"| NG\n    P --> D[\"Record decision and waivers\"]\n    CG --> D\n    NG --> D\n    D -.->|\"material change\"| I","caption":"Each checklist item flows through the same evidence and classification gate; the worst outcome across\nitems sets the decision."},"interview":[{"q":"What is a deployment readiness review, and why not let the tech lead decide?","a":"- **Definition:** a scheduled go/no-go where every launch criterion (quality, security, operability,\n  capacity, release mechanics, people) is judged against linked evidence by a named owner.\n- **Why not one person:** they cannot hold the whole checklist, they miss items outside their expertise,\n  and schedule pressure turns gaps into \"we'll fix it in hypercare\"."},{"q":"The eval report is green, but an incident shows the model was evaluated on an old index. How would you prevent that?","a":"- **Fingerprint the release:** model, prompt hash, retrieval config, index build and tool list, hashed\n  and stamped on every eval and load-test result.\n- **Gate on a match:** evidence whose fingerprint differs from the release candidate reverts the item to\n  not done, so the stale run is caught mechanically."},{"q":"When would you skip a full readiness meeting?","a":"- **Low-risk, reversible changes:** a prompt tweak behind a flag needs an automated eval gate, and a\n  low-risk internal tool can use a checklist in the pull request.\n- **Never for material changes:** a new model or provider, a write-capable tool, a new data source or\n  region needs at least the affected items re-reviewed."},{"q":"How do you keep waivers from becoming permanent?","a":"- **Mandatory fields:** named risk owner, compensating control and expiry date; no waiver without all\n  three.\n- **Track them:** a register with expiry alerts, and a signal when waivers outnumber fixes across\n  releases, which means the criteria or the schedule are wrong."},{"q":"How do you know your readiness review is working?","a":"- **Escape analysis:** for incidents in the first weeks after launch, ask whether a checklist item would\n  have caught each one and add it if so.\n- **Health signals:** few conditional-go conditions, zero green items without evidence, and the same\n  waiver not reappearing quarter after quarter."}],"tradeoffs":["**Coverage vs calendar time.** A formal review catches cross-team gaps but adds calendar time; for a low-risk internal tool a lightweight checklist in the pull request is enough, and a full meeting is ceremony.","**Strictness vs honesty.** Strict blocking criteria prevent bad launches, but if everything is blocking the team learns to game evidence. Keep the blocking list short and reserve it for irreversible or customer-visible harm.","**Speed vs debt.** Waivers let launches proceed under known risk, but each one is debt. The trade flips when waivers outnumber fixes across releases, which means the criteria or the schedule are wrong.","**Consistency vs throughput.** A central review board brings consistency across customers but becomes a bottleneck at scale. Mature teams move to self-service checklists with automated evidence and reserve humans for exceptions."],"pitfalls":["**Reviewing the day before launch.** Every finding becomes a waiver; the symptom is a \"conditional go\" with more conditions than the previous three launches combined. Fix: hold it early enough to fix blockers.","**Accepting \"yes, done\" without a link.** Symptom: an incident where the alert, runbook or rollback marked green never existed. Fix: no link, not done.","**Reviewing the architecture approved months ago.** Symptom: a tool, data source or model in production that no security reviewer ever saw. Fix: review what is deploying now, by fingerprint.","**Waivers with no expiry or owner.** Symptom: the same accepted risk in next quarter's review and nobody can say who agreed to it. Fix: owner and expiry are mandatory fields.","**Treating AI quality as a single number.** Symptom: the launch passed the aggregate eval but fails a critical slice (one claim type, one language) the business cares most about. Fix: per-slice criteria."],"when":"- **Use before:** any first production launch, handing operations to a customer team, and material\n  changes: a new model or provider, a new tool with side effects, a new data source, a new region.\n- **Scale it to risk:** a prompt tweak behind a flag needs an automated eval gate, not a meeting; an agent\n  that can issue refunds needs the full review.\n- **Measure the review by what escapes it:** track incidents in the first weeks after launch and ask\n  whether a checklist item would have caught each one, then add it.\n- **Interviews:** a strong answer describes criteria, evidence, waivers with expiry, and the re-review\n  trigger, not just \"we had a launch checklist\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Produces the evidence: versioned eval reports, load-test results, a rehearsed rollback, and alerts proven to fire, linked from each checklist item rather than described.","architect":"Owns the criteria and the blocking list, decides which changes trigger re-review, and signs off on which risks the organisation is willing to accept with waivers.","fde":"Runs the review with the customer's security, ops and business owners in the room, so their sign-offs and accepted risks are recorded before the go-live date is defended."},"check":{"q":"Two days before go-live, the load test shows the system handles only 60% of forecast peak because\nthe customer's provider quota is lower than requested. The sponsor wants to launch. What do you do?","a":"First classify the gap: is overload customer-visible harm or graceful degradation? If the system\nhas a rate limiter and a clear \"busy, try again\" path, capacity can be non-blocking, so I would propose\na conditional go with a waiver: the customer's ops lead owns the risk, the compensating control is a\nrate limit plus a staged rollout capped below 60% of peak, and the waiver expires when the quota\nincrease lands. If overload would cause silent failures or timeouts mid-workflow, it is blocking and the\nanswer is no-go or a reduced launch scope. Either way the decision is written down with its owner."},"quiz":[{"q":"What most distinguishes a readiness review from a team simply agreeing they are ready?","options":["It is attended by more senior people","Each criterion is agreed in advance and judged against linked evidence with a named owner","It replaces the need for evals and load tests","It always happens after the first week of production traffic"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The value is structural: criteria fixed before schedule pressure, evidence instead of assertions, and recorded ownership. Seniority alone does not prevent unchecked seams, and the review consumes evals rather than replacing them."},{"q":"A non-blocking item failed. What makes it acceptable to launch anyway?","options":["The tech lead believes it will be fixed during hypercare","The item is moved to the backlog with a low priority","A waiver with a named risk owner, a compensating control and an expiry date","The sponsor approves the launch date"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A waiver makes the accepted risk explicit and temporary. Backlog items and verbal assurances have no owner or deadline, so the gap silently becomes permanent and nobody is accountable when it bites."},{"q":"Which change most clearly requires re-running at least part of the readiness review?","options":["Rewording a help-text string in the UI","Rotating an API key on its normal schedule","Adding a new dashboard panel","Giving the agent a new tool that can write to the customer's CRM"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A review approves a specific configuration. A write-capable tool changes the security surface, the failure modes and the rollback story, so the affected items (security, guardrails, runbooks) must be re-checked."}]},{"key":"deployment/uat","sources":[],"why":"- **\"Passes our tests\" is not \"does the job\".** Engineering tests check the builders' understanding of\n  the requirement. They miss a misunderstood requirement, an unmentioned workflow step, or a correct but\n  useless output: a claims summary missing the one field adjusters always check, or a cited policy in a\n  format compliance cannot file.\n- **Only users know those things**, and they often discover them only by using the system.\n- **Acceptance is often contractual.** In enterprise and forward-deployed work it triggers payment or the\n  pilot-to-production move. Without a defined UAT it becomes a negotiation over impressions: two bad demo\n  answers stall the project, or everyone says \"looks good\" and the system is abandoned three weeks after\n  launch.\n- **The naive version fails for AI.** \"Give users access for two weeks and ask what they think\" means a\n  handful of queries per person, judged on memorable failures. Output varies run to run, so one tester\n  passes what another fails, and without a rubric nobody can settle it.\n- **So design it like an experiment:** fixed criteria, representative cases, a scoring rubric, and a\n  sample large enough to separate \"good enough\" from \"not quite\".","how":"### 1. Fix acceptance criteria before testing starts\n- **Criteria come from scoping, written as measurable statements:** \"at least 85% of triage\n  recommendations rated correct or acceptable by adjusters\", \"zero responses that disclose another\n  customer's data\", \"p95 response under 8 seconds\".\n- **Hard gates** (safety, privacy, legal): any single failure blocks.\n- **Rate thresholds** (quality metrics): judged on a sample.\n- **Keep them separate**, because a single privacy leak is not averaged away by 99 good answers.\n\n### 2. Build a representative case set\n- **Draw from real historical work**, stratified by the slices the business cares about (claim type,\n  language, document quality, customer tier), plus deliberately chosen edge cases.\n- **Representativeness matters** because the pass rate on easy cases tells you nothing about the hard\n  ones.\n- **Store a reference answer per case**, written by subject-matter experts, so scoring is not improvised.\n\n### 3. Run in a production-like environment\n- **Use the real integrations** (identity, document stores, downstream systems) with production-shaped\n  data, isolated from production writes.\n- **Not mocks**, because mocks hide the integration failures UAT is best at catching: permission errors,\n  slow connectors, malformed documents.\n\n### 4. Score with a rubric, not a thumbs-up\n- **A small scale with written definitions**, for example *correct*, *acceptable with minor edit*,\n  *wrong*, *harmful*.\n- **Overlap a fraction of cases between testers and measure agreement**, because if two adjusters\n  disagree on the same output, the criterion is ambiguous and must be fixed before the numbers mean\n  anything.\n\n### 5. Size the sample and handle non-determinism\nA rate threshold is a claim about the true pass rate, so the lower end of the confidence interval must\nclear the bar. A planning estimate with the normal approximation:\n\n```\nn ≈ z² · p(1 − p) / m²        z = 1.96 for 95%, p = expected pass rate, m = margin you can afford\n```\n\n| Expected rate | Bar | Margin | Normal estimate | Wilson (reporting) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 88% | 85% | 3 points | 1.96² × 0.88 × 0.12 / 0.03² ≈ 450 cases | roughly 550 cases (slightly more conservative) |\n\n- **The m² in the denominator is the lesson:** halving the margin quadruples the cases. A criterion set\n  just below the expected rate is expensive to prove, so negotiate the bar and the sample together during\n  scoping rather than at sign-off.\n- **Repeated runs are not independent samples.** Running each case three times does not turn 200 cases\n  into 600 observations, because runs of one case share input and difficulty and fail together.\n- **Score at the case level:** for example \"passes on at least 2 of 3 runs\", or the per-case pass\n  fraction averaged across cases.\n- **Report the run-to-run flip rate separately.** A case that passes once and fails once is a stability\n  defect in its own right, even if the average looks fine.\n\n### 6. Triage defects by cause\nEvery failure gets a severity (blocker, major, minor) and a cause; the cause decides the fix and whether\na re-test is needed.\n\n| Cause | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Integration** | Wrong data reached the model |\n| **Retrieval** | Right data existed, was not found |\n| **Model/prompt** | Right context, wrong answer |\n| **Requirement** | System did what was specified; the specification was wrong |\n\n- **A requirement finding is a change request, not a defect.** Treating it as a defect is how UAT never\n  ends.\n\n### 7. Exit and sign off\n1. Hard gates are clean.\n2. Rate thresholds are met with enough confidence.\n3. No open blockers remain.\n4. A named person on the customer side signs off, recorded.\n5. Every failing case found during UAT goes into the customer-specific regression suite so it stays\n   fixed.","example":"### Is 88% on 200 cases enough? A Wilson interval check\n```python\nfrom math import sqrt\n\ndef wilson(passed, n, z=1.96):\n    p = passed / n\n    denom = 1 + z * z / n\n    centre = (p + z * z / (2 * n)) / denom\n    half = z * sqrt(p * (1 - p) / n + z * z / (4 * n * n)) / denom\n    return centre - half, centre + half\n\nbar = 0.85\nfor passed, n in [(176, 200), (352, 400), (528, 600)]:\n    lo, hi = wilson(passed, n)\n    print(f\"{passed}/{n}: {lo:.1%} to {hi:.1%}  clears bar: {lo >= bar}\")\n# 176/200: 82.8% to 91.8%  clears bar: False\n# 352/400: 84.4% to 90.8%  clears bar: False\n# 528/600: 85.2% to 90.4%  clears bar: True\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: an insurer's triage sign-off\nThe criterion is \"at least 85% of triage recommendations rated correct or acceptable\". Adjusters score\n200 stratified cases and 176 pass: **88%**. The project manager wants to sign off.\n\n| Sample at 88% | 95% Wilson interval | Clears 85%? |\n|---|---|---|\n| 200 cases | roughly **82.8% to 91.8%** | No |\n| 400 cases | roughly 84.4% to 90.8% | No, still not clear |\n| about 600 cases | roughly 85.2% to 90.4% | Yes |\n\n- **The sample cannot tell** \"comfortably above the bar\" from \"slightly below it\", because the lower bound\n  is below 85%.\n- **The options are concrete:** score about 400 more cases, renegotiate to judge on the point estimate (an\n  explicit, recorded decision), or improve the system so the observed rate rises.\n\nThe slice view changes the story:\n\n| Slice | Cases | Passed | Rate |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Handwritten claim forms | 40 of 200 (20%) | 25 of 40 | about 62% |\n| Typed forms | 160 | 151 of 160 | about 94% |\n\n- **The aggregate hides an unfit slice.** Of the 24 failures, 15 are handwritten forms.\n- **Triage points to integration:** 11 of the 15 are OCR-quality failures, not model reasoning. The fix\n  is the document parser, followed by a re-test of that slice only.\n\n### Case-level scoring with repeated runs\n```python\nruns = {  # case_id -> pass/fail for 3 runs\n    \"c1\": [True, True, True], \"c2\": [True, False, True],\n    \"c3\": [False, False, False], \"c4\": [True, False, False],\n}\ncase_pass = {c: sum(r) >= 2 for c, r in runs.items()}          # \"passes on at least 2 of 3\"\nflip_rate = sum(len(set(r)) > 1 for r in runs.values()) / len(runs)\nprint(sum(case_pass.values()) / len(runs), flip_rate)        # 0.5 0.5 -> report both\n```","patterns":["**Pre-registered acceptance doc.** At scoping, write hard gates, rate thresholds, sample size and the slices to report, and get the sponsor's sign-off on that document. Monitor how many criteria change after UAT starts (should be zero without a recorded change request).","**Calibration round before scoring.** Testers score the same 20 to 30 outputs, discuss disagreements, and tighten rubric definitions before the main round. Monitor inter-rater agreement (for example Cohen's kappa) on the overlap set throughout.","**Repeated runs with case-level verdicts.** The harness runs each case several times, records a case-level verdict, and reports flip rate as its own metric. Monitor flip rate per slice; a rising flip rate after a prompt change is a stability regression.","**Defect tracker with a cause field.** Every failure is filed with severity and one of integration, retrieval, model/prompt or requirement; requirement findings route to the change-request process. Monitor defects by cause per round to see whether rounds converge.","**Failures become regression cases.** Each failing UAT case is exported with its trace into the customer-specific regression suite, which then gates every later release."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    C[\"Stratified case set\"] --> R[\"Run each case N times\"]\n    R --> S[\"Testers score with rubric\"]\n    S --> F{\"Case passes?\"}\n    F -->|\"no\"| T{\"Triage by cause\"}\n    T -->|\"integration, retrieval, model\"| X[\"Fix and re-test slice\"]\n    T -->|\"requirement\"| CR[\"Change request to sponsor\"]\n    X --> R\n    F -->|\"yes\"| A[\"Aggregate per slice\"]\n    A --> G{\"Gates clean and CI lower bound above bar?\"}\n    G -->|\"no\"| M[\"More cases or improve system\"]\n    G -->|\"yes\"| SO[\"Named customer sign-off\"]\n    X -.->|\"add to\"| RS[(\"Regression suite\")]","caption":"Each case is run, scored against the rubric and, if it fails, triaged by cause; exit needs clean gates\nand a confident pass rate."},"interview":[{"q":"How is UAT different from your own eval suite?","a":"- **Who judges:** the customer's real users score outputs on realistic tasks, so misunderstood\n  requirements and missing workflow steps surface; builders' evals only check the builders'\n  understanding.\n- **What it decides:** acceptance is often a contractual milestone, so criteria, rubric and sign-off owner\n  are fixed in advance rather than argued from impressions."},{"q":"Your UAT shows 88% against an 85% bar on 150 cases. Would you sign off?","a":"- **Not on the point estimate alone:** the 95% interval at that size reaches well below 85%, so the data\n  is consistent with being under the bar.\n- **Offer options:** score more cases (around 600 total at 88% clears the bar), explicitly agree to judge\n  on the point estimate, or improve the system.\n- **Check slices first:** a weak slice may matter more than the headline number."},{"q":"How do you handle non-determinism in UAT scoring?","a":"- **Repeated runs are correlated:** three runs of 200 cases are not 600 independent samples, so the\n  confidence interval uses the case count.\n- **Use a case-level rule** such as passes on at least 2 of 3 runs, and report flip rate separately as a\n  stability defect."},{"q":"A tester files a \"bug\": the output matches the spec but does not fit their filing process. What do you do?","a":"- **Classify it as a requirement finding:** the system did what was specified; the spec was wrong.\n- **Route it as a change request** with the sponsor, so its cost and priority are explicit; filing it as a\n  defect hides scope change and stops UAT from converging."},{"q":"When would you judge against a baseline instead of an absolute bar?","a":"- **When a current process exists:** if manual triage is right 80% of the time, a system at 84% may be\n  worth launching even below an 85% absolute bar.\n- **Cost:** you must measure the baseline on the same cases and rubric, and hard gates (privacy, safety)\n  still apply regardless."}],"tradeoffs":["**Statistical power vs SME time.** Larger, stratified case sets give meaningful results but consume scarce expert time; when experts are expensive, spend the budget on high-risk slices and accept wider intervals elsewhere.","**Scripted vs exploratory.** Scripted cases make results comparable and repeatable, while exploratory use surfaces unknown workflow needs; run both, and weight exploratory findings more heavily early in UAT.","**Absolute bar vs baseline.** Strict exit criteria protect the customer, but may block a system already better than the current manual process; when a baseline exists, judging against it can be the more honest criterion.","**Realism vs lead time.** Production-like data catches real integration failures but needs data access approvals that can take weeks; start those at kickoff, not at UAT."],"pitfalls":["**Defining criteria during UAT.** Symptom: a moving target where each round of fixes surfaces a new expectation nobody wrote down. Fix: pre-register criteria in scoping.","**Letting the build team score outputs.** Symptom: a high UAT pass rate followed by low adoption and complaints in the first week of hypercare. Fix: the customer's users score.","**Running each case once.** Symptom: a failure the engineers \"cannot reproduce\" because a re-run happens to pass. Fix: repeated runs with case-level verdicts.","**Treating every change request as a defect.** Symptom: UAT rounds that never converge and a backlog where scope creep is indistinguishable from bugs. Fix: a requirement cause class.","**Judging on the aggregate only.** Symptom: a passing headline number while one slice, such as one language or document type, fails badly. Fix: per-slice reporting."],"when":"- **Use when:** people other than the builders will use the system and acceptance carries weight: a\n  customer go-live, a pilot-to-production gate, a contractual milestone.\n- **Plan it during scoping**, because the case set, rubric, environment and data approvals take longer\n  than the testing itself.\n- **Go lighter for internal tools** with a small user group: a few power users and a short rubric.\n- **Measure:** pass rate with a confidence interval, per-slice pass rates, inter-rater agreement and\n  defect counts by cause.\n- **Interviews:** the depth signal is criteria fixed in advance, sample size, non-determinism and triage\n  by cause, rather than \"users tried it and liked it\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the UAT harness: case loading, repeated runs per case, rubric capture, and export of every failure into the regression suite with its trace attached.","architect":"Defines the UAT environment boundary, ensuring production-like integrations and data without production writes, and owns how acceptance criteria map to measurable signals.","fde":"Recruits the customer's testers, runs calibration on the rubric, triages findings with them, and separates genuine defects from change requests in front of the sponsor."},"check":{"q":"UAT testers rate a summarisation feature at 90% acceptable, but two senior adjusters say it is\nunusable. How do you reconcile this before sign-off?","a":"First check whether the disagreement is about the rubric or the cases. Have the seniors and other\ntesters score the same overlapping outputs; low agreement means \"acceptable\" is defined differently and\nthe rubric needs tightening, perhaps because seniors need a field others ignore. Then check slices: the\nseniors may handle complex claims that are under-represented in the case set. If so, stratify, add cases\nfrom their workload and report per-slice rates. If their concern is a workflow need never in the\ncriteria, record it as a change request with the sponsor rather than a defect, and decide whether it\nblocks."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why split UAT criteria into hard gates and rate thresholds?","options":["Hard gates are tested by engineers and rate thresholds by users","Some failures, like a privacy leak, are unacceptable even once, so they cannot be averaged into a pass rate","Rate thresholds only apply to latency, not quality","Hard gates make UAT shorter by skipping sampling"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A pass rate averages over cases, which is right for quality but wrong for safety, privacy or legal failures where a single occurrence is a blocker regardless of how many other cases succeed."},{"q":"UAT shows 88% pass on 200 cases against an 85% bar. What is the most defensible conclusion?","options":["The system passes because 88% exceeds 85%","The system fails because some cases failed","The sample is too small to show the true rate clears 85%, so either test more cases or explicitly agree to judge on the point estimate","The result is invalid because AI outputs are non-deterministic"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The 95% interval for 176 of 200 reaches below 85%, so the data is consistent with being under the bar. Non-determinism does not invalidate sampling; it is one more reason to treat the rate statistically."},{"q":"A tester reports that the system correctly followed the specification, but the specified output format does not fit their filing process. How should it be classified?","options":["As a blocker defect in the model","As a retrieval failure","As a minor integration bug","As a requirement finding, handled as a change request with the sponsor"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The system did what was specified; the specification was wrong or incomplete. Treating it as a defect hides scope change and stops UAT from converging, while a change request makes the cost explicit."}]},{"key":"deployment/rollout-criteria-and-rollback-triggers","sources":[],"why":"- **A staged rollout only reduces risk if someone decides at each stage.** Without written criteria, that\n  is whoever watches the dashboard, when numbers are ambiguous, with a launch date behind them.\n- **The predictable failure is \"probably noise, keep going\"**, then an incident at 100% that was visible\n  at 5%. The opposite also happens: a rollback on pure noise, and the team loses confidence in releasing\n  at all.\n- **AI releases are often not code.** A new prompt, model version, re-embedded index or retrieval\n  parameter can degrade quality with no rise in errors or latency at all.\n- **Quality signals are slow and noisy.** Thumbs-down rates, escalations and LLM-judge scores need\n  hundreds of requests and hours or days before a difference beats noise. And some AI actions are\n  irreversible: emails sent or records updated by an agent are not undone by redeploying.\n- **\"Watch the dashboards and use judgement\" fails** because judgement under pressure is biased toward\n  continuing. Writing criteria in advance moves the hard thinking to a calm moment and makes the live\n  decision mechanical.","how":"### 1. Define the stages and what each must prove\n- **A typical ladder:** internal users, then a small percentage or one friendly tenant, then a larger\n  fraction (a quarter), then everyone.\n- **Minimum duration per stage**, because traffic varies by hour and weekday; a stage that only saw\n  Sunday-night traffic proved little about Monday morning.\n- **Minimum sample per stage** (requests, sessions or completed tasks), because rare failures cannot be\n  observed in small samples.\n\nError counts are roughly Poisson: with *n* requests at baseline rate *p* you expect *np* errors with a\nstandard deviation of about √(np).\n\n| Requests at a 1% baseline | Expected errors | Doubled rate (2%) | Distinguishable? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 100 | about 1 (±1) | about 2 | No: a canary at 2% is indistinguishable from noise |\n| 3,000 | about 30 (±5.5) | about 60 | Yes: a gap of more than five standard deviations |\n\n- **Derive the minimum sample** from the baseline rate and the smallest regression you care about, not\n  as a round number.\n\n### Quality signals need far bigger samples\nThe regressions that matter are a few points on a rate near 90%, not a doubling. To detect an LLM-judge\npass rate falling from 91% to 88% at the usual significance (5%) and power (80%):\n\n```\nn per arm ≈ (z_α/2 + z_β)² · (p₁q₁ + p₂q₂) / (p₁ − p₂)²\n          = (1.96 + 0.84)² · (0.91·0.09 + 0.88·0.12) / 0.03² ≈ 1,600 judged responses\n```\n\n- **That counts judged responses, not requests.** Judging 10% of traffic means about 16,000 requests\n  before a 3-point quality drop is reliably visible.\n- **So quality criteria force longer bake times than error criteria**, and a stage with \"enough requests\"\n  may still have too few judgements to decide anything about quality.\n\n### 2. Compare against a baseline, not an absolute\n- **Run old and new side by side** on comparable traffic, so seasonal or upstream effects hit both.\n- **Express criteria as relative regressions** (\"canary p95 latency no more than 20% above control\")\n  **plus absolute floors**, so tiny baselines do not produce absurd ratios.\n\n### 3. Split triggers into hard and soft\n| | Hard triggers | Soft triggers |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Signals** | Error rate, timeouts, p95 or p99 latency, guardrail block rate, cost per request, provider 429s, crash loops | Online LLM-judge score on sampled traffic, user feedback rate, escalation-to-human rate, task completion rate |\n| **Nature** | Fast to measure, unambiguous | Slower, noisier |\n| **Action** | Automated, immediate rollback | Set a *hold*; a named person decides within a set time |\n\n### 4. Make the outcome one of three states\n- **Promote:** all criteria met, duration and sample reached.\n- **Hold:** not enough evidence yet, or a soft trigger needs review.\n- **Roll back:** any hard trigger tripped after the minimum sample.\n- **The explicit hold state** prevents both premature promotion and panic rollbacks.\n\n### 5. Make rollback cheap and rehearsed\n- **Version every behaviour-changing artefact:** prompt, model identifier (pinned, never a floating\n  alias), retrieval config, index.\n- **Ship behind a flag** so rollback is a config change, not a redeploy.\n- **Keep the old index serving** until the new one has baked, because re-embedding a corpus can take hours\n  and is not a rollback you can do mid-incident.\n- **Fence agent side effects:** gate write actions behind approval or dry-run during early stages, because\n  no trigger can un-send an email.\n\n### 6. Decide roll forward versus roll back\n- **Roll back by default**, because it restores a known state.\n- **Roll forward with a fix only** when rollback is impossible (a schema migration already applied, an\n  external contract already changed) or the old version is itself broken by an external change, such as a\n  deprecated provider model.","example":"### Stage gate with hard, soft and evidence checks\nA stage gate evaluated every 15 minutes during a canary of a new prompt and model version. The numbers\nare illustrative thresholds a team might agree, not recommendations.\n\n```python\ndef evaluate(canary, control, minutes_in_stage, min_minutes=120, min_requests=3000, min_judged=1600):\n    # Hard triggers: checked as soon as there is enough data to trust them.\n    if canary[\"requests\"] >= min_requests:\n        err_limit = max(2 * control[\"error_rate\"], 0.01)          # relative, with an absolute floor\n        if canary[\"error_rate\"] > err_limit:\n            return \"ROLLBACK\", \"error rate\"\n        if canary[\"p95_ms\"] > 1.2 * control[\"p95_ms\"]:\n            return \"ROLLBACK\", \"p95 latency\"\n        if canary[\"cost_per_req\"] > 1.3 * control[\"cost_per_req\"]:\n            return \"ROLLBACK\", \"cost per request\"\n        if canary[\"guardrail_block_rate\"] > control[\"guardrail_block_rate\"] + 0.02:\n            return \"ROLLBACK\", \"guardrail blocks\"\n    # Not enough evidence yet: neither promote nor roll back.\n    if (canary[\"requests\"] < min_requests or minutes_in_stage < min_minutes\n            or canary[\"judged\"] < min_judged):\n        return \"HOLD\", \"collecting evidence\"\n    # Soft trigger: sampled LLM-judge quality needs a human if it drops.\n    if canary[\"judge_pass\"] < control[\"judge_pass\"] - 0.03:\n        return \"HOLD\", \"quality drop: page release owner\"\n    return \"PROMOTE\", \"all criteria met\"\n\ncontrol = {\"error_rate\": 0.004, \"p95_ms\": 4100, \"cost_per_req\": 0.011,\n           \"guardrail_block_rate\": 0.006, \"judge_pass\": 0.91, \"judged\": 5000}\ncanary  = {\"requests\": 18000, \"judged\": 1800, \"error_rate\": 0.005, \"p95_ms\": 4500, \"cost_per_req\": 0.012,\n           \"guardrail_block_rate\": 0.007, \"judge_pass\": 0.86}\nprint(evaluate(canary, control, minutes_in_stage=240))\n# ('HOLD', 'quality drop: page release owner')\n```\n\n- **No hard trigger fires.** Errors, latency (4500 is under 1.2 × 4100 = 4920) and cost are within limits.\n- **The judge pass rate fell five points.** With 1,800 judged canary responses (10% of 18,000 requests)\n  against 5,000 on control, the standard error of the difference is just under one point, so a five-point\n  drop is more than five standard errors: very unlikely to be noise.\n- **It still holds and pages a human** rather than rolling back, because a judge-score drop can also mean\n  the judge misreads the new model's style (for example shorter answers it penalises). The human reads a\n  sample of failing judgements and decides.\n- **The judged-sample floor matters.** Had the same drop appeared after only 200 judgements, the gate\n  would have returned \"collecting evidence\".\n\n### Deriving the minimum request count\nRequire the expected gap between baseline and regressed error counts to exceed *k* standard deviations\nof the baseline count: n·p·(r − 1) ≥ k·√(n·p), so n ≥ k² / (p·(r − 1)²).\n\n```python\nfrom math import ceil\n\ndef min_requests(baseline_rate, regression_ratio, k=5):\n    \"\"\"Requests needed before a regression_ratio-fold error rise stands k sigma above noise.\"\"\"\n    return ceil(k * k / (baseline_rate * (regression_ratio - 1) ** 2))\n\nprint(min_requests(0.01, 2))     # 2500  -> 3,000 is a sensible rounded-up floor\nprint(min_requests(0.004, 2))    # 6250  -> lower baselines need more traffic\nprint(min_requests(0.01, 1.5))   # 10000 -> smaller regressions cost quadratically more\n```","patterns":["**Metric-driven canary controller.** On Kubernetes, Argo Rollouts or Flagger step traffic weights and run analysis queries against Prometheus between steps, aborting when a metric fails its threshold. Use them for hard triggers (error rate, p95, 429s); monitor aborted rollouts and time spent per step.","**Flag-based percentage rollout for prompts and models.** Keep prompt version and pinned model ID in a feature-flag or config service, roll out by percentage or tenant, and roll back by flipping the flag. Monitor flag change events alongside the quality dashboard so every shift is attributable.","**Judged-sample floor on quality gates.** Route a fixed fraction of canary and control traffic to an LLM judge and refuse to promote until the judged count reaches the computed floor (about 1,600 per arm for a 3-point drop near 90%). Monitor judged count per stage, not just request count.","**Old index kept warm.** Serve the new index behind a config switch while the previous index stays deployed until the bake ends. Monitor retrieval hit-rate and judge scores per index version.","**Write-action fencing in early stages.** In internal and first-tenant stages, agent tools with side effects run in dry-run or behind human approval. Monitor how many write actions would have executed, to size the blast radius before enabling them."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    T[\"Evaluation tick, every 15 min\"] --> S{\"Minimum requests reached?\"}\n    S -->|\"yes\"| H{\"Any hard trigger tripped?\"}\n    H -->|\"yes\"| RB[\"Roll back via flag\"]\n    H -->|\"no\"| E{\"Duration and judged sample met?\"}\n    S -->|\"no\"| HO[\"Hold: collecting evidence\"]\n    E -->|\"no\"| HO\n    E -->|\"yes\"| Q{\"Judge pass rate dropped?\"}\n    Q -->|\"yes\"| HP[\"Hold: page release owner\"]\n    Q -->|\"no\"| P[\"Promote to next stage\"]\n    HO -.-> T\n    P -.->|\"next stage\"| T","caption":"Every evaluation tick runs hard triggers first, then the evidence floors, then the soft quality check;\nonly a clean pass promotes."},"interview":[{"q":"Why have an explicit \"hold\" state instead of just promote or roll back?","a":"- **Evidence often is not there yet:** before the minimum sample, neither promoting nor rolling back is\n  justified by data.\n- **Soft signals need a human:** a judge-score drop may be a real regression or the judge misreading a new\n  style, so hold and page a named owner rather than automating either way."},{"q":"How would you pick the minimum request count for a canary stage?","a":"- **Start from the baseline rate and smallest regression you care about:** errors are roughly Poisson, so\n  the standard deviation of the count is about √(np).\n- **Worked numbers:** at a 1% baseline, 3,000 requests give about 30 ± 5.5 errors versus about 60 for a\n  doubled rate, more than five standard deviations apart; 100 requests cannot tell."},{"q":"A prompt change rolled out green on errors and latency, then complaints arrived. What was missing?","a":"- **Quality signals:** prompt and model changes can degrade answers with no error or latency change.\n- **Fix the gate:** add sampled LLM-judge scoring on canary and control as a soft trigger, with a judged\n  sample floor (about 1,600 per arm to see 91% falling to 88%)."},{"q":"When would you roll forward instead of rolling back?","a":"- **When rollback is impossible:** a schema migration already applied, or an external contract already\n  changed.\n- **When the old version is broken too:** for example a deprecated provider model; otherwise roll back,\n  because it restores a known state."},{"q":"What does rollback not undo for an agent, and how do you design around it?","a":"- **Side effects:** emails sent or records updated by the bad version stay in customer systems after a\n  clean revert.\n- **Design:** dry-run or approval-gated write tools in early stages, and log every external write with the\n  release version so cleanup is possible."}],"tradeoffs":["**Trustworthy decisions vs release speed.** Long bake times and large minimum samples make decisions trustworthy but slow every release; the balance flips toward speed for low-risk changes behind flags and toward caution for model swaps.","**Reaction time vs thrash.** Automated rollback reacts in minutes but can thrash on noisy metrics; reserve automation for hard, low-noise signals and route noisy quality signals to a human hold.","**Robust comparison vs cost.** Relative thresholds against a live control absorb traffic shifts, but require running two versions at once, which doubles some costs and is awkward for stateful or per-tenant releases.","**Blast radius vs signal.** Rolling out by tenant gives clean blast-radius control and clearer attribution, but small tenants produce too little traffic to judge, so per-tenant stages need longer durations."],"pitfalls":["**Writing criteria after the rollout starts.** Symptom: thresholds that conveniently sit just above whatever the canary is currently showing. Fix: commit criteria with the release plan.","**Judging a stage before its minimum sample.** Symptom: releases promoted on a quiet hour and incidents that start when peak traffic arrives. Fix: enforce duration and sample floors in the gate.","**Monitoring only errors and latency for a prompt or model change.** Symptom: a green rollout followed by a week of user complaints about worse answers. Fix: add sampled quality as a soft trigger.","**Pointing at a floating model alias.** The provider changes the model underneath you; symptom: a quality shift no release on your side explains and rollback does not fix. Fix: pin model versions.","**Assuming rollback undoes everything.** Symptom: a clean revert while records written or messages sent by the bad version remain in customer systems. Fix: fence write actions early."],"when":"- **Use for any behaviour-changing release:** code, prompt, model, retrieval configuration, index,\n  guardrail policy, tool set.\n- **Scale the ladder to risk:** a copy change behind a flag may need only a short internal stage; a model\n  swap for an agent with write tools needs staged tenants, a long bake and human-reviewed quality gates.\n- **Measure** what the customer feels (errors, latency, cost, task success) and what predicts it (judge\n  score on sampled traffic, escalations).\n- **Interviews:** strong answers name the three-state decision, minimum samples, relative-plus-absolute\n  thresholds, hard versus soft triggers, and the irreversibility of agent side effects.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the gate logic, version pinning and flags, and wires metrics so the canary and control are comparable on the same dashboard and alert rules.","architect":"Decides which artefacts are versioned and independently rollbackable, which signals are hard triggers, and how irreversible actions are fenced during early rollout stages.","fde":"Agrees the stages and thresholds with the customer before go-live, including who on their side can call a rollback and how affected users are told."},"check":{"q":"A new model version is at 10% of traffic. Error rate and latency are fine, but the thumbs-down\nrate is 4.1% versus 3.2% on control after 900 rated responses. Roll back, hold or promote?","a":"Hold, not promote, and probably not an automatic rollback yet. Thumbs-down is a noisy soft\nsignal: 900 ratings at 4.1% is about 37 negatives against about 29 expected at the control rate. The\nstandard error of a rate near 4% on 900 ratings is roughly 0.65 points (more once the control's own\nnoise is added), so the 0.9-point gap is at most about 1.4 standard errors, well within chance, before even counting bias from which users choose to rate. I would extend the stage until the sample\nis large enough, read a sample of the negative responses to see whether they share a cause, and run the\nLLM judge on matched traffic for a less biased signal. If the failures cluster on a real regression,\nroll back; if they are spread and the gap closes with more data, continue."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should rollback triggers be written before the rollout begins?","options":["So they can be loaded into the deployment tool's configuration file","Because decisions made while watching ambiguous live numbers are biased toward continuing the release","Because thresholds cannot be changed once traffic is flowing","Because auditors require a document for every deployment"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pre-committed thresholds move the reasoning to a calm moment. In the moment, sunk cost and schedule pressure make \"probably noise\" the default reading of a bad number, which is how 5% problems reach 100%."},{"q":"Which signal is the best candidate for an automated, immediate rollback trigger?","options":["Average user satisfaction rating from in-app surveys","LLM-judge quality score on 50 sampled responses","Error rate exceeding twice the control rate after the minimum request count","Number of support tickets opened this week"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Error rate is fast, low-noise and unambiguous once a minimum sample exists. Survey ratings, small judge samples and tickets are slow or noisy, so they should trigger a human-reviewed hold rather than automation."},{"q":"A rollout re-embedded the whole corpus with a new embedding model. What makes rollback safe?","options":["Re-running the old embedding model over the corpus if quality drops","Pinning the new embedding model version","Rolling back the application code only","Keeping the old index serving in parallel until the new one has passed its bake period"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Re-embedding takes hours, so it is not a mid-incident rollback. Keeping the old index live lets rollback be a configuration switch, while code-only rollback would query vectors from an incompatible model."}]},{"key":"deployment/runbooks","sources":["Beyer, Jones, Petoff, Murphy, 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering"],"why":"- **Fix-it knowledge lives in a few heads.** Which dashboard shows the real problem, which flag switches\n  to the fallback model, which log query finds the failing tenant. That works until those engineers are\n  asleep, on holiday, or on another project.\n- **Forward-deployed work fails structurally.** After handoff the customer's operations team is on call\n  for a system they did not build, often in an environment the vendor cannot access. At 3 a.m. under\n  pressure nobody reasons well from first principles.\n- **\"The on-call will figure it out\" fails.** Every incident becomes an investigation, stretching time to\n  mitigation from minutes to hours while users are affected.\n- **An architecture document fails too.** It explains how the system works, not what to do when the\n  provider returns 429s, and nobody reads twenty pages during an outage.\n- **AI adds unfamiliar failure modes.** Provider degradation or rate limits, cost spikes from a prompt loop\n  or abusive user, guardrail-block surges, a stale or partially rebuilt index, jailbreak or\n  prompt-injection reports, confident wrong answers after an upstream change. Some responses (pausing\n  write tools, notifying security) need explicit permission, which the runbook writes down in advance.","how":"### One situation per runbook\n- **One alert or symptom, not a whole service**, because the reader arrives from a specific page and\n  needs the steps for that situation, not a tour.\n- **Every alert links to its runbook.** An alert with no runbook means the alert is unnecessary or the\n  knowledge is missing.\n\n### A fixed structure\n1. **Trigger:** the alert name and threshold, or the user-reported symptom.\n2. **Impact:** who is affected and how badly, with a severity guide, because severity decides who is\n   woken up and how fast customers are told.\n3. **Confirm:** one or two checks that tell a real problem from a false alarm, with exact dashboard links\n   or queries.\n4. **Mitigate:** the steps that stop user harm, in order, with exact commands or flag names. Mitigation\n   comes before diagnosis because restoring service is urgent and root cause can wait for the postmortem.\n5. **Verify:** what metric must return to normal, and for how long, before declaring recovery.\n6. **Escalate:** who to call if mitigation fails or the situation does not match, with names or rotas,\n   not \"the platform team\".\n7. **Afterwards:** what to record for the postmortem (timeline, traces, request IDs).\n\n### Precision over prose\n- **Imperative, copy-pasteable steps:** the flag name, the query, the expected output.\n- **Explicit decision points:** \"if 429s are from one tenant, go to step 6; otherwise continue\".\n- **No vague steps.** \"Check the logs\" is where incidents stall.\n\n### Pre-authorised actions\n- **State what the on-call may do without asking:** switch to a fallback model, disable an agent's write\n  tools, block a tenant, shed load.\n- **Why:** waiting for a manager's approval at night extends the incident.\n\n### Automation is the next step, not a replacement\n- **Promote repeated steps:** a step run identically every time becomes a script, then automatic\n  remediation.\n- **Keep the runbook after automating**, describing what the automation does and what to do when it\n  fails, because automation fails in exactly the unusual situations where humans need context.\n\n### Store them where the outage is not\n- **Do not share fate with the system:** not only on a wiki behind the same single sign-on that may be\n  failing, and not only in a cloud account the customer's on-call cannot access.\n- **Keep an offline or mirrored copy** for scenarios (identity outage, network partition into an\n  air-gapped site) where the primary location goes down too.\n- **The alert carries the link**, because searching for the right page costs minutes the incident does\n  not have.\n\n### Runbook vs playbook\n- **Terminology varies:** some teams say *playbook* for the broader incident-handling process (roles,\n  communication, severity) and *runbook* for the per-situation procedure described here.\n- **Have both**, and do not expect one to do the other's job.\n\n### Keep them alive\n- **Owner and last-tested date** on every runbook.\n- **Rehearse in game days** (deliberately injected failures in staging), and update after every incident\n  where the runbook was wrong or missing.\n- **Untested means broken:** a runbook that has never been executed should be assumed not to work.","example":"### An LLM provider-degraded runbook\nAn abridged runbook for an LLM-backed assistant, written for a customer operations team after handoff.\nFlag names, dashboards and thresholds are illustrative stand-ins for the team's own.\n\n```markdown\n### RB-07 LLM provider degraded (429s / 5xx / timeouts)\nTrigger: alert `llm_upstream_error_rate > 5% for 5 min` (any tenant)\nImpact: answers fail or slow down for all users. SEV2; SEV1 if > 25% for 15 min.\n\nConfirm\n1. Dashboard \"LLM upstream\" -> panel \"errors by status code\".\n   429 = rate limit, 5xx/timeout = provider outage. Check the provider status page.\n2. Panel \"requests by tenant\": is one tenant > 50% of traffic? If yes, go to step 6.\n\nMitigate (pre-authorised, no approval needed)\n3. 5xx/timeouts: set flag `llm.route=fallback` (secondary provider/region). Expect\n   slightly lower answer quality; banner `degraded_mode=on` informs users.\n4. 429s, no single hot tenant: set `llm.max_concurrency=40` (default 80) to stop retry storms.\n5. Pause batch jobs: set `jobs.summarise_nightly.enabled=false`.\n6. One tenant flooding: set `tenant.<id>.rate_limit_rps=2`; notify their admin (template T-3).\n\nVerify\n7. Upstream error rate < 1% and p95 < 8 s for 15 min. Then revert step 3 first, watch 15 min,\n   then restore concurrency, then re-enable batch jobs last.\n\nEscalate\n8. Not recovered 30 min after step 3, or fallback also failing: page vendor on-call\n   (rota \"assistant-l3\"), open a ticket with the provider quoting request IDs from the trace.\nAfterwards: export the incident timeline and five failing trace IDs to the postmortem doc.\n```\n\nWhat it encodes:\n- **A rate-limit vs outage split**, because the same alert has opposite fixes. For an outage you move\n  traffic away; for rate limiting you reduce your own pressure, and failing over a rate-limited workload\n  can simply exhaust the fallback's quota too.\n- **A hot-tenant branch** and **actions the operator may take without approval**.\n- **An explicit recovery condition** before anything is reverted.\n- **A deliberate revert order:** one change at a time with a watch period, so if errors return you know\n  which reverted change they depended on. The most load-generating change (batch jobs) comes back last,\n  when the system has proven it is stable.\n\n### Linking alerts to runbooks, and linting the link\nPrometheus alerting rules conventionally carry a `runbook_url` annotation, so the page that wakes the\non-call contains the link.\n\n```yaml\ngroups:\n  - name: assistant\n    rules:\n      - alert: LLMUpstreamErrorRate\n        expr: llm_upstream_error_rate > 0.05\n        for: 5m\n        labels:\n          severity: sev2\n        annotations:\n          summary: \"LLM upstream errors above 5% for 5 minutes\"\n          runbook_url: \"https://runbooks.example.internal/RB-07\"\n```\n\nA CI check fails the build when an alert has no runbook or a runbook has gone untested too long.\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import date\n\nalerts = [  # parsed from the alert rules file\n    {\"alert\": \"LLMUpstreamErrorRate\", \"runbook_url\": \"https://runbooks.example.internal/RB-07\"},\n    {\"alert\": \"GuardrailBlockSurge\"},                       # no runbook link\n]\nrunbooks = {\"RB-07\": {\"owner\": \"cust-ops\", \"last_tested\": date(2026, 3, 2)}}\n\ndef lint(alerts, runbooks, today, max_age_days=180):\n    problems = []\n    for a in alerts:\n        url = a.get(\"runbook_url\")\n        if not url:\n            problems.append(f\"{a['alert']}: no runbook_url\")\n            continue\n        rb = runbooks.get(url.rsplit(\"/\", 1)[-1])\n        if rb is None:\n            problems.append(f\"{a['alert']}: runbook {url} not found\")\n        elif (today - rb[\"last_tested\"]).days > max_age_days:\n            problems.append(f\"{a['alert']}: runbook last tested {rb['last_tested']}\")\n    return problems\n\nprint(lint(alerts, runbooks, date(2026, 10, 20)))\n# ['LLMUpstreamErrorRate: runbook last tested 2026-03-02', 'GuardrailBlockSurge: no runbook_url']\n```","patterns":["**Alert-embedded runbook links.** Put the runbook URL in every paging alert (for example the `runbook_url` annotation in Prometheus rules) so it travels into the pager notification. Monitor the share of paging alerts without a link; target zero.","**Game days before handoff.** Inject the failure in staging (block the provider endpoint, throttle a connector, corrupt an index alias) and have the customer's on-call run the runbook unaided. Monitor time to mitigate and every step where they hesitated.","**Runbook review in the postmortem template.** Each postmortem asks \"was the runbook right, missing or wrong?\" and opens an update task. Monitor incidents where the runbook was wrong.","**Mirrored copy outside the blast radius.** Export runbooks to a location that does not depend on the system's identity provider or network, refreshed on every change. Monitor the mirror's last sync time.","**Step-to-script promotion.** When a step is run identically in several incidents, wrap it in a script or chat-ops command and reference that command from the runbook. Monitor how often automated remediations fail and fall back to manual steps."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    A[\"Alert fires with runbook link\"] --> C{\"Confirm: real problem?\"}\n    C -->|\"no\"| FA[\"Record false alarm, tune alert\"]\n    C -->|\"yes\"| B{\"Which branch matches?\"}\n    B -->|\"provider outage\"| M1[\"Fail over to fallback\"]\n    B -->|\"rate limit\"| M2[\"Cut own concurrency\"]\n    B -->|\"no match\"| E[\"Escalate to named rota\"]\n    M1 --> V{\"Recovered for watch period?\"}\n    M2 --> V\n    V -->|\"no\"| E\n    V -->|\"yes\"| R[\"Revert one change at a time\"]\n    R --> P[\"Record timeline for postmortem\"]","caption":"The responder's path through one runbook: confirm before acting, mitigate before diagnosing, and\nescalate when the steps do not match or do not work."},"interview":[{"q":"What makes a runbook different from system documentation?","a":"- **Scope:** one alert or symptom with trigger, impact, confirm, mitigate, verify and escalate steps,\n  rather than an explanation of how the system works.\n- **Why it matters:** the responder is often not the builder, and under pressure needs exact commands, not\n  a twenty-page tour."},{"q":"Your provider alert fires with 429s. Would you fail over to the fallback provider?","a":"- **Not first:** 429s mean you are being rate-limited, so the fix is reducing your own pressure (lower\n  concurrency, pause batch jobs, throttle a hot tenant).\n- **Failing over can backfire:** the same load can exhaust the fallback's quota too. Failover fits 5xx and\n  timeouts, which signal a provider outage."},{"q":"Which actions would you pre-authorise for the on-call, and which not?","a":"- **Pre-authorise reversible, harm-stopping actions:** switch to a fallback model, disable an agent's write\n  tools, block or rate-limit a tenant, shed load.\n- **Keep approval for irreversible or customer-contract changes**, and keep the confirm step strict so\n  disruptive steps follow evidence rather than false alarms."},{"q":"How do you know a runbook still works six months after handoff?","a":"- **Last-tested date and owner on each one**, with a CI check that flags stale runbooks and alerts missing\n  a link.\n- **Game days and postmortem reviews:** rehearse in staging and update the runbook after every incident\n  where it was wrong or missing."},{"q":"Where should runbooks live, and why does it matter?","a":"- **Outside the system's blast radius:** not only behind the same single sign-on or in a cloud account the\n  customer cannot access.\n- **With a mirrored or offline copy**, so an identity outage or network partition does not hide the page\n  that tells the on-call what to do."}],"tradeoffs":["**Speed for strangers vs staleness.** Detailed, command-level runbooks make incidents fast for unfamiliar operators but go stale quickly as the system changes; the cost flips when nobody owns updates, and a wrong runbook is worse than none.","**Fast mitigation vs over-reaction.** Pre-authorising disruptive actions shortens incidents but risks over-reaction on false alarms; keep the confirm step strict so disruptive mitigation follows evidence.","**No human delay vs hidden behaviour.** Automating remediation removes human delay but hides the system's behaviour from operators; keep humans in the loop for actions with customer-visible or irreversible effects.","**Precision vs navigability.** Many narrow runbooks match alerts precisely but become hard to navigate; a short index by symptom helps when the triggering alert is not obvious."],"pitfalls":["**Explaining the architecture instead of giving steps.** Symptom: an on-call who read the page and still had to page the original engineer. Fix: imperative, exact steps.","**Never testing them.** Symptom: a step references a flag, dashboard or command renamed months ago, discovered mid-incident. Fix: game days and a last-tested date.","**Alerts with no linked runbook.** Symptom: repeated pages that each get the same ad-hoc response and no consistent mitigation time. Fix: lint for the link in CI.","**Leaving out AI-specific situations** such as cost spikes, guardrail-block surges or prompt-injection reports. Symptom: a generic \"service degraded\" response to a problem that needed a kill switch.","**Keeping runbooks only behind the system's own login** or on infrastructure that fails with it. Symptom: in an identity or network outage the on-call cannot open the page that tells them what to do.","**Writing them only for the vendor team.** Symptom: a customer operations team that escalates every page during hypercare because the runbooks assume access and context they do not have."],"when":"- **Use for:** every alert that pages a human, and every known failure mode a different team will\n  handle, before go-live and as a readiness-review item.\n- **Start with the likeliest and most harmful situations:** provider outage, rate limits, cost runaway,\n  index staleness, unsafe output reports, authentication failures to customer systems.\n- **Measure:** time to mitigate, how often responders escalate despite the runbook, and how often an\n  incident finds a runbook wrong.\n- **Interviews:** alert-linked runbooks with pre-authorised mitigations and rehearsal show operational\n  maturity beyond \"we have monitoring\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes runbooks for the failure modes they built in, with exact flags and queries, and adds a kill switch or fallback path wherever a runbook needs one that does not exist.","architect":"Ensures the system exposes the levers runbooks need (fallback routing, per-tenant limits, tool disablement) and defines which actions are pre-authorised for on-call.","fde":"Rewrites runbooks for the customer's operators, in their tools and access model, and runs a game day with them before handoff so the procedures are proven in their environment."},"check":{"q":"During hypercare the customer's on-call escalated every one of six incidents to your team, even\nthough runbooks existed. What would you investigate and change?","a":"I would review each incident with the on-call to see where they stopped. Common causes: the\nrunbook assumed access they lack (a vendor dashboard or cloud account); steps were vague (\"check the\nmodel\") instead of exact; the confirm step did not distinguish their situation so they did not trust the\nmitigation; or disruptive actions were not explicitly pre-authorised, so they waited for permission. Fixes\nfollow from the cause: rewrite with their tools and links, add decision branches, state pre-authorisation\nin writing with their management's agreement, then run a game day so they execute each runbook once\nunder realistic conditions."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why do runbooks put mitigation steps before diagnosis?","options":["Diagnosis is the vendor's responsibility, not the on-call's","Stopping user harm is urgent, while root cause can be found afterwards from preserved evidence","Diagnosis cannot be written down in advance","Mitigation steps are easier to automate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The incident's cost accrues while users are affected, so restoring service comes first. Recording timelines and trace IDs preserves what the postmortem needs, so diagnosis is deferred, not lost."},{"q":"Which runbook step is most likely to stall an incident?","options":["\"Set flag llm.route=fallback\"","\"If one tenant exceeds 50% of traffic, go to step 6\"","\"Check the logs for anything unusual\"","\"Verify upstream error rate stays below 1% for 15 minutes\""],"answer":2,"explanation":"Vague instructions force the responder to reason from scratch, which is what runbooks exist to avoid. The other steps are exact actions, explicit branches or measurable recovery conditions."},{"q":"A team automated a runbook's mitigation as an auto-remediation job. What should happen to the runbook?","options":["Delete it, since the automation now handles the situation","Freeze it as historical documentation","Keep it, describing what the automation does and what to do if the automation fails or does not fire","Convert it into a postmortem template"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Automation fails in unusual conditions, which is when operators most need context. The runbook becomes the fallback for the automation and explains its behaviour so responders do not fight it."}]},{"key":"deployment/health-checks","sources":["Official docs: Kubernetes (liveness, readiness and startup probes)"],"why":"- **Infrastructure cannot see inside an instance.** Without checks, traffic keeps flowing to a process\n  that has deadlocked, run out of GPU memory, or is still loading a multi-gigabyte model, while every\n  dashboard shows \"running\".\n- **Rolling deploys depend on them.** A new version is only safe to receive traffic once it says it is\n  ready.\n- **A single shallow `/health` returning 200 is too shallow.** A server can answer HTTP while its model\n  failed to load or its connection pool is exhausted.\n- **A single deep check wired to restarts is dangerous.** Checking every dependency (database, vector\n  store, LLM provider) in the restart probe means one provider blip fails every instance at once: a\n  fleet-wide restart storm with cold model loads on top.\n- **\"Healthy\" is not one question.** Restart, route traffic, and \"does the product work end to end\" have\n  different answers and consequences, so each gets its own check and scope.","how":"### The four checks\n| Check | Question | Checks | Action on failure |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Liveness** | Is this process irrecoverably stuck? | Event loop responds, no deadlocked worker | Restart (kubelet, after repeated failures) |\n| **Readiness** | Can it serve a request successfully right now? | Model loaded, GPU usable, not overloaded, not shutting down | Remove from Service endpoints, no restart |\n| **Startup** | Has slow initialisation finished? | Weights loaded, GPU warmed, in-memory index built | Liveness and readiness wait until it passes |\n| **Synthetic end-to-end** | Does the product produce a correct answer? | Golden request through the real entry point | Alert and dashboard, never restart |\n\n### Liveness: should this process be restarted?\n- **Check only what a restart would fix:** the event loop responds, a worker thread is not deadlocked.\n- **Never check external dependencies**, because restarting your service does not fix someone else's\n  outage, and fleet-scale restarts add load exactly when the system is weakest.\n\n### Readiness: should this instance receive traffic right now?\n- **Check local conditions only.** In Kubernetes a failing readiness probe removes the pod from the\n  Service's endpoints without restarting it, so traffic flows to other replicas.\n- **Shared dependencies are risky here.** If every replica checks the same LLM provider and it fails, every\n  replica goes unready and the service has zero endpoints, so even a fallback path cannot be served.\n  Instead keep serving and degrade (fallback model, cached answer, clear error) and report the\n  dependency through metrics and alerts.\n- **Drain before exiting:** stop new requests, then finish in-flight ones, because long LLM streams cut\n  mid-answer are user-visible errors on every deploy. Behind a plain load balancer, fail readiness first\n  and wait before exit.\n- **Kubernetes removes a terminating pod from endpoints itself, but asynchronously**, so the usual pattern\n  is a short pre-stop delay plus a graceful-shutdown timeout longer than the longest stream.\n\n### Startup: has slow initialisation finished?\n- **Loading weights, warming a GPU or building an in-memory index can take minutes.** A startup probe\n  covers that window; Kubernetes does not run liveness and readiness until it succeeds.\n- **Without it** teams either set a huge liveness delay (slow to detect real hangs later) or watch pods\n  get killed mid-load in a restart loop.\n\n### Synthetic end-to-end checks\n- **Probes describe one instance, not the product.** A synthetic check runs from outside on a schedule:\n  a fixed golden request through the real entry point (auth, retrieval, model, guardrails), asserting on\n  the answer, for example that it cites the expected document.\n- **It catches what probes never see:** a stale index, an expired credential to a customer system, a model\n  silently changed behind an alias.\n- **Results feed alerts and dashboards, not restarts.**\n\n### Probe timing is arithmetic, not taste\nEach probe acts only after `failureThreshold` consecutive failures, checked every `periodSeconds`:\n\n```\nreaction_time ≈ periodSeconds × failureThreshold   (+ up to timeoutSeconds per attempt)\n```\n\n| Probe | What the product means | How to set it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Startup** | Window for initialisation | Exceed the worst case, not the typical one. Weights load in 90 seconds normally but 4 minutes on a cold node pulling from object storage: `periodSeconds: 10, failureThreshold: 30` gives a 300-second window; a 60-second window kills every pod on a cold node and loops forever |\n| **Liveness** | How long a hung process keeps its share of traffic before restart | Short detects hangs fast but also restarts pods merely slow under a load spike (a long GC pause, a CPU-starved event loop) |\n| **Readiness** | How long an overloaded or draining instance keeps getting new requests | The matching `successThreshold` decides how quickly it is trusted again |\n\n- **Choose from measured load times and latency, not copied defaults.** That is most of the difference\n  between probes that help and probes that cause outages.\n\n### Making checks cheap and honest\n- **Fast, and never calling the LLM**, because probes run often.\n- **Deeper checks on a background schedule; the endpoint returns the cached result**, because a probe that\n  waits on a slow dependency times out and reports failure for the wrong reason.\n- **Return a body saying which condition failed**, so operators following a runbook see why an instance is\n  unready.","example":"### FastAPI service with separate probes\nA FastAPI service that loads a model at startup, separates liveness from readiness and reports\noverload. The model loader is a stand-in for real initialisation. It runs as a background task because\nan `await` inside the lifespan would delay the server from accepting connections until loading ends:\nprobes would see connection refused rather than a 503 with a reason.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom contextlib import asynccontextmanager\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, Response\n\nstate = {\"model\": None, \"in_flight\": 0}\nMAX_IN_FLIGHT = 32                       # illustrative per-replica concurrency budget\n\nasync def load_model():\n    await asyncio.sleep(5)               # stand-in; real blocking loads go in asyncio.to_thread\n    state[\"model\"] = object()\n\n@asynccontextmanager\nasync def lifespan(app):\n    task = asyncio.create_task(load_model())   # load in background so probes answer meanwhile\n    yield\n    task.cancel()\n\napp = FastAPI(lifespan=lifespan)\n\n@app.get(\"/livez\")                      # restart only if the process itself is stuck\nasync def livez():\n    return {\"ok\": True}                 # reaching this handler proves the event loop runs\n\n@app.get(\"/startupz\")                   # gates liveness and readiness until the model is loaded\nasync def startupz(resp: Response):\n    if state[\"model\"] is None:\n        resp.status_code = 503\n    return {\"model_loaded\": state[\"model\"] is not None}\n\n@app.get(\"/readyz\")                     # route traffic here only if a request would succeed now\nasync def readyz(resp: Response):\n    reasons = []\n    if state[\"model\"] is None: reasons.append(\"model not loaded\")\n    if state[\"in_flight\"] >= MAX_IN_FLIGHT: reasons.append(\"at capacity\")\n    if reasons:\n        resp.status_code = 503\n    return {\"ready\": not reasons, \"reasons\": reasons}\n```\n\n- **No check calls the LLM provider or the vector store.** Their health is reported by metrics and a\n  separate synthetic check, so an upstream outage degrades the service instead of emptying the load\n  balancer.\n- **A real deployment increments and decrements `in_flight`** around each generation (including the whole\n  duration of a streamed response).\n- **Draining is configured in the platform**, for example a Kubernetes pre-stop delay, rather than by\n  overriding the server's own signal handling.\n\n### Kubernetes probe configuration for the service above\nIllustrative values for a model that loads in 90 seconds typically and up to 4 minutes on a cold node.\n\n```yaml\nspec:\n  terminationGracePeriodSeconds: 120      # longer than the longest streamed answer\n  containers:\n    - name: assistant\n      image: registry.example.internal/assistant:1.4.2\n      ports:\n        - containerPort: 8000\n      startupProbe:\n        httpGet: { path: /startupz, port: 8000 }\n        periodSeconds: 10\n        failureThreshold: 30              # 300 s window, above the 4-minute worst case\n      livenessProbe:\n        httpGet: { path: /livez, port: 8000 }\n        periodSeconds: 10\n        failureThreshold: 3               # about 30 s of hang before restart\n        timeoutSeconds: 2\n      readinessProbe:\n        httpGet: { path: /readyz, port: 8000 }\n        periodSeconds: 5\n        failureThreshold: 2               # about 10 s before routing away\n        successThreshold: 1\n      lifecycle:\n        preStop:\n          exec:\n            command: [\"sleep\", \"10\"]      # let endpoint removal propagate before SIGTERM\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: the 90-second provider blip\n| Design | What happens during a 90-second provider outage |\n|---|---|\n| Provider checked in **liveness** | Every pod fails together, all restart, each reloads weights; recovery takes many minutes after the provider is back |\n| Provider checked in **readiness** | No restarts, but every pod goes unready; zero endpoints for roughly the outage's duration, so even the fallback cannot be served |\n| Provider in **metrics and synthetic check only** | Pods stay ready; requests use the fallback model or return a clear error; an alert links to the runbook |","patterns":["**Separate endpoints per probe.** Expose `/livez`, `/readyz` and `/startupz` (the naming Kubernetes itself uses for its API server) with distinct logic, and wire each to the matching probe. Monitor container restart counts and ready replica count per deployment.","**Startup window from measured cold loads.** Measure model load time on a cold node (image pull plus weight download), then set startup `periodSeconds × failureThreshold` above the worst case with margin. Monitor startup duration percentiles after each model or image change.","**Pre-stop delay plus graceful timeout.** Add a short pre-stop sleep so endpoint removal propagates, and set `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` above the longest stream; the server should finish in-flight requests on SIGTERM. Monitor client-side disconnects and truncated streams during deploys.","**Golden-request synthetic check.** Run a scheduled job (every few minutes) from outside the cluster that sends a fixed question through the public entry point and asserts on a cited document ID. Alert on consecutive failures and track synthetic latency separately from real traffic.","**Capacity-aware readiness with autoscaling.** Fail readiness at a per-replica in-flight budget, and pair it with a horizontal autoscaler and a front-door rate limit so the fleet never reaches zero ready endpoints. Monitor the fraction of replicas reporting \"at capacity\"."],"diagram":{"code":"stateDiagram-v2\n    [*] --> Starting\n    Starting --> Starting: startup probe fails, under threshold\n    Starting --> Restarted: startup window exceeded\n    Starting --> Ready: startup passes, readiness passes\n    Ready --> NotReady: readiness fails\n    NotReady --> Ready: readiness passes again\n    Ready --> Restarted: liveness fails threshold times\n    NotReady --> Restarted: liveness fails threshold times\n    Ready --> Draining: termination signal\n    Draining --> [*]: in-flight streams finished\n    Restarted --> Starting","caption":"Kubernetes runs the startup probe first; after it passes, liveness and readiness run independently and\ntrigger different actions."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between liveness and readiness, and why does it matter?","a":"- **Liveness decides restarts:** it asks whether the process is irrecoverably stuck, so it checks only\n  what a restart fixes.\n- **Readiness decides routing:** a failing pod leaves the Service endpoints without restarting, so it\n  checks whether this instance can serve now.\n- **Mixing them** turns a shared-dependency blip into a restart storm or an empty endpoint list."},{"q":"Your model takes 90 seconds to load, sometimes 4 minutes. How do you configure the probes?","a":"- **Startup probe sized to the worst case:** reaction time is about `periodSeconds × failureThreshold`, so\n  10 × 30 gives 300 seconds; a 60-second window would loop forever on cold nodes.\n- **Then short liveness and readiness**, which Kubernetes only starts after startup passes, so hangs are\n  still detected quickly later."},{"q":"Users see streamed answers cut off during every deploy. What do you change?","a":"- **Drain properly:** a short pre-stop delay so endpoint removal propagates, then a grace period longer than\n  the longest stream so in-flight requests finish.\n- **Verify:** count truncated streams and client disconnects during a rollout before and after the change."},{"q":"All probes are green but users report wrong answers. What check is missing?","a":"- **A synthetic end-to-end check:** probes describe local instance state, so a stale index, expired\n  customer credential or model silently changed behind an alias leaves every probe green.\n- **Design it:** a golden request through the real entry point, asserting on a cited document, feeding\n  alerts rather than restarts."},{"q":"When would you put a dependency into readiness after all?","a":"- **When it is per-instance:** a local GPU or a sidecar only that pod uses, where routing away from this\n  replica actually helps.\n- **Not for shared dependencies:** if every replica shares it, all go unready together and nothing,\n  not even a fallback, can be served."}],"tradeoffs":["**Detection vs coupling.** Deep readiness checks catch more broken instances but couple every replica to shared dependencies; the balance flips toward deep checks only for dependencies that are per-instance, such as a local GPU.","**Fast detection vs flapping.** Aggressive probe intervals and low failure thresholds detect problems fast but cause flapping and needless restarts under load spikes; tolerate a few consecutive failures before acting.","**Graceful shedding vs empty endpoints.** Load-based readiness (\"at capacity\") sheds traffic gracefully, but if every replica hits capacity at once the service has no endpoints; pair it with autoscaling and a front-door rate limit.","**End-to-end proof vs cost and drift.** Synthetic checks prove end-to-end correctness, but each costs a real model call and can drift from real usage; keep them few, cheap and representative of the critical path."],"pitfalls":["**Checking the LLM provider or database in liveness.** Symptom: every pod restarts at once during a short upstream incident, followed by slow cold starts. Fix: liveness is local only.","**No startup probe for a service loading large weights.** Symptom: pods killed by liveness during model loading and stuck in a restart loop. Fix: a startup probe sized to the worst case.","**Exiting without draining**, so new requests still arrive during shutdown. Symptom: streamed responses cut off mid-answer on every deploy or scale-down. Fix: pre-stop delay and graceful timeout.","**Only shallow HTTP pings.** Symptom: instances that report healthy while every request fails because the model never loaded or a GPU is in an error state. Fix: readiness checks model and GPU state.","**Treating green probes as proof the product works.** Symptom: all-green dashboards while users get wrong answers from a stale index, which only a synthetic end-to-end check would have caught."],"when":"- **Every deployed service:** liveness and readiness.\n- **Slow initialisation:** add a startup probe.\n- **Customer-facing AI system:** at least one synthetic end-to-end check on the critical path.\n- **Decide scope by the action triggered:** restart, route away, or alert.\n- **Measure:** probe failure counts, restart counts, ready replica count and synthetic pass rate, with each\n  health alert linked to a runbook.\n- **Interviews:** explain why liveness must not include dependencies, how readiness interacts with draining\n  and rolling deploys, and why a probe cannot tell you answers are correct.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements separate liveness, readiness and startup endpoints, keeps them fast and dependency-free, and writes the golden-request synthetic check with a meaningful assertion.","architect":"Decides which failures cause restarts, which cause routing changes and which only alert, so a shared dependency outage degrades the service rather than taking it down.","fde":"Adapts checks to the customer's load balancers and monitoring, which may only support one endpoint, and makes synthetic checks run inside their network against their real integrations."},"check":{"q":"After a 90-second outage at the LLM provider, the whole fleet restarted and the service was down\nfor twelve minutes. What went wrong in the health check design, and how would you change it?","a":"The provider was almost certainly checked by the liveness probe: readiness failures only remove\npods from endpoints and never restart them, so a fleet-wide restart points at liveness (a provider check\nin readiness would instead have emptied the endpoints for roughly the outage's duration). When the\nprovider failed, every replica failed at once; liveness restarted them all, and each restart\nmeant reloading model weights or warming caches, so recovery took far longer than the upstream outage.\nI would make liveness local only (process responsive), keep readiness to per-instance conditions such as\nmodel loaded, capacity and draining, and report provider health through metrics, alerts and a synthetic\ncheck. The request path should degrade with a fallback model or clear error, and add a startup probe so\nslow loads are not killed."},"quiz":[{"q":"What happens in Kubernetes when a pod fails its readiness probe but passes liveness?","options":["The container is restarted","The pod is removed from Service endpoints and stops receiving traffic, without a restart","The pod is rescheduled onto another node","Nothing, readiness is informational only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Readiness controls routing: an unready pod is taken out of the Service's endpoints until it passes again. Restarts are driven by liveness failures, and rescheduling is a scheduler decision, not a probe result."},{"q":"Why should a liveness probe avoid checking the external LLM provider?","options":["Provider calls are too expensive to run every few seconds","Liveness probes cannot make outbound network calls","The provider's own status page already reports its health","Restarting your instances cannot fix the provider, and simultaneous restarts turn a brief upstream issue into a fleet-wide outage"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Cost is a real concern, but the decisive reason is the action liveness triggers. A restart only helps for failures inside the process; for shared dependencies it multiplies the damage."},{"q":"Which failure would a synthetic end-to-end check catch that liveness and readiness probes would not?","options":["A deadlocked worker thread","A pod that has not finished loading model weights","A retrieval index that stopped updating, so answers cite outdated documents","A pod that is shutting down"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Probes check one instance's local condition. A stale index leaves every instance healthy while answers are wrong, which only a check asserting on real end-to-end output can detect."}]},{"key":"deployment/hypercare","sources":[],"why":"- **Production is the full distribution; UAT only sampled it.** Every user, document type, odd workflow\n  and integration under real load arrives at once.\n- **The gap is larger for AI**, because quality depends on inputs the team has never seen: queries phrased\n  in ways the golden set did not anticipate, the scanned fax from the one branch office that still uses\n  them, users pasting whole email threads into the assistant.\n- **Adoption is fragile in the first days.** Users switching from a manual process form their opinion\n  fast. Early failures met with silence or a two-week ticket queue send them back to the old way, and\n  usage never recovers even after the bugs are fixed.\n- **\"Go-live is the finish line\" fails.** Standard support is tuned for a stable system with known issues:\n  it cannot tell a prompt regression from a user-training gap and has no route to change the prompt,\n  retrieval or model.\n- **Open-ended hypercare fails the other way:** the delivery team quietly becomes the customer's permanent\n  operations team. Hypercare bridges the two: concentrated expertise for a bounded time, with a defined\n  exit.","how":"### Plan it before go-live\n- **Agree it in the readiness review:** duration, staffing, hours of coverage, escalation paths, what\n  counts as an incident versus a request, and exit criteria.\n- **Duration is a planning choice, not a standard.** A few weeks is common, scaled to risk and user count.\n- **Plan in advance** because the customer's support organisation, the vendor team and the sponsor must\n  know who answers what before the first problem arrives.\n- **Fix the mechanics:** a single intake channel so issues are not scattered across email and chat;\n  severity definitions with an agreed response time for each; coverage hours that match when users work\n  (including other time zones); who on each side is reachable out of hours for a severity-1.\n\n### Heightened monitoring\n- **Tighten dashboards and alert thresholds**, and have someone actively watch them rather than wait for\n  pages.\n- **Watch AI signals that rarely page anyone:** user feedback rates, escalation-to-human rate, guardrail\n  blocks, retrieval misses (\"no relevant documents found\"), cost per task.\n- **Read a daily sample of real conversations.** It is the fastest way to learn where the system fails,\n  because metrics show that something changed while transcripts show why.\n\n### A daily triage loop\nEvery issue, ticket and piece of negative feedback is classified by cause, which decides the owner and the\nfix. A short daily review with the customer keeps both sides on the same list.\n\n| Cause | Meaning | Owner and fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Defect** | System bug, integration failure | Engineers |\n| **Quality** | Retrieval or model produced a poor answer | Prompt or retrieval tuning |\n| **Knowledge gap** | The answer is not in the indexed content | Customer's content owners |\n| **Training** | User misunderstood what the system does | Enablement |\n| **Change request** | New scope | Sponsor |\n\n### Fast but safe fixes\n- **Faster cadence, same gates.** Every quality fix still passes the regression suite, and every real\n  failure becomes a new regression case, because a prompt change fixing today's complaint can quietly\n  break a case UAT already accepted.\n- **Same rollout criteria, shorter stages**, not around them.\n- **Freeze everything else.** A model upgrade, a new feature, or an index rebuild for another team waits\n  until hypercare ends, because feedback signals are already noisy and two changes in one week make it\n  impossible to say which moved the thumbs-down rate.\n- **Batch fixes into one or two predictable releases a week**, each with a written note of what changed,\n  so every movement in the metrics stays attributable.\n\n### Exit criteria and handoff\nHypercare ends when written criteria hold for a sustained period, for example:\n1. No open severity-1 or severity-2 issues.\n2. Incident and ticket volume stable or falling over consecutive weeks, **normalised by active users**\n   (because volume also falls when people stop using the system) and read alongside usage retention.\n3. Quality metrics at or above the UAT baseline on real traffic.\n4. The customer's support team resolves issues using the runbooks without escalating.\n5. The known-issues list and backlog are handed over with owners.\n\nThe handoff is explicit: a meeting, a signed-off document and a named date when the support model changes.","example":"### Worked scenario: an HR policy assistant\nAn HR policy assistant for a 4,000-employee company, three weeks of planned hypercare.\n\nExit criteria agreed at the readiness review: zero open sev-1/sev-2, weekly actionable issues below 10\nfor two consecutive weeks, thumbs-down rate at or below the 6% observed in UAT, and the customer's\nservice desk resolving at least 80% of tickets without vendor escalation.\n\n| Week | Users | Actionable issues | Thumbs-down | Desk unaided |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 1,900 | 41 | 11% | n/a |\n| 2 | 2,600 | 18 | 7% | 65% |\n| 3 | 2,900 | 9 | 5.8% | 78% |\n\n- **Week 1 triage:** 9 defects (most from single sign-on timeouts for one region), 14 quality, 11\n  knowledge gaps (the parental-leave policy for one country was never in the indexed content), 5\n  training, 2 change requests.\n- **Week 1 fixes:** the team fixes SSO, the customer's HR content owner uploads the missing policy, the 14\n  quality failures become regression cases, and a retrieval fix passes the suite and ships behind a flag.\n- **Week 2 near miss:** a prompt change improved answer tone but dropped citations on long answers. The\n  regression suite caught it in staging, so it never shipped and never became a production incident.\n- **Week 3 is real improvement:** per active user, issues fell from about 2.2 per 100 users in week 1 to\n  about 0.3 while the user count kept rising, so the drop is not people giving up.\n- **But exit is not met:** volume is below 10 for only one week and desk resolution is below 80%. Rather\n  than letting hypercare drift, the team extends it by one week with a single focus: a runbook session\n  with the service desk on the three ticket types they still escalate. Week 4 meets all criteria and\n  support is handed over.\n\n### Exit criteria as code\nThe same criteria, evaluated from weekly numbers so the exit decision is not argued from impressions.\n\n```python\nweeks = [  # open_sev12 values are illustrative\n    {\"week\": 1, \"users\": 1900, \"issues\": 41, \"open_sev12\": 0, \"thumbs_down\": 0.11,  \"desk_unaided\": None},\n    {\"week\": 2, \"users\": 2600, \"issues\": 18, \"open_sev12\": 0, \"thumbs_down\": 0.07,  \"desk_unaided\": 0.65},\n    {\"week\": 3, \"users\": 2900, \"issues\": 9,  \"open_sev12\": 0, \"thumbs_down\": 0.058, \"desk_unaided\": 0.78},\n]\n\ndef exit_check(weeks, max_issues=10, td_baseline=0.06, desk_min=0.80):\n    unmet = []\n    last = weeks[-1]\n    if last[\"open_sev12\"] > 0:\n        unmet.append(\"open sev-1/sev-2\")\n    if len(weeks) < 2 or any(w[\"issues\"] >= max_issues for w in weeks[-2:]):\n        unmet.append(\"issues below 10 for two consecutive weeks\")\n    if last[\"thumbs_down\"] > td_baseline:\n        unmet.append(\"thumbs-down above UAT baseline\")\n    if (last[\"desk_unaided\"] or 0) < desk_min:\n        unmet.append(\"desk resolution below 80%\")\n    return unmet\n\nfor w in weeks:\n    print(w[\"week\"], f\"{100 * w['issues'] / w['users']:.1f} issues per 100 users\")\nprint(exit_check(weeks))\n# 1 2.2 / 2 0.7 / 3 0.3 issues per 100 users\n# ['issues below 10 for two consecutive weeks', 'desk resolution below 80%']\n```","patterns":["**Single intake channel with a cause field.** Route every report (tickets, chat, feedback buttons) into one tracker queue whose required fields include cause and severity. Monitor issues per 100 active users by cause each day.","**Daily transcript sampling.** Pull a fixed random sample of real conversations each morning, under the data-access rules agreed in the readiness review, and have one person read them before triage. Monitor new failure patterns found per day; when it reaches zero for several days, sampling can drop in frequency.","**Change freeze with a fix train.** Only hypercare fixes ship, batched into one or two scheduled releases a week, each passing the regression suite and a shortened rollout with a written change note. Monitor metric shifts against release notes so every movement is attributable.","**Failures become regression cases.** Every quality failure from triage is added to the customer-specific regression suite before its fix ships. Monitor suite growth and the number of fixes blocked by a regression.","**Shadow-then-lead support transfer.** The customer's service desk first shadows the vendor on tickets, then leads with vendor backup, using the runbooks. Monitor the share of tickets resolved without vendor escalation against the exit threshold."],"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    G[\"Go-live\"] --> M[\"Monitor metrics and read transcripts\"]\n    M --> T{\"Daily triage by cause\"}\n    T -->|\"defect or quality\"| F[\"Fix, pass regression suite\"]\n    T -->|\"knowledge gap\"| K[\"Customer content owner\"]\n    T -->|\"training\"| EN[\"Enablement\"]\n    T -->|\"change request\"| SP[\"Sponsor backlog\"]\n    F --> R[\"Batched release, shortened rollout\"]\n    R --> X{\"Exit criteria held?\"}\n    K --> X\n    EN --> X\n    X -->|\"no\"| EXT[\"Extend with one focus\"]\n    EXT --> M\n    X -->|\"yes\"| H[\"Signed handoff to support\"]","caption":"The daily loop runs until the exit criteria hold; an unmet criterion extends hypercare with a specific\nfocus rather than letting it drift."},"interview":[{"q":"What is hypercare, and why not hand straight over to standard support at go-live?","a":"- **Definition:** a bounded period of elevated support after go-live, with closer monitoring, daily triage,\n  faster fixes and named contacts, ending on written exit criteria.\n- **Why not standard support:** it is tuned for stable systems, cannot tell a prompt regression from a\n  training gap, and cannot change the prompt, retrieval or model."},{"q":"Issue volume halved in week two. How do you know the system actually improved?","a":"- **Normalise by active users:** volume also falls when people stop using the system, so compute issues\n  per 100 active users and read it next to usage retention.\n- **Example:** 41 issues for 1,900 users (about 2.2 per 100) falling to 9 for 2,900 users (about 0.3) while\n  usage grows is real improvement."},{"q":"A customer complaint arrives daily about answers on a topic. How do you triage it?","a":"- **Classify by cause first:** is the content indexed (knowledge gap), retrieved but answered badly\n  (quality), broken by an integration (defect), misunderstood by users (training), or new scope (change\n  request)?\n- **Route to the owner:** a knowledge gap goes to the customer's content owners, because tuning the prompt\n  for content that is not there risks hallucinated answers."},{"q":"Why freeze unrelated changes during hypercare?","a":"- **Attribution:** feedback signals are already noisy, and two changes in one week make it impossible to\n  tell which moved the thumbs-down rate.\n- **How:** only hypercare fixes ship, batched into one or two predictable releases a week with written\n  change notes, each through the regression suite and a shortened rollout."},{"q":"The exit date has arrived but criteria are not met. What do you do?","a":"- **Extend with a single focus, not open-endedly:** name the unmet criterion and the work that closes it,\n  for example a runbook session on the ticket types the desk still escalates.\n- **Do not exit on the calendar:** handing over before the customer can run the system just moves the\n  escalations into normal support."}],"tradeoffs":["**Expert coverage vs customer ownership.** Longer hypercare catches more issues with experts on hand, but ties up the delivery team and delays the customer's own ownership; extend only against unmet exit criteria, never by default.","**Fast fixes vs learning.** Embedding vendor engineers with the customer gives the fastest fixes, but can stop the customer team from learning; the balance flips once routine issues are being solved by vendor staff on request.","**Cadence vs regression risk.** A faster release cadence fixes real pain quickly but raises regression risk; it is safe only when every change passes the regression suite and a shortened rollout.","**Insight vs privacy cost.** Reading many real transcripts finds failure patterns early, but costs time and needs privacy approval; agree data-access rules during the readiness review, not during an incident."],"pitfalls":["**No exit criteria.** Symptom: the delivery team still answers the customer's tickets months after go-live, and nobody can say when it ends. Fix: written criteria agreed before go-live.","**Prompt tweaks that skip the regression suite.** Symptom: a fix for this week's issue reopens a case accepted in UAT. Fix: no fix ships without the suite.","**Lumping all feedback into \"bugs\".** Symptom: engineers spend days on missing content or user training while real defects wait. Fix: classify by cause and route to the right owner.","**Measuring only incidents and uptime.** Symptom: a quiet incident queue alongside falling usage, because unhappy users silently returned to the old process. Fix: normalise by active users and track retention.","**Doing the support work instead of transferring it.** Symptom: a customer service desk that escalates every ticket on the first day after handoff. Fix: the desk leads tickets before exit."],"when":"- **Use when:** real users meet a new AI system, the customer's own team will eventually operate it, or\n  adoption matters commercially.\n- **Size by risk and scale:** a small internal pilot may need a few days of close attention; an enterprise\n  rollout to thousands of users needs weeks with dedicated staff.\n- **Track:** issue volume by cause, user feedback, usage and retention, quality on real traffic versus the\n  UAT baseline, and the share of tickets the customer resolves alone.\n- **Interviews:** strong answers describe hypercare as bounded, criteria-driven and aimed at transferring\n  ownership, with AI-specific feedback classification.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Turns each real failure into a regression case, ships fixes through shortened rollouts, and builds the daily views of feedback, escalations and sampled transcripts used in triage.","architect":"Ensures the system supports hypercare safely: feature flags for quick fixes, regression gates that cannot be skipped, and data-access rules for reviewing real conversations.","fde":"Runs the daily triage with the customer, separates defects from content, training and scope issues, and drives the handoff so their team can operate the system without escalating."},"check":{"q":"Three weeks into hypercare, incidents are near zero but weekly active users have fallen 30% from\nweek one. The sponsor says hypercare can close. Do you agree?","a":"Not yet. A quiet incident queue with falling usage often means users stopped reporting because they\nstopped using the system. I would look at who dropped off and what they last did, read a sample of their\nfinal sessions, check feedback and abandonment by task type, and talk to a few users directly. Likely\ncauses are a quality gap on a common task, missing content, or a workflow that is slower than the old\nprocess. If the cause is fixable, extend hypercare against a specific adoption criterion; if it is a\ntraining or change-management issue, hand that to the customer's enablement owner explicitly. Closing on\nincident counts alone would hide the real failure."},"quiz":[{"q":"What should normally end a hypercare period?","options":["The planned number of weeks has passed","The delivery team is needed on another project","Written exit criteria have held for a sustained period and ownership is formally handed over","The first week with zero incidents"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Hypercare is bounded by criteria, not the calendar or staffing. A single quiet week can be noise or reflect falling usage, and calendar-based exits hand over a system the customer cannot yet run."},{"q":"Users report that the assistant cannot answer questions about one country's leave policy. The ingestion pipeline works, but the customer's HR team never supplied that policy for indexing. How should this be classified in triage?","options":["As a model quality defect needing prompt changes","As a user training issue","As a knowledge gap owned by the customer's content owners","As a change request to the sponsor"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The system cannot answer from content it does not have. Tuning prompts would risk hallucinated answers; the right owner is whoever controls the source content, and the fix is to ingest the missing policy."},{"q":"Why must quality fixes during hypercare still pass the regression suite?","options":["Because the customer's change-management policy always requires it","Because a prompt or retrieval change that fixes one complaint can silently break cases already accepted in UAT","Because regression suites are the only way to measure latency","Because hypercare fixes are deployed to all users at once"],"answer":1,"explanation":"AI behaviour changes are global: a prompt edit affects every query. The regression suite, extended with each real failure, is what keeps a fast fix cadence from trading one problem for another."}]}]});
